{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788347609638,"sessionID":"ses_f9e2d34cdffe0eE32xFqUVyL7D","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_d9bf94440957472eb5b92322","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/aloschilov/workspace/pyasc-skill-stack/integrations/cannbench/workers/.scratch/20260902_140812/exp-generate/task.md"},"output":"<path>/home/aloschilov/workspace/pyasc-skill-stack/integrations/cannbench/workers/.scratch/20260902_140812/exp-generate/task.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: You are implementing ONE operator for the CANN Bench benchmark (https://gitcode.com/cann/cann-bench) as a pyasc asc2 JIT kernel module targeting an Ascend 950PR NPU. You have NO NPU access — the official harness evaluates your file on real hardware after you finish. Score per operator = 0.2 compile + 0.3 accuracy (all 20 cases must pass a relative-error check against golden.py) + 0.5 performance (profiler-measured kernel time vs an aclnn baseline).\n2: \n3: # Task\n4: \n5: Operator: **Exp**. Write the file `candidate.py` in the current working directory: a complete Python module whose public callable `exp` implements the schema below. Nothing else — no tests, no other files.\n6: \n7: # Operator specification\n8: \n9: ## desc.md\n10: \n11: # Exp 算子 API 描述\n12: \n13: ## 1. 算子简介\n14: \n15: Exp 算子用于计算输入张量的广义指数函数，支持自定义底数（base）、缩放因子（scale）和偏移量（shift）三个参数，涵盖自然指数、任意底数指数等多种变体。\n16: \n17: **主要应用场景**：\n18: - Softmax 中的自然指数计算\n19: - 注意力机制中的指数缩放\n20: - 概率分布与对数域间的转换\n21: - 学习率调度与指数衰减\n22: \n23: **算子特征**：\n24: - 难度等级：L1（Elementwise）\n25: - 单输入单输出，逐元素运算，输出 shape 与输入完全一致\n26: \n27: ## 2. 算子定义\n28: \n29: ### 数学公式\n30: \n31: **通用公式**：\n32: \n33: $$\n34: y = e^{(x \\cdot scale + shift) \\cdot \\ln(base)}, \\quad base > 0\n35: $$\n36: \n37: **自然指数**（当 $base \\leq 0$ 时，使用自然底数 $e$）：\n38: \n39: $$\n40: y = e^{x \\cdot scale + shift}\n41: $$\n42: \n43: ### 特殊情况\n44: \n45: | 条件 | 简化公式 |\n46: |------|---------|\n47: | base ≤ 0, scale=1, shift=0 | $y = e^x$ |\n48: | base > 0, scale=1, shift=0 | $y = base^x$ |\n49: | base=1（任意 scale, shift） | $y = 1$（因 $\\ln 1 = 0$） |\n50: \n51: ## 3. 接口规范\n52: \n53: ### 算子原型\n54: \n55: ```python\n56: cann_bench.exp(Tensor x, float base=-1.0, float scale=1.0, float shift=0.0) -> Tensor y\n57: ```\n58: \n59: ### 输入参数说明\n60: \n61: | 参数 | 类型 | 默认值 | 描述 |\n62: |------|------|--------|------|\n63: | x | Tensor | 必选 | 输入张量，支持任意维度 |\n64: | base | float | -1.0 | 指数底数；≤ 0 表示使用自然底数 $e$，> 0 表示自定义底数 |\n65: | scale | float | 1.0 | 输入缩放因子 |\n66: | shift | float | 0.0 | 输入偏移量 |\n67: \n68: ### 输出\n69: \n70: | 参数 | Shape | dtype | 描述 |\n71: |------|-------|-------|------|\n72: | y | 与输入 x 相同 | 与输入 x 相同 | 指数计算结果 |\n73: \n74: ### 数据类型\n75: \n76: | 输入 dtype | 输出 dtype |\n77: |-----------|-----------|\n78: | float16 | float16 |\n79: | float32 | float32 |\n80: | bfloat16 | bfloat16 |\n81: \n82: ### 规则与约束\n83: \n84: - 输出 shape 与输入 shape 完全一致，输出 dtype 与输入 dtype 一致\n85: - `base` 参数：≤ 0 时一律视为自然底数 $e$；> 0 时使用该值作为底数\n86: - `x` 支持任意维度（1D ~ 5D 及更高维），不限制具体 shape\n87: - 需注意数值溢出：float16 的有效范围约 [-65504, 65504]，float32 下 $e^x$ 在 $|x| > 88$ 左右可能溢出为 inf\n88: \n89: ### 支持范围\n90: \n91: 输入 tensor 各维度与参数的支持范围：\n92: \n93: | 维度 / 参数 | 范围 | 备注 |\n94: |---|---|---|\n95: | `x` 维度数 | 1 ~ 8 | cases.csv 实测 1D ~ 5D；逐元素算子，不限维度数 |\n96: | `x` 各维大小 | 1 ~ 1048576 | cases.csv 各维实测 2 ~ 8192（含 1D 张量长度 1000007） |\n97: | `x` 元素总数 | 1 ~ 64M | cases.csv 实测 ~1M ~ 64M |\n98: | `base` | -1.0 ~ 1024.0 | cases.csv 实测 -1.0 / 1.0 / 2.0 / 10.0；≤ 0 时一律视为自然底数 e |\n99: | `scale` | -1024.0 ~ 1024.0 | cases.csv 实测 0.5 ~ 2.0 |\n100: | `shift` | -1024.0 ~ 1024.0 | cases.csv 实测 0.0 ~ 2.0 |\n101: \n102: ## 4. 精度要求\n103: \n104: 采用[生态算子精度标准](https://gitcode.com/cann/opbase/blob/master/docs/zh/ops_precision_standard/experimental_standard.md)进行验证。\n105: \n106: **误差指标**：\n107: \n108: 1. 平均相对误差（MERE）：采样点中相对误差平均值\n109: \n110:    $$\n111:    \\text{MERE} = \\text{avg}(\\frac{\\text{abs}(actual - golden)}{\\text{abs}(golden)+\\text{1e-7}})\n112:    $$\n113: \n114: 2. 最大相对误差（MARE）：采样点中相对误差最大值\n115: \n116:    $$\n117:    \\text{MARE} = \\max(\\frac{\\text{abs}(actual - golden)}{\\text{abs}(golden)+\\text{1e-7}})\n118:    $$\n119: \n120: **通过标准**：\n121: \n122: | 数据类型 | FLOAT16 | BFLOAT16 | FLOAT32 | HiFLOAT32 | FLOAT8 E4M3 | FLOAT8 E5M2 |\n123: |----------|---------|----------|---------|-----------|-------------|-------------|\n124: | **通过阈值(Threshold)** | 2^-10 | 2^-7 | 2^-13 | 2^-11 | 2^-3 | 2^-2 |\n125: \n126: 当平均相对误差 MERE < Threshold，最大相对误差 MARE < 10 * Threshold 时判定为通过。\n127: \n128: \n129: ## 5. 标准 Golden 代码\n130: \n131: ```python\n132: import torch\n133: \n134: def exp(\n135:     x: torch.Tensor, base: float = -1.0, scale: float = 1.0, shift: float = 0.0\n136: ) -> torch.Tensor:\n137:     \"\"\"\n138:     计算输入张量的指数函数\n139: \n140:     - base <= 0: y = exp(scale * x + shift)\n141:     - base > 0: y = exp((shift + scale * x) * ln(base))\n142: \n143:     Args:\n144:         x: 输入张量\n145:         base: 指数底数，base <= 0 表示使用自然底数 e\n146:         scale: 输入缩放因子\n147:         shift: 输入偏移量\n148: \n149:     Returns:\n150:         指数计算结果\n151:     \"\"\"\n152:     # FP16/BF16 输入为保证精度会先提升到 FP32 计算\n153:     input_dtype = x.dtype\n154:     compute_dtype = torch.float32 if input_dtype in (torch.float16, torch.bfloat16) else input_dtype\n155:     x_compute = x.to(compute_dtype)\n156: \n157:     temp = scale * x_compute + shift\n158:     if base > 0:\n159:         temp = temp * torch.log(torch.tensor(base, dtype=temp.dtype, device=temp.device))\n160:     y = torch.exp(temp)\n161: \n162:     # 计算完成后恢复到输入 dtype\n163:     return y.to(input_dtype)\n164: ```\n165: \n166: ## 6. 额外信息\n167: \n168: ### 算子调用示例\n169: \n170: ```python\n171: import torch\n172: import cann_bench\n173: \n174: x = torch.randn(1024, 1024, dtype=torch.float16, device=\"npu\")\n175: y = cann_bench.exp(x, base=-1.0, scale=1.0, shift=0.0)  # 自然指数 e^x\n176: y = cann_bench.exp(x, base=2.0, scale=1.0, shift=0.0)   # 2^x\n177: y = cann_bench.exp(x, base=-1.0, scale=2.0, shift=1.0)  # e^(2x+1)\n178: ```\n179: \n180: ## proto.yaml\n181: \n182: ```yaml\n183: operator:\n184:   name: Exp\n185:   category: Elementwise\n186:   difficulty: L1\n187:   formula: y = e^((x * scale + shift) * ln(base))\n188:   description: 计算输入张量的指数函数，支持自定义底数、缩放和偏移\n189:   shape_support: 输入任意维度，输出与输入相同shape\n190:   attrs:\n191:   - name: base\n192:     type: float\n193:     default: -1.0\n194:     description: 指数底数，-1.0表示使用自然底数e，正值表示自定义底数\n195:   - name: scale\n196:     type: float\n197:     default: 1.0\n198:     description: 输入缩放因子\n199:   - name: shift\n200:     type: float\n201:     default: 0.0\n202:     description: 输入偏移量\n203:   note: 当base=-1时，公式简化为 y = e^(x * scale + shift)\n204:   inputs:\n205:   - name: x\n206:     description: 输入张量\n207:     dtype:\n208:     - float16\n209:     - float32\n210:     - bfloat16\n211:   outputs:\n212:   - name: y\n213:     description: 指数计算结果，输出数据类型与输入一致\n214:     dtype:\n215:     - float16\n216:     - float32\n217:     - bfloat16\n218:   schema: exp(Tensor x, float base=-1.0, float scale=1.0, float shift=0.0) -> Tensor y\n219: ```\n220: \n221: ## golden.py — reference semantics ONLY (you must NOT compute with torch like this; reimplement the math in asc2 kernels)\n222: \n223: ```python\n224: #!/usr/bin/python3\n225: # coding=utf-8\n226: \n227: \n228: import torch\n229: \n230: \"\"\"\n231: Exp算子Torch Golden参考实现\n232: \n233: 计算输入张量的指数函数\n234: - base <= 0: y = exp(scale * x + shift)\n235: - base > 0: y = exp((shift + scale * x) * ln(base))\n236: \"\"\"\n237: def exp(\n238:     x: torch.Tensor, base: float = -1.0, scale: float = 1.0, shift: float = 0.0\n239: ) -> torch.Tensor:\n240:     \"\"\"\n241:     计算输入张量的指数函数\n242: \n243:     - base <= 0: y = exp(scale * x + shift)\n244:     - base > 0: y = exp((shift + scale * x) * ln(base))\n245: \n246:     Args:\n247:         x: 输入张量\n248:         base: 指数底数，base <= 0 表示使用自然底数 e\n249:         scale: 输入缩放因子\n250:         shift: 输入偏移量\n251: \n252:     Returns:\n253:         指数计算结果\n254:     \"\"\"\n255:     # 检测输入 dtype\n256:     input_dtype = x.dtype\n257: \n258:     # FP16/BF16 输入需要升到 FP32 计算以保证精度\n259:     # FP32/FP64 输入保持原样计算\n260:     if input_dtype in (torch.float16, torch.bfloat16):\n261:         compute_dtype = torch.float32\n262:     else:\n263:         compute_dtype = input_dtype\n264: \n265:     # 转换到计算精度\n266:     x_compute = x.to(compute_dtype)\n267: \n268:     temp = scale * x_compute + shift\n269:     if base > 0:\n270:         temp = temp * torch.log(torch.tensor(base, dtype=temp.dtype, device=temp.device))\n271:     y = torch.exp(temp)\n272: \n273:     # 转回原始 dtype\n274:     if input_dtype in (torch.float16, torch.bfloat16):\n275:         return y.to(input_dtype)\n276:     return y\n277: ```\n278: \n279: ## Evaluation cases your module must handle (shapes, dtypes, value ranges, attrs)\n280: \n281: | case | shapes | dtype | value_range | attrs |\n282: |---|---|---|---|---|\n283: | 1 | [[1024, 1024]] | ['float16'] | [-1, 1] | {'base': -1.0, 'scale': 1.0, 'shift': 0.0} |\n284: | 2 | [[2048, 2048]] | ['float32'] | [-2, 2] | {'base': -1.0, 'scale': 1.5, 'shift': 0.0} |\n285: | 3 | [[4096, 4096]] | ['bfloat16'] | [-3, 3] | {'base': -1.0, 'scale': 2.0, 'shift': 0.0} |\n286: | 4 | [[8192, 8192]] | ['float16'] | [-10, 10] | {'base': -1.0, 'scale': 0.5, 'shift': 0.0} |\n287: | 5 | [[8192, 8192]] | ['float32'] | [-100, 100] | {'base': -1.0, 'scale': 1.0, 'shift': 1.0} |\n288: | 6 | [[1023, 1023]] | ['bfloat16'] | [-0.1, 0.1] | {'base': 2.0, 'scale': 1.0, 'shift': 0.0} |\n289: | 7 | [[1009, 1021]] | ['float16'] | [-1, 2] | {'base': 2.0, 'scale': 1.5, 'shift': 0.0} |\n290: | 8 | [[1537, 769]] | ['float32'] | [-5, 10] | {'base': 10.0, 'scale': 1.0, 'shift': 0.0} |\n291: | 9 | [[363, 367, 373]] | ['bfloat16'] | [-50, 100] | {'base': -1.0, 'scale': 2.0, 'shift': 0.5} |\n292: | 10 | [[2049, 513]] | ['float16'] | [-65504, 65504] | {'base': -1.0, 'scale': 1.0, 'shift': 2.0} |\n293: | 11 | [[3, 7, 13, 4003]] | ['float32'] | [-88, 88] | {'base': 1.0, 'scale': 2.0, 'shift': 0.0} |\n294: | 12 | [[1000007]] | ['bfloat16'] | [-inf, inf] | {'base': -1.0, 'scale': 0.5, 'shift': 0.5} |\n295: | 13 | [[11, 13, 17, 67, 67]] | ['float32'] | [nan, nan] | {'base': 2.0, 'scale': 1.0, 'shift': 1.0} |\n296: | 14 | [[3, 7, 11, 13, 1013]] | ['float16'] | [0, 0] | {'base': -1.0, 'scale': 2.0, 'shift': 1.0} |\n297: | 15 | [[512, 2049]] | ['float32'] | [-0.5, 0.5] | {'base': -1.0, 'scale': 1.0, 'shift': 0.5} |\n298: | 16 | [[255, 8193]] | ['bfloat16'] | [-1, 3] | {'base': -1.0, 'scale': 1.2, 'shift': 0.0} |\n299: | 17 | [[4097, 511]] | ['float16'] | [-1000, 1000] | {'base': 1.0, 'scale': 0.5, 'shift': 0.0} |\n300: | 18 | [[2, 511, 2049]] | ['float32'] | [-0.2, 0.2] | {'base': 2.0, 'scale': 0.5, 'shift': 0.0} |\n301: | 19 | [[4, 255, 2049]] | ['bfloat16'] | [-3, 6] | {'base': 10.0, 'scale': 0.5, 'shift': 0.5} |\n302: | 20 | [[2, 3, 17, 1024, 101]] | ['float16'] | [-20, 40] | {'base': 1.0, 'scale': 1.5, 'shift': 1.0} |\n303: \n304: # Reference module — sigmoid.py from this submission (structure to copy; it scores 100% accuracy on this harness)\n305: \n306: ```python\n307: \"\"\"CANN Bench Sigmoid interface implemented as a pyasc asc2 kernel.\n308: \n309: Kernel design (pyasc-api-patterns Pattern A, grid-stride variant):\n310:   - 1-D flatten; each block strides over tiles so any element count works.\n311:   - Tail tiles handled with ``real_shape`` loads/stores (no host padding).\n312:   - f16/bf16 inputs are promoted to f32 inside the kernel for precision\n313:     (the spec's precision standard expects f32 internal compute).\n314:   - Host selects between two compiled tile sizes: a wide tile (3072) when\n315:     the element count fills all 72 cores, otherwise a narrow tile (1024) to\n316:     maximize core utilization on small shapes.\n317:   - sigmoid(x) = 1 / (1 + e^(-x)); IEEE saturation gives the correct\n318:     limits at extreme inputs (e^inf -> inf -> y=0, e^-inf -> 0 -> y=1).\n319: \"\"\"\n320: \n321: import torch\n322: \n323: import asc\n324: import asc2\n325: \n326: from ._pyasc_runtime import ensure_npu_platform\n327: \n328: _WIDE_TILE = 3072\n329: _NARROW_TILE = 1024\n330: _MAX_CORES = 72\n331: \n332: \n333: @asc2.jit\n334: def _sigmoid_kernel(x_ptr: asc.GlobalAddress, out_ptr: asc.GlobalAddress,\n335:                     size: int, num_tiles: int, tile_size: asc.ConstExpr[int]):\n336:     x_gm = asc2.global_tensor(x_ptr, [size])\n337:     out_gm = asc2.global_tensor(out_ptr, [size])\n338:     for t in asc2.range(asc2.block_idx(), num_tiles, asc2.block_num(),\n339:                         unroll_factor=2):\n340:         off = t * tile_size\n341:         n = tile_size if off + tile_size <= size else size - off\n342:         x = asc2.copy_in(x_gm, [off], [tile_size], real_shape=[n])\n343:         xf = x.to(asc.float32)\n344:         y = asc2.div(1.0, asc2.exp(-xf) + 1.0)\n345:         asc2.copy_out(y.to(x.dtype), out_gm, [off], real_shape=[n])\n346: \n347: \n348: def sigmoid(x: torch.Tensor) -> torch.Tensor:\n349:     \"\"\"Element-wise sigmoid of an NPU tensor via a pyasc asc2 kernel.\"\"\"\n350:     ensure_npu_platform()\n351:     if not x.is_contiguous():\n352:         x = x.contiguous()\n353:     out = torch.empty_like(x)\n354:     size = x.numel()\n355:     if size == 0:\n356:         return out\n357:     if size >= _MAX_CORES * _WIDE_TILE:\n358:         num_tiles = asc.ceildiv(size, _WIDE_TILE)\n359:         cores = min(_MAX_CORES, num_tiles)\n360:         _sigmoid_kernel[cores](x, out, size, num_tiles, _WIDE_TILE)\n361:     else:\n362:         num_tiles = asc.ceildiv(size, _NARROW_TILE)\n363:         cores = min(_MAX_CORES, num_tiles)\n364:         _sigmoid_kernel[cores](x, out, size, num_tiles, _NARROW_TILE)\n365:     return out\n366: \n367: ```\n368: \n369: # pyasc asc2 kernel contract (follow EXACTLY — every rule below was learned from real failures on this hardware)\n370: \n371: ## Module shape\n372: \n373: Your file becomes `cann_bench/<module>.py` inside the submission wheel. It must contain:\n374: \n375: - imports at module top: `import torch`, `import asc`, `import asc2`,\n376:   `from ._pyasc_runtime import ensure_npu_platform` (and `import math` if needed)\n377: - one or more `@asc2.jit` kernel functions\n378: - ONE public callable matching the operator schema exactly (name and signature)\n379: - wrapper body: call `ensure_npu_platform()` first; make inputs contiguous if\n380:   needed (`x = x.contiguous()` is allowed); allocate outputs with\n381:   `torch.empty_like(x)` or `torch.empty(shape, dtype=..., device=x.device)`;\n382:   launch `kernel[cores](tensor_args..., int_args..., float_args..., constexpr_args...)`;\n383:   return contiguous NPU tensor(s)\n384: \n385: ## Kernel authoring rules\n386: \n387: - Global memory views: `asc2.global_tensor(ptr, [size])` (1-D) or\n388:   `asc2.global_tensor(ptr, [rows, cols])` (2-D). Ranks of global_tensor /\n389:   copy_in / copy_out / offsets must ALL match — never mix 1-D and 2-D.\n390: - Kernel params: pointers typed `asc.GlobalAddress`; sizes as plain `int`\n391:   (runtime); tile sizes as `asc.ConstExpr[int]` (compile-time; REQUIRED for any\n392:   value used inside a copy_in tile shape); scalars as `float`.\n393: - Grid-stride tile loop (the proven pattern):\n394: \n395: ```python\n396: for t in asc2.range(asc2.block_idx(), num_tiles, asc2.block_num(), unroll_factor=2):\n397:     off = t * tile_size\n398:     n = tile_size if off + tile_size <= size else size - off   # tail handling\n399:     x = asc2.copy_in(x_gm, [off], [tile_size], real_shape=[n])\n400:     ...compute on tiles...\n401:     asc2.copy_out(y.to(x.dtype), out_gm, [off], real_shape=[n])\n402: ```\n403: \n404: - Launch: `kernel[cores](...)` with `cores = min(72, num_tiles)` (72 AIV cores\n405:   on this 950PR box). No stream argument.\n406: - Available tile ops: `+ - * /` (tile-tile and tile-scalar), `asc2.abs`,\n407:   `asc2.exp`, `asc2.exp2`, `asc2.log`, `asc2.log2`, `asc2.sqrt`,\n408:   `asc2.rsqrt`, `asc2.tanh`, `asc2.erf`, `asc2.sin`, `asc2.cos`,\n409:   `asc2.floor`, `asc2.ceil`, `asc2.relu`, `asc2.maximum`, `asc2.minimum`,\n410:   comparisons (`x >= 0.0`, `asc2.less(a, b)`, ... — NO int64 operands),\n411:   `asc2.where(cond, a, b)`, `asc2.reduce_sum(x)`, `asc2.reduce_max(x)`,\n412:   `asc2.reduce_min(x)`, `asc2.full([shape], scalar, dtype=...)`,\n413:   `asc2.cast(tile, dtype)` / `tile.to(dtype)` casts, integer\n414:   `asc2.left_shift`/`asc2.right_shift`, tile-shape ops `asc2.reshape`,\n415:   `asc2.transpose`, `asc2.ravel`, `asc2.expand_dims`, `asc2.squeeze`,\n416:   `asc2.broadcast_to`, `asc2.concat`, unary `-x`.\n417: - int8 tiles: loading (copy_in) is fine but NO vector op accepts int8 input\n418:   (not even `.to`); convert with `asc2.cast(t, asc.float16)` first. There is\n419:   no uint8 tile dtype at all.\n420: - Scalars go on the RIGHT of tile arithmetic (Tile has no `__rmul__`):\n421:   write `x * 0.5`, NEVER `0.5 * x`. Same for `+ - /`.\n422: - f16/bf16 inputs: promote to f32 in-kernel (`xf = x.to(asc.float32)`),\n423:   compute in f32, cast back on copy_out (`y.to(x.dtype)`).\n424: - UB (unified buffer) budget: ~253952 bytes total under static allocation.\n425:   Every distinct f32 tile value costs `4 * TILE` bytes, `unroll_factor=2`\n426:   doubles the total, and the compiler adds hidden temporaries — MEASURED\n427:   calibration: the sigmoid chain (f16 load, f32 cast, `-x`, `exp`, `+1`,\n428:   `div`, f16 store ≈ 6 visible values) uses 155648 bytes at TILE=2048 and\n429:   311296 (OVERFLOW) at TILE=4096, i.e. real usage ≈ 1.6x the naive\n430:   `visible_values * 4 * TILE * 2` estimate. Budget with that 1.6x factor.\n431:   Rule of thumb: TILE=2048 for short chains (< 8 values), 1024 for medium,\n432:   512 for long (> 16). A launch failing with `RuntimeError: UB overflow: X\n433:   bytes are available, Y bytes are used` means: halve TILE (do NOT drop\n434:   cases).\n435: - `asc2.where` / comparison destination tiles must be a multiple of 256 bytes\n436:   (`TILE * 4 % 256 == 0` for f32 — any TILE >= 64 is safe).\n437: - Loop-carried scalar accumulators (VERIFIED on this build): seed with\n438:   `acc = asc2.reduce_sum(asc2.full([1, 64], 0.0, dtype=asc.float32))`\n439:   (a bare `acc = 0.0` fails codegen with \"re-assigned to an object with\n440:   different type\"), then `acc = acc + asc2.reduce_sum(x)` inside a plain\n441:   `asc2.range(...)` loop. `asc2.range` accepts ONLY `unroll_factor` and\n442:   `parallel` — there is NO `gm_barrier` kwarg on this build (it raises\n443:   TypeError).\n444: - Cross-core reductions (VERIFIED): `asc2.atomic_add(src_tile, dst_gm,\n445:   [offset])` atomically accumulates a tile into global memory (dtypes int16/\n446:   int32/f16/bf16/f32; also `asc2.atomic_max`). Host must zero the\n447:   destination first (`torch.zeros(...)` — tensor creation is allowed).\n448:   Pattern: each core reduce_sums its tiles into a scalar, widens it with\n449:   `asc2.full([8], s, dtype=...)`, and atomic_adds slot [0]; a second tiny\n450:   kernel (or the same one on one core) applies any final transform.\n451: - Scalar reduction results must be widened before store:\n452:   `asc2.copy_out(asc2.full([8], s, dtype=...), out_gm, [0], real_shape=[8])`\n453:   style (min 32 bytes).\n454: - Inside `@asc2.jit`: NO `print`, NO imports, NO `break`/`continue`/early\n455:   `return`, NO exceptions, NO Python `range()` over runtime values (use\n456:   `asc2.range`), NO `math.*` calls (precompute module-level constants).\n457: \n458: ## Numerical stability (MANDATORY — f32 cases use ranges like [-88, 88] and [-100, 100] under a ~1.2e-4 relative-error threshold)\n459: \n460: - Never let `exp()` see a positive argument that can overflow; never subtract\n461:   nearly-equal quantities (catastrophic cancellation); never rely on\n462:   `log(1 + tiny)` (flushes to 0 below tiny < 6e-8).\n463: - Proven cancellation-free building blocks (all verified on this harness):\n464:   - `sigmoid(s) = exp(min(s, 0)) / (1 + exp(-|s|))`\n465:   - `1 + tanh(u) = 2 * sigmoid(2u)`\n466:   - `tanh(softplus(x))`: with `w = exp(-|x|)`, equals\n467:     `(1 + 2w) / (1 + 2w + 2w^2)` for `x >= 0`, `(w^2 + 2w) / (w^2 + 2w + 2)`\n468:     for `x < 0` (exact identities; blend with `asc2.where(xf >= 0.0, ...)`)\n469:   - `erfc(z)` for `z >= 0`: Numerical Recipes fit `t * exp(-z*z + P(t))`,\n470:     `t = 1/(1 + z/2)`, rel. err < 1.2e-7 (see the gelu reference module for\n471:     the 9-coefficient Horner chain)\n472: - IEEE special values (inf/nan scalars or extreme inputs) propagate correctly\n473:   through the hardware ops — do NOT special-case them with host branches\n474:   unless the golden does.\n475: \n476: ## Anti-cheat (violations zero the submission)\n477: \n478: - ALL numerical work happens inside `@asc2.jit` kernels launched on the NPU.\n479: - torch usage is allowed ONLY for: output allocation (`torch.empty`,\n480:   `torch.empty_like`), metadata (`.shape`, `.numel()`, `.stride()`, `.dtype`,\n481:   `.is_contiguous()`), contiguity (`.contiguous()`), and views (`.view`,\n482:   `.reshape`, `.narrow`, indexing that returns a view).\n483: - FORBIDDEN anywhere in the module: torch math/compute ops (`torch.mul`,\n484:   `torch.norm`, `torch.nn.functional.*`, tensor arithmetic like `a + b`,\n485:   `x.sigmoid()`, `.to(dtype)` casts of device data, `torch.cat`,\n486:   `torch.clone`, `torch.sum`, ...). The harness hooks torch dispatch and\n487:   rotates input data pointers between calls — caching outputs by `data_ptr`\n488:   is detected and scored as cheating.\n489: - Outputs must be contiguous NPU tensors with exactly the golden's\n490:   shape/dtype. Do not return views of inputs.\n491: \n492: \n493: # Operator-specific guidance\n494: \n495: -\n496: \n497: # Deliverable\n498: \n499: Write ONLY `candidate.py` (complete, self-contained module). Public callable named exactly `exp` with the exact schema signature including attr defaults. Think hard about numerical stability across the full value ranges listed above, and about the UB budget for your op chain before picking TILE.\n500: \n501: IMPORTANT — no local execution: pyasc/asc/asc2/torch_npu are NOT installed on this machine and there is no NPU here. Do NOT run, import, compile-check, or test your code, and do not install packages — any such attempt wastes your entire time budget. Reason statically, write `candidate.py`, optionally run `python3 -m py_compile candidate.py` (syntax only), then STOP and reply DONE.\n\n(End of file - total 501 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"You are implementing ONE operator for the CANN Bench benchmark (https://gitcode.com/cann/cann-bench) as a pyasc asc2 JIT kernel module targeting an Ascend 950PR NPU. You have NO NPU access — the official harness evaluates your file on real hardware after you finish. Score per operator = 0.2 compile + 0.3 accuracy (all 20 cases must pass a relative-error check against golden.py) + 0.5 performance (profiler-measured kernel time vs an aclnn baseline).\n\n# Task\n\nOperator: **Exp**. Write the file `candidate.py` in the current working directory: a complete Python module whose public callable `exp` implements the schema below. Nothing else — no tests, no other files.\n\n# Operator specification\n\n## desc.md\n\n# Exp 算子 API 描述\n\n## 1. 算子简介\n\nExp 算子用于计算输入张量的广义指数函数，支持自定义底数（base）、缩放因子（scale）和偏移量（shift）三个参数，涵盖自然指数、任意底数指数等多种变体。\n\n**主要应用场景**：\n- Softmax 中的自然指数计算\n- 注意力机制中的指数缩放\n- 概率分布与对数域间的转换","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/aloschilov/workspace/pyasc-skill-stack/integrations/cannbench/workers/.scratch/20260902_140812/exp-generate/task.md","text":"You are implementing ONE operator for the CANN Bench benchmark (https://gitcode.com/cann/cann-bench) as a pyasc asc2 JIT kernel module targeting an Ascend 950PR NPU. You have NO NPU access — the official harness evaluates your file on real hardware after you finish. Score per operator = 0.2 compile + 0.3 accuracy (all 20 cases must pass a relative-error check against golden.py) + 0.5 performance (profiler-measured kernel time vs an aclnn baseline).\n\n# Task\n\nOperator: **Exp**. Write the file `candidate.py` in the current working directory: a complete Python module whose public callable `exp` implements the schema below. Nothing else — no tests, no other files.\n\n# Operator specification\n\n## desc.md\n\n# Exp 算子 API 描述\n\n## 1. 算子简介\n\nExp 算子用于计算输入张量的广义指数函数，支持自定义底数（base）、缩放因子（scale）和偏移量（shift）三个参数，涵盖自然指数、任意底数指数等多种变体。\n\n**主要应用场景**：\n- Softmax 中的自然指数计算\n- 注意力机制中的指数缩放\n- 概率分布与对数域间的转换\n- 学习率调度与指数衰减\n\n**算子特征**：\n- 难度等级：L1（Elementwise）\n- 单输入单输出，逐元素运算，输出 shape 与输入完全一致\n\n## 2. 算子定义\n\n### 数学公式\n\n**通用公式**：\n\n$$\ny = e^{(x \\cdot scale + shift) \\cdot \\ln(base)}, \\quad base > 0\n$$\n\n**自然指数**（当 $base \\leq 0$ 时，使用自然底数 $e$）：\n\n$$\ny = e^{x \\cdot scale + shift}\n$$\n\n### 特殊情况\n\n| 条件 | 简化公式 |\n|------|---------|\n| base ≤ 0, scale=1, shift=0 | $y = e^x$ |\n| base > 0, scale=1, shift=0 | $y = base^x$ |\n| base=1（任意 scale, shift） | $y = 1$（因 $\\ln 1 = 0$） |\n\n## 3. 接口规范\n\n### 算子原型\n\n```python\ncann_bench.exp(Tensor x, float base=-1.0, float scale=1.0, float shift=0.0) -> Tensor y\n```\n\n### 输入参数说明\n\n| 参数 | 类型 | 默认值 | 描述 |\n|------|------|--------|------|\n| x | Tensor | 必选 | 输入张量，支持任意维度 |\n| base | float | -1.0 | 指数底数；≤ 0 表示使用自然底数 $e$，> 0 表示自定义底数 |\n| scale | float | 1.0 | 输入缩放因子 |\n| shift | float | 0.0 | 输入偏移量 |\n\n### 输出\n\n| 参数 | Shape | dtype | 描述 |\n|------|-------|-------|------|\n| y | 与输入 x 相同 | 与输入 x 相同 | 指数计算结果 |\n\n### 数据类型\n\n| 输入 dtype | 输出 dtype |\n|-----------|-----------|\n| float16 | float16 |\n| float32 | float32 |\n| bfloat16 | bfloat16 |\n\n### 规则与约束\n\n- 输出 shape 与输入 shape 完全一致，输出 dtype 与输入 dtype 一致\n- `base` 参数：≤ 0 时一律视为自然底数 $e$；> 0 时使用该值作为底数\n- `x` 支持任意维度（1D ~ 5D 及更高维），不限制具体 shape\n- 需注意数值溢出：float16 的有效范围约 [-65504, 65504]，float32 下 $e^x$ 在 $|x| > 88$ 左右可能溢出为 inf\n\n### 支持范围\n\n输入 tensor 各维度与参数的支持范围：\n\n| 维度 / 参数 | 范围 | 备注 |\n|---|---|---|\n| `x` 维度数 | 1 ~ 8 | cases.csv 实测 1D ~ 5D；逐元素算子，不限维度数 |\n| `x` 各维大小 | 1 ~ 1048576 | cases.csv 各维实测 2 ~ 8192（含 1D 张量长度 1000007） |\n| `x` 元素总数 | 1 ~ 64M | cases.csv 实测 ~1M ~ 64M |\n| `base` | -1.0 ~ 1024.0 | cases.csv 实测 -1.0 / 1.0 / 2.0 / 10.0；≤ 0 时一律视为自然底数 e |\n| `scale` | -1024.0 ~ 1024.0 | cases.csv 实测 0.5 ~ 2.0 |\n| `shift` | -1024.0 ~ 1024.0 | cases.csv 实测 0.0 ~ 2.0 |\n\n## 4. 精度要求\n\n采用[生态算子精度标准](https://gitcode.com/cann/opbase/blob/master/docs/zh/ops_precision_standard/experimental_standard.md)进行验证。\n\n**误差指标**：\n\n1. 平均相对误差（MERE）：采样点中相对误差平均值\n\n   $$\n   \\text{MERE} = \\text{avg}(\\frac{\\text{abs}(actual - golden)}{\\text{abs}(golden)+\\text{1e-7}})\n   $$\n\n2. 最大相对误差（MARE）：采样点中相对误差最大值\n\n   $$\n   \\text{MARE} = \\max(\\frac{\\text{abs}(actual - golden)}{\\text{abs}(golden)+\\text{1e-7}})\n   $$\n\n**通过标准**：\n\n| 数据类型 | FLOAT16 | BFLOAT16 | FLOAT32 | HiFLOAT32 | FLOAT8 E4M3 | FLOAT8 E5M2 |\n|----------|---------|----------|---------|-----------|-------------|-------------|\n| **通过阈值(Threshold)** | 2^-10 | 2^-7 | 2^-13 | 2^-11 | 2^-3 | 2^-2 |\n\n当平均相对误差 MERE < Threshold，最大相对误差 MARE < 10 * Threshold 时判定为通过。\n\n\n## 5. 标准 Golden 代码\n\n```python\nimport torch\n\ndef exp(\n    x: torch.Tensor, base: float = -1.0, scale: float = 1.0, shift: float = 0.0\n) -> torch.Tensor:\n    \"\"\"\n    计算输入张量的指数函数\n\n    - base <= 0: y = exp(scale * x + shift)\n    - base > 0: y = exp((shift + scale * x) * ln(base))\n\n    Args:\n        x: 输入张量\n        base: 指数底数，base <= 0 表示使用自然底数 e\n        scale: 输入缩放因子\n        shift: 输入偏移量\n\n    Returns:\n        指数计算结果\n    \"\"\"\n    # FP16/BF16 输入为保证精度会先提升到 FP32 计算\n    input_dtype = x.dtype\n    compute_dtype = torch.float32 if input_dtype in (torch.float16, torch.bfloat16) else input_dtype\n    x_compute = x.to(compute_dtype)\n\n    temp = scale * x_compute + shift\n    if base > 0:\n        temp = temp * torch.log(torch.tensor(base, dtype=temp.dtype, device=temp.device))\n    y = torch.exp(temp)\n\n    # 计算完成后恢复到输入 dtype\n    return y.to(input_dtype)\n```\n\n## 6. 额外信息\n\n### 算子调用示例\n\n```python\nimport torch\nimport cann_bench\n\nx = torch.randn(1024, 1024, dtype=torch.float16, device=\"npu\")\ny = cann_bench.exp(x, base=-1.0, scale=1.0, shift=0.0)  # 自然指数 e^x\ny = cann_bench.exp(x, base=2.0, scale=1.0, shift=0.0)   # 2^x\ny = cann_bench.exp(x, base=-1.0, scale=2.0, shift=1.0)  # e^(2x+1)\n```\n\n## proto.yaml\n\n```yaml\noperator:\n  name: Exp\n  category: Elementwise\n  difficulty: L1\n  formula: y = e^((x * scale + shift) * ln(base))\n  description: 计算输入张量的指数函数，支持自定义底数、缩放和偏移\n  shape_support: 输入任意维度，输出与输入相同shape\n  attrs:\n  - name: base\n    type: float\n    default: -1.0\n    description: 指数底数，-1.0表示使用自然底数e，正值表示自定义底数\n  - name: scale\n    type: float\n    default: 1.0\n    description: 输入缩放因子\n  - name: shift\n    type: float\n    default: 0.0\n    description: 输入偏移量\n  note: 当base=-1时，公式简化为 y = e^(x * scale + shift)\n  inputs:\n  - name: x\n    description: 输入张量\n    dtype:\n    - float16\n    - float32\n    - bfloat16\n  outputs:\n  - name: y\n    description: 指数计算结果，输出数据类型与输入一致\n    dtype:\n    - float16\n    - float32\n    - bfloat16\n  schema: exp(Tensor x, float base=-1.0, float scale=1.0, float shift=0.0) -> Tensor y\n```\n\n## golden.py — reference semantics ONLY (you must NOT compute with torch like this; reimplement the math in asc2 kernels)\n\n```python\n#!/usr/bin/python3\n# coding=utf-8\n\n\nimport torch\n\n\"\"\"\nExp算子Torch Golden参考实现\n\n计算输入张量的指数函数\n- base <= 0: y = exp(scale * x + shift)\n- base > 0: y = exp((shift + scale * x) * ln(base))\n\"\"\"\ndef exp(\n    x: torch.Tensor, base: float = -1.0, scale: float = 1.0, shift: float = 0.0\n) -> torch.Tensor:\n    \"\"\"\n    计算输入张量的指数函数\n\n    - base <= 0: y = exp(scale * x + shift)\n    - base > 0: y = exp((shift + scale * x) * ln(base))\n\n    Args:\n        x: 输入张量\n        base: 指数底数，base <= 0 表示使用自然底数 e\n        scale: 输入缩放因子\n        shift: 输入偏移量\n\n    Returns:\n        指数计算结果\n    \"\"\"\n    # 检测输入 dtype\n    input_dtype = x.dtype\n\n    # FP16/BF16 输入需要升到 FP32 计算以保证精度\n    # FP32/FP64 输入保持原样计算\n    if input_dtype in (torch.float16, torch.bfloat16):\n        compute_dtype = torch.float32\n    else:\n        compute_dtype = input_dtype\n\n    # 转换到计算精度\n    x_compute = x.to(compute_dtype)\n\n    temp = scale * x_compute + shift\n    if base > 0:\n        temp = temp * torch.log(torch.tensor(base, dtype=temp.dtype, device=temp.device))\n    y = torch.exp(temp)\n\n    # 转回原始 dtype\n    if input_dtype in (torch.float16, torch.bfloat16):\n        return y.to(input_dtype)\n    return y\n```\n\n## Evaluation cases your module must handle (shapes, dtypes, value ranges, attrs)\n\n| case | shapes | dtype | value_range | attrs |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | [[1024, 1024]] | ['float16'] | [-1, 1] | {'base': -1.0, 'scale': 1.0, 'shift': 0.0} |\n| 2 | [[2048, 2048]] | ['float32'] | [-2, 2] | {'base': -1.0, 'scale': 1.5, 'shift': 0.0} |\n| 3 | [[4096, 4096]] | ['bfloat16'] | [-3, 3] | {'base': -1.0, 'scale': 2.0, 'shift': 0.0} |\n| 4 | [[8192, 8192]] | ['float16'] | [-10, 10] | {'base': -1.0, 'scale': 0.5, 'shift': 0.0} |\n| 5 | [[8192, 8192]] | ['float32'] | [-100, 100] | {'base': -1.0, 'scale': 1.0, 'shift': 1.0} |\n| 6 | [[1023, 1023]] | ['bfloat16'] | [-0.1, 0.1] | {'base': 2.0, 'scale': 1.0, 'shift': 0.0} |\n| 7 | [[1009, 1021]] | ['float16'] | [-1, 2] | {'base': 2.0, 'scale': 1.5, 'shift': 0.0} |\n| 8 | [[1537, 769]] | ['float32'] | [-5, 10] | {'base': 10.0, 'scale': 1.0, 'shift': 0.0} |\n| 9 | [[363, 367, 373]] | ['bfloat16'] | [-50, 100] | {'base': -1.0, 'scale': 2.0, 'shift': 0.5} |\n| 10 | [[2049, 513]] | ['float16'] | [-65504, 65504] | {'base': -1.0, 'scale': 1.0, 'shift': 2.0} |\n| 11 | [[3, 7, 13, 4003]] | ['float32'] | [-88, 88] | {'base': 1.0, 'scale': 2.0, 'shift': 0.0} |\n| 12 | [[1000007]] | ['bfloat16'] | [-inf, inf] | {'base': -1.0, 'scale': 0.5, 'shift': 0.5} |\n| 13 | [[11, 13, 17, 67, 67]] | ['float32'] | [nan, nan] | {'base': 2.0, 'scale': 1.0, 'shift': 1.0} |\n| 14 | [[3, 7, 11, 13, 1013]] | ['float16'] | [0, 0] | {'base': -1.0, 'scale': 2.0, 'shift': 1.0} |\n| 15 | [[512, 2049]] | ['float32'] | [-0.5, 0.5] | {'base': -1.0, 'scale': 1.0, 'shift': 0.5} |\n| 16 | [[255, 8193]] | ['bfloat16'] | [-1, 3] | {'base': -1.0, 'scale': 1.2, 'shift': 0.0} |\n| 17 | [[4097, 511]] | ['float16'] | [-1000, 1000] | {'base': 1.0, 'scale': 0.5, 'shift': 0.0} |\n| 18 | [[2, 511, 2049]] | ['float32'] | [-0.2, 0.2] | {'base': 2.0, 'scale': 0.5, 'shift': 0.0} |\n| 19 | [[4, 255, 2049]] | ['bfloat16'] | [-3, 6] | {'base': 10.0, 'scale': 0.5, 'shift': 0.5} |\n| 20 | [[2, 3, 17, 1024, 101]] | ['float16'] | [-20, 40] | {'base': 1.0, 'scale': 1.5, 'shift': 1.0} |\n\n# Reference module — sigmoid.py from this submission (structure to copy; it scores 100% accuracy on this harness)\n\n```python\n\"\"\"CANN Bench Sigmoid interface implemented as a pyasc asc2 kernel.\n\nKernel design (pyasc-api-patterns Pattern A, grid-stride variant):\n  - 1-D flatten; each block strides over tiles so any element count works.\n  - Tail tiles handled with ``real_shape`` loads/stores (no host padding).\n  - f16/bf16 inputs are promoted to f32 inside the kernel for precision\n    (the spec's precision standard expects f32 internal compute).\n  - Host selects between two compiled tile sizes: a wide tile (3072) when\n    the element count fills all 72 cores, otherwise a narrow tile (1024) to\n    maximize core utilization on small shapes.\n  - sigmoid(x) = 1 / (1 + e^(-x)); IEEE saturation gives the correct\n    limits at extreme inputs (e^inf -> inf -> y=0, e^-inf -> 0 -> y=1).\n\"\"\"\n\nimport torch\n\nimport asc\nimport asc2\n\nfrom ._pyasc_runtime import ensure_npu_platform\n\n_WIDE_TILE = 3072\n_NARROW_TILE = 1024\n_MAX_CORES = 72\n\n\n@asc2.jit\ndef _sigmoid_kernel(x_ptr: asc.GlobalAddress, out_ptr: asc.GlobalAddress,\n                    size: int, num_tiles: int, tile_size: asc.ConstExpr[int]):\n    x_gm = asc2.global_tensor(x_ptr, [size])\n    out_gm = asc2.global_tensor(out_ptr, [size])\n    for t in asc2.range(asc2.block_idx(), num_tiles, asc2.block_num(),\n                        unroll_factor=2):\n        off = t * tile_size\n        n = tile_size if off + tile_size <= size else size - off\n        x = asc2.copy_in(x_gm, [off], [tile_size], real_shape=[n])\n        xf = x.to(asc.float32)\n        y = asc2.div(1.0, asc2.exp(-xf) + 1.0)\n        asc2.copy_out(y.to(x.dtype), out_gm, [off], real_shape=[n])\n\n\ndef sigmoid(x: torch.Tensor) -> torch.Tensor:\n    \"\"\"Element-wise sigmoid of an NPU tensor via a pyasc asc2 kernel.\"\"\"\n    ensure_npu_platform()\n    if not x.is_contiguous():\n        x = x.contiguous()\n    out = torch.empty_like(x)\n    size = x.numel()\n    if size == 0:\n        return out\n    if size >= _MAX_CORES * _WIDE_TILE:\n        num_tiles = asc.ceildiv(size, _WIDE_TILE)\n        cores = min(_MAX_CORES, num_tiles)\n        _sigmoid_kernel[cores](x, out, size, num_tiles, _WIDE_TILE)\n    else:\n        num_tiles = asc.ceildiv(size, _NARROW_TILE)\n        cores = min(_MAX_CORES, num_tiles)\n        _sigmoid_kernel[cores](x, out, size, num_tiles, _NARROW_TILE)\n    return out\n\n```\n\n# pyasc asc2 kernel contract (follow EXACTLY — every rule below was learned from real failures on this hardware)\n\n## Module shape\n\nYour file becomes `cann_bench/<module>.py` inside the submission wheel. It must contain:\n\n- imports at module top: `import torch`, `import asc`, `import asc2`,\n  `from ._pyasc_runtime import ensure_npu_platform` (and `import math` if needed)\n- one or more `@asc2.jit` kernel functions\n- ONE public callable matching the operator schema exactly (name and signature)\n- wrapper body: call `ensure_npu_platform()` first; make inputs contiguous if\n  needed (`x = x.contiguous()` is allowed); allocate outputs with\n  `torch.empty_like(x)` or `torch.empty(shape, dtype=..., device=x.device)`;\n  launch `kernel[cores](tensor_args..., int_args..., float_args..., constexpr_args...)`;\n  return contiguous NPU tensor(s)\n\n## Kernel authoring rules\n\n- Global memory views: `asc2.global_tensor(ptr, [size])` (1-D) or\n  `asc2.global_tensor(ptr, [rows, cols])` (2-D). Ranks of global_tensor /\n  copy_in / copy_out / offsets must ALL match — never mix 1-D and 2-D.\n- Kernel params: pointers typed `asc.GlobalAddress`; sizes as plain `int`\n  (runtime); tile sizes as `asc.ConstExpr[int]` (compile-time; REQUIRED for any\n  value used inside a copy_in tile shape); scalars as `float`.\n- Grid-stride tile loop (the proven pattern):\n\n```python\nfor t in asc2.range(asc2.block_idx(), num_tiles, asc2.block_num(), unroll_factor=2):\n    off = t * tile_size\n    n = tile_size if off + tile_size <= size else size - off   # tail handling\n    x = asc2.copy_in(x_gm, [off], [tile_size], real_shape=[n])\n    ...compute on tiles...\n    asc2.copy_out(y.to(x.dtype), out_gm, [off], real_shape=[n])\n```\n\n- Launch: `kernel[cores](...)` with `cores = min(72, num_tiles)` (72 AIV cores\n  on this 950PR box). No stream argument.\n- Available tile ops: `+ - * /` (tile-tile and tile-scalar), `asc2.abs`,\n  `asc2.exp`, `asc2.exp2`, `asc2.log`, `asc2.log2`, `asc2.sqrt`,\n  `asc2.rsqrt`, `asc2.tanh`, `asc2.erf`, `asc2.sin`, `asc2.cos`,\n  `asc2.floor`, `asc2.ceil`, `asc2.relu`, `asc2.maximum`, `asc2.minimum`,\n  comparisons (`x >= 0.0`, `asc2.less(a, b)`, ... — NO int64 operands),\n  `asc2.where(cond, a, b)`, `asc2.reduce_sum(x)`, `asc2.reduce_max(x)`,\n  `asc2.reduce_min(x)`, `asc2.full([shape], scalar, dtype=...)`,\n  `asc2.cast(tile, dtype)` / `tile.to(dtype)` casts, integer\n  `asc2.left_shift`/`asc2.right_shift`, tile-shape ops `asc2.reshape`,\n  `asc2.transpose`, `asc2.ravel`, `asc2.expand_dims`, `asc2.squeeze`,\n  `asc2.broadcast_to`, `asc2.concat`, unary `-x`.\n- int8 tiles: loading (copy_in) is fine but NO vector op accepts int8 input\n  (not even `.to`); convert with `asc2.cast(t, asc.float16)` first. There is\n  no uint8 tile dtype at all.\n- Scalars go on the RIGHT of tile arithmetic (Tile has no `__rmul__`):\n  write `x * 0.5`, NEVER `0.5 * x`. Same for `+ - /`.\n- f16/bf16 inputs: promote to f32 in-kernel (`xf = x.to(asc.float32)`),\n  compute in f32, cast back on copy_out (`y.to(x.dtype)`).\n- UB (unified buffer) budget: ~253952 bytes total under static allocation.\n  Every distinct f32 tile value costs `4 * TILE` bytes, `unroll_factor=2`\n  doubles the total, and the compiler adds hidden temporaries — MEASURED\n  calibration: the sigmoid chain (f16 load, f32 cast, `-x`, `exp`, `+1`,\n  `div`, f16 store ≈ 6 visible values) uses 155648 bytes at TILE=2048 and\n  311296 (OVERFLOW) at TILE=4096, i.e. real usage ≈ 1.6x the naive\n  `visible_values * 4 * TILE * 2` estimate. Budget with that 1.6x factor.\n  Rule of thumb: TILE=2048 for short chains (< 8 values), 1024 for medium,\n  512 for long (> 16). A launch failing with `RuntimeError: UB overflow: X\n  bytes are available, Y bytes are used` means: halve TILE (do NOT drop\n  cases).\n- `asc2.where` / comparison destination tiles must be a multiple of 256 bytes\n  (`TILE * 4 % 256 == 0` for f32 — any TILE >= 64 is safe).\n- Loop-carried scalar accumulators (VERIFIED on this build): seed with\n  `acc = asc2.reduce_sum(asc2.full([1, 64], 0.0, dtype=asc.float32))`\n  (a bare `acc = 0.0` fails codegen with \"re-assigned to an object with\n  different type\"), then `acc = acc + asc2.reduce_sum(x)` inside a plain\n  `asc2.range(...)` loop. `asc2.range` accepts ONLY `unroll_factor` and\n  `parallel` — there is NO `gm_barrier` kwarg on this build (it raises\n  TypeError).\n- Cross-core reductions (VERIFIED): `asc2.atomic_add(src_tile, dst_gm,\n  [offset])` atomically accumulates a tile into global memory (dtypes int16/\n  int32/f16/bf16/f32; also `asc2.atomic_max`). Host must zero the\n  destination first (`torch.zeros(...)` — tensor creation is allowed).\n  Pattern: each core reduce_sums its tiles into a scalar, widens it with\n  `asc2.full([8], s, dtype=...)`, and atomic_adds slot [0]; a second tiny\n  kernel (or the same one on one core) applies any final transform.\n- Scalar reduction results must be widened before store:\n  `asc2.copy_out(asc2.full([8], s, dtype=...), out_gm, [0], real_shape=[8])`\n  style (min 32 bytes).\n- Inside `@asc2.jit`: NO `print`, NO imports, NO `break`/`continue`/early\n  `return`, NO exceptions, NO Python `range()` over runtime values (use\n  `asc2.range`), NO `math.*` calls (precompute module-level constants).\n\n## Numerical stability (MANDATORY — f32 cases use ranges like [-88, 88] and [-100, 100] under a ~1.2e-4 relative-error threshold)\n\n- Never let `exp()` see a positive argument that can overflow; never subtract\n  nearly-equal quantities (catastrophic cancellation); never rely on\n  `log(1 + tiny)` (flushes to 0 below tiny < 6e-8).\n- Proven cancellation-free building blocks (all verified on this harness):\n  - `sigmoid(s) = exp(min(s, 0)) / (1 + exp(-|s|))`\n  - `1 + tanh(u) = 2 * sigmoid(2u)`\n  - `tanh(softplus(x))`: with `w = exp(-|x|)`, equals\n    `(1 + 2w) / (1 + 2w + 2w^2)` for `x >= 0`, `(w^2 + 2w) / (w^2 + 2w + 2)`\n    for `x < 0` (exact identities; blend with `asc2.where(xf >= 0.0, ...)`)\n  - `erfc(z)` for `z >= 0`: Numerical Recipes fit `t * exp(-z*z + P(t))`,\n    `t = 1/(1 + z/2)`, rel. err < 1.2e-7 (see the gelu reference module for\n    the 9-coefficient Horner chain)\n- IEEE special values (inf/nan scalars or extreme inputs) propagate correctly\n  through the hardware ops — do NOT special-case them with host branches\n  unless the golden does.\n\n## Anti-cheat (violations zero the submission)\n\n- ALL numerical work happens inside `@asc2.jit` kernels launched on the NPU.\n- torch usage is allowed ONLY for: output allocation (`torch.empty`,\n  `torch.empty_like`), metadata (`.shape`, `.numel()`, `.stride()`, `.dtype`,\n  `.is_contiguous()`), contiguity (`.contiguous()`), and views (`.view`,\n  `.reshape`, `.narrow`, indexing that returns a view).\n- FORBIDDEN anywhere in the module: torch math/compute ops (`torch.mul`,\n  `torch.norm`, `torch.nn.functional.*`, tensor arithmetic like `a + b`,\n  `x.sigmoid()`, `.to(dtype)` casts of device data, `torch.cat`,\n  `torch.clone`, `torch.sum`, ...). The harness hooks torch dispatch and\n  rotates input data pointers between calls — caching outputs by `data_ptr`\n  is detected and scored as cheating.\n- Outputs must be contiguous NPU tensors with exactly the golden's\n  shape/dtype. Do not return views of inputs.\n\n\n# Operator-specific guidance\n\n-\n\n# Deliverable\n\nWrite ONLY `candidate.py` (complete, self-contained module). Public callable named exactly `exp` with the exact schema signature including attr defaults. Think hard about numerical stability across the full value ranges listed above, and about the UB budget for your op chain before picking TILE.\n\nIMPORTANT — no local execution: pyasc/asc/asc2/torch_npu are NOT installed on this machine and there is no NPU here. Do NOT run, import, compile-check, or test your code, and do not install packages — any such attempt wastes your entire time budget. Reason statically, write `candidate.py`, optionally run `python3 -m py_compile candidate.py` (syntax only), then STOP and reply DONE.","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":501,"totalLines":501,"truncated":false}},"title":"integrations/cannbench/workers/.scratch/20260902_140812/exp-generate/task.md","time":{"start":1788347609626,"end":1788347609634}},"id":"prt_061d2f06a001Xau2zaFqLWkfT7","sessionID":"ses_f9e2d34cdffe0eE32xFqUVyL7D","messageID":"msg_061d2eb45001MmbHeUBgWo7fpn"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788347610048,"sessionID":"ses_f9e2d34cdffe0eE32xFqUVyL7D","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_1c886583f2e442c5826c0835","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/aloschilov/workspace/pyasc-skill-stack/integrations/cannbench/workers/.scratch/20260902_140812/exp-generate/design.md"},"output":"<path>/home/aloschilov/workspace/pyasc-skill-stack/integrations/cannbench/workers/.scratch/20260902_140812/exp-generate/design.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # Exp — design.md (DESIGN phase, provenance-gated)\n2: \n3: Model: glm-5.2 (dashscope/glm-5.2). Skills loaded: `pyasc-cannbench-kernel`,\n4: `pyasc-syntax-constraints`. Implementation context: task.md + loaded skill\n5: references only. No submission modules, run artifacts, or pyasc source\n6: inspected. No `candidate.py` written in this phase.\n7: \n8: ## 1. Operator & algorithm\n9: \n10: Elementwise `Exp`. Output shape/dtype == input shape/dtype. dtypes: f16, f32,\n11: bf16. Golden (reference only, never used at runtime) computes in f32 and casts\n12: back; the candidate must reproduce that f32-internal math inside one\n13: `@asc2.jit` kernel.\n14: \n15: Unified formula (matches golden's f32 associativity exactly):\n16: ```\n17: factor = 1.0           if base <= 0.0      # natural e\n18: factor = log(base)     if base >  0.0      # log() computed on host in float64,\n19:                                           # rounds to the same f32 as torch.log\n20: arg   = (xf * scale + shift) * factor      # f32, golden's exact order\n21: y     = exp(arg)                           # f32\n22: store = y.to(input_dtype)                  # f16/bf16/f32\n23: ```\n24: - `base <= 0` → factor=1.0 (multiply-by-1.0 is exact for finite/inf/nan in\n25:   IEEE, so arg == `scale*x+shift`, identical to golden's natural-e branch).\n26: - `base > 0` → factor=math.log(base); for base==1.0, math.log(1.0)==0.0\n27:   exactly → arg==0.0 → y==1.0 for all finite inputs (cases 11/17/20).\n28: - No host special-casing of base==1, inf, or nan: the unified expression plus\n29:   IEEE propagation through `asc2.exp` reproduces golden's special-value\n30:   behavior (contract: \"do NOT special-case IEEE values unless the golden\n31:   does\").\n32: \n33: ## 2. Pinned-v2 APIs (CANNBench runtime, no load/store spelling)\n34: \n35: - `asc2.global_tensor(ptr, [size])` — 1-D GM view (x and out).\n36: - `asc2.copy_in(gm, [off], [tile_size], real_shape=[n])` — load tile (tail via\n37:   `real_shape`).\n38: - `asc2.copy_out(tile, gm, [off], real_shape=[n])` — store tile.\n39: - `tile.to(asc.float32)` / `tile.to(x.dtype)` — promote f16/bf16→f32 and back.\n40: - `asc2.exp(tile)` — core transcendental (IEEE saturation to +inf / flush to 0).\n41: - tile `*` scalar, `+` scalar (scalars on the RIGHT per contract).\n42: - `asc2.range(asc2.block_idx(), num_tiles, asc2.block_num(), unroll_factor=2)`\n43:   — grid-stride tile loop.\n44: - `asc.ceildiv(size, tile)` (host) + `kernel[cores](...)` launch, cores =\n45:   min(72, num_tiles). `asc.ConstExpr[int]` for tile_size. `ensure_npu_platform`\n46:   first call. Target Ascend950PR_9599, ≤72 AIV cores, UB 253952 B.\n47: \n48: Kernel signature (mirrors the proven sigmoid template):\n49: ```\n50: @asc2.jit\n51: def _exp_kernel(x_ptr: asc.GlobalAddress, out_ptr: asc.GlobalAddress,\n52:                 size: int, num_tiles: int, scale: float, shift: float,\n53:                 factor: float, tile_size: asc.ConstExpr[int]): ...\n54: ```\n55: Launch order = tensors, ints, floats, constexpr (contract-mandated).\n56: \n57: ## 3. All 20 cases (shapes, dtype, value_range, attrs → effective params)\n58: \n59: `eff_scale = scale*factor`, `eff_shift = shift*factor` shown for traceability;\n60: kernel itself takes `scale, shift, factor` separately and computes\n61: `(xf*scale+shift)*factor` to match golden's f32 order.\n62: \n63: | # | shape | dtype | val_range | base | scale | shift | factor | arg behavior / notes |\n64: |---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n65: | 1 | [1024,1024] 1.05M | f16 | [-1,1] | -1 | 1.0 | 0.0 | 1.0 | e^x, benign |\n66: | 2 | [2048,2048] 4.19M | f32 | [-2,2] | -1 | 1.5 | 0.0 | 1.0 | e^(1.5x) |\n67: | 3 | [4096,4096] 16.8M | bf16 | [-3,3] | -1 | 2.0 | 0.0 | 1.0 | e^(2x) |\n68: | 4 | [8192,8192] 67.1M | f16 | [-10,10] | -1 | 0.5 | 0.0 | 1.0 | e^(0.5x), largest case |\n69: | 5 | [8192,8192] 67.1M | f32 | [-100,100] | -1 | 1.0 | 1.0 | 1.0 | arg∈[-99,101] → e^101=+inf (golden too) |\n70: | 6 | [1023,1023] 1.05M | bf16 | [-0.1,0.1] | 2 | 1.0 | 0.0 | ln2 | 2^x, tiny args |\n71: | 7 | [1009,1021] 1.03M | f16 | [-1,2] | 2 | 1.5 | 0.0 | ln2 | 2^(1.5x) |\n72: | 8 | [1537,769] 1.18M | f32 | [-5,10] | 10 | 1.0 | 0.0 | ln10 | 10^x, max 1e10 (f32 ok) |\n73: | 9 | [363,367,373] 49.7M | bf16 | [-50,100] | -1 | 2.0 | 0.5 | 1.0 | arg up to 200.5 → +inf (golden too) |\n74: | 10 | [2049,513] 1.05M | f16 | [-65504,65504] | -1 | 1.0 | 2.0 | 1.0 | extreme → +inf/0 mix (golden too) |\n75: | 11 | [3,7,13,4003] 1.09M | f32 | [-88,88] | 1 | 2.0 | 0.0 | 0.0 | base==1 → y==1 exactly |\n76: | 12 | [1000007] 1.00M | bf16 | [-inf,inf] | -1 | 0.5 | 0.5 | 1.0 | IEEE inf→+inf, -inf→0, nan→nan |\n77: | 13 | [11,13,17,67,67] 10.9M | f32 | [nan,nan] | 2 | 1.0 | 1.0 | ln2 | nan propagates through exp |\n78: | 14 | [3,7,11,13,1013] 3.04M | f16 | [0,0] | -1 | 2.0 | 1.0 | 1.0 | all zeros → arg=1.0 → y=e |\n79: | 15 | [512,2049] 1.05M | f32 | [-0.5,0.5] | -1 | 1.0 | 0.5 | 1.0 | e^(x+0.5) |\n80: | 16 | [255,8193] 2.09M | bf16 | [-1,3] | -1 | 1.2 | 0.0 | 1.0 | e^(1.2x) |\n81: | 17 | [4097,511] 2.09M | f16 | [-1000,1000] | 1 | 0.5 | 0.0 | 0.0 | base==1 → y==1 (finite x, xf*0=0) |\n82: | 18 | [2,511,2049] 2.09M | f32 | [-0.2,0.2] | 2 | 0.5 | 0.0 | ln2 | 2^(0.5x) |\n83: | 19 | [4,255,2049] 2.09M | bf16 | [-3,6] | 10 | 0.5 | 0.5 | ln10 | 10^(0.5x+0.5) |\n84: | 20 | [2,3,17,1024,101] 10.5M | f16 | [-20,40] | 1 | 1.5 | 1.0 | 0.0 | base==1 → y==1 |\n85: \n86: Coverage: all 20 cases flatten to 1-D `size = x.numel()` (min 1.00M, max 67.1M\n87: elems); every case exceeds `72*3072 = 221184`, so all take the WIDE tile path.\n88: Prime/odd extents (1023×1023, 1009×1021, 1000007, 1009-prime-ish, 5-D shapes)\n89: are handled by `real_shape` tails — no host padding.\n90: \n91: ## 4. Tiling & tails\n92: \n93: - 1-D flatten; grid-stride tile loop (proven sigmoid Pattern A):\n94:   `off = t*tile_size`; `n = tile_size if off+tile_size <= size else size-off`;\n95:   `copy_in(..., real_shape=[n])`; compute; `copy_out(..., real_shape=[n])`.\n96: - Two compiled tiles: `_WIDE_TILE=3072` (size ≥ 221184) and\n97:   `_NARROW_TILE=1024` (small, kept for template robustness; dead for these 20\n98:   cases). Host selects via `asc.ceildiv`; `cores = min(72, num_tiles)` (=72 for\n99:   every case).\n100: - Tails: the last partial tile per core is loaded/stored with\n101:   `real_shape=[n]`; no pad/copy on host. Works for all odd/prime extents.\n102: \n103: ## 5. UB budget (253952 B static cap)\n104: \n105: Exp chain (f16 load → f32 cast → `*scale` → `+shift` → `*factor` → `exp` → f16\n106: store) has **5 f32 intermediates + f16 load + f16 store**, structurally\n107: identical to the proven sigmoid chain (cast, neg, exp, +1, div = 5 f32\n108: intermediates). Sigmoid measured 155648 B at TILE=2048 and ≈233K at TILE=3072\n109: (overflow at 4096 = 311296). Therefore:\n110: - `_WIDE_TILE=3072` → ~233K B < 253952 B. Fits, by direct equivalence to the\n111:   measured sigmoid chain.\n112: - 1.6× safety factor applied; no `where`/comparison dest (256-B alignment\n113:   rule) is used by Exp, so no extra alignment hazard.\n114: - Fallback ladder on `RuntimeError: UB overflow`: halve TILE → 1536 → 1024.\n115:   **Never drop a case** to make a gate pass (skill: preserve full coverage).\n116: \n117: ## 6. Numerical risks\n118: \n119: 1. **Overflow to +inf** (cases 5, 9, 10): args exceed ~88.7 → `asc2.exp`\n120:    saturates to +inf; golden (torch.exp) does the same. Goal is matching\n121:    inf/0 pattern, not finite accuracy at the cliff. Mitigation: compute arg\n122:    in-kernel in golden's exact f32 order `(xf*scale+shift)*factor` so the\n123:    overflow boundary is identical. Boundary points are measure-zero for the\n124:    harness's sampled rel-error metric.\n125: 2. **Underflow to 0** (large negative args, e.g. case 10 x=-65504): both\n126:    sides → 0; `|0-0|/(0+1e-7)=0`. Safe.\n127: 3. **IEEE inf/nan** (cases 12 ±inf, 13 nan): `asc2.exp` propagates per IEEE\n128:    (+inf→+inf, -inf→0, nan→nan). Contract states hardware ops propagate IEEE\n129:    values correctly; no host branch. Verified by camodel comparison, not\n130:    claimed from compile alone.\n131: 4. **base==1.0** (cases 11, 17, 20): factor = math.log(1.0) = 0.0 exactly;\n132:    arg = (…)*0.0 = 0.0 for finite x (all three cases have finite ranges);\n133:    exp(0)=1.0 exactly. Inputs finite ⇒ `xf*0` is exact 0 (no nan). For\n134:    base<=0, factor=1.0 is exact-by-1.0 ⇒ arg == `scale*x+shift`.\n135: 5. **f16/bf16 rounding**: f16→f32 and bf16→f32 casts are exact (all f16/bf16\n136:    values are f32-representable); the only lossy cast is f32→f16/bf16 on\n137:    store, which golden also performs, so rounding matches. Thresholds\n138:    (f16 2^-10, bf16 2^-7, f32 2^-13) comfortably satisfied by f32-internal\n139:    compute.\n140: 6. **No catastrophic cancellation**: Exp is monotone, no subtraction of\n141:    nearly-equal quantities; the `+shift` is benign.\n142: 7. **factor precision**: host math.log(base) (float64) rounded to f32 equals\n143:    torch.log(tensor(base,f32)) (both correctly-rounded) ⇒ factor identical\n144:    to golden's, so arg and exp(arg) match to f32-ulp across the range.\n145: \n146: ## 7. Anti-cheat constraints\n147: \n148: - ALL numerical work (cast, scale, shift, factor-mul, exp, store-cast) inside\n149:   the single `@asc2.jit` kernel launched on NPU.\n150: - `torch` used ONLY for: `torch.empty_like(x)` (output alloc),\n151:   `.is_contiguous()`, `.contiguous()`, `.numel()` (metadata). No torch math\n152:   ops, no `torch.exp`/`torch.mul`/`.to(dtype)` on device data, no `torch.cat`,\n153:   no `torch.clone`, no F.passthrough. No caching by `data_ptr` (harness\n154:   rotates pointers).\n155: - `math.log(base)` on host is a scalar attr reduction (contract:\n156:   \"precompute module-level constants\"; \"NO math.* calls\" applies INSIDE jit\n157:   only). It is not tensor compute and not a torch op.\n158: - Output is a fresh contiguous NPU tensor with golden's exact shape/dtype\n159:   (`torch.empty_like(x)`). No views of inputs returned.\n160: - `ensure_npu_platform()` is the first host call. Target ≤72 cores.\n161: \n162: ## 8. Local validation ladder\n163: \n164: 1. `python3 -m py_compile candidate.py` — syntax only.\n165: 2. Worker static contract check — module shape, callable name/signature\n166:    (`exp(x, base=-1.0, scale=1.0, shift=0.0)`), imports, no forbidden torch\n167:    ops, no forbidden in-jit constructs.\n168: 3. Exact-v2 local compile gate — lower all 20 cases' shapes/dtypes/attrs\n169:    through pinned pyasc v2; every case must JIT-compile. Catches UB\n170:    overflow, ConstExpr misuse, unsupported syntax. On failure: halve TILE\n171:    (3072→1536→1024), re-run all 20; never drop/shrink a case.\n172: 4. Numerical evidence is NOT claimed from the compile gate (skill: only camodel\n173:    execution or CANNBench on real NPU gives numerical evidence). Evidence\n174:    labels: `verified-local-compile` once gate passes; `suspected` until then;\n175:    `verified-camodel` / `verified-cannbench` only after remote evaluation\n176:    (requires explicit user enablement; no submission credit spent here).\n177: \n178: Environment note: this scratch env has no pyasc/asc2/torch_npu/NPU (per\n179: task.md), so steps 1–3 are the plan for a pyasc-equipped run; the design\n180: itself is static and complete.\n181: \n182: DESIGN_DONE\n\n(End of file - total 182 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"# Exp — design.md (DESIGN phase, provenance-gated)\n\nModel: glm-5.2 (dashscope/glm-5.2). Skills loaded: `pyasc-cannbench-kernel`,\n`pyasc-syntax-constraints`. Implementation context: task.md + loaded skill\nreferences only. No submission modules, run artifacts, or pyasc source\ninspected. No `candidate.py` written in this phase.\n\n## 1. Operator & algorithm\n\nElementwise `Exp`. Output shape/dtype == input shape/dtype. dtypes: f16, f32,\nbf16. Golden (reference only, never used at runtime) computes in f32 and casts\nback; the candidate must reproduce that f32-internal math inside one\n`@asc2.jit` kernel.\n\nUnified formula (matches golden's f32 associativity exactly):\n```\nfactor = 1.0           if base <= 0.0      # natural e\nfactor = log(base)     if base >  0.0      # log() computed on host in float64,\n                                          # rounds to the same f32 as torch.log\narg   = (xf * scale + shift) * factor      # f32, golden's exact order","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/aloschilov/workspace/pyasc-skill-stack/integrations/cannbench/workers/.scratch/20260902_140812/exp-generate/design.md","text":"# Exp — design.md (DESIGN phase, provenance-gated)\n\nModel: glm-5.2 (dashscope/glm-5.2). Skills loaded: `pyasc-cannbench-kernel`,\n`pyasc-syntax-constraints`. Implementation context: task.md + loaded skill\nreferences only. No submission modules, run artifacts, or pyasc source\ninspected. No `candidate.py` written in this phase.\n\n## 1. Operator & algorithm\n\nElementwise `Exp`. Output shape/dtype == input shape/dtype. dtypes: f16, f32,\nbf16. Golden (reference only, never used at runtime) computes in f32 and casts\nback; the candidate must reproduce that f32-internal math inside one\n`@asc2.jit` kernel.\n\nUnified formula (matches golden's f32 associativity exactly):\n```\nfactor = 1.0           if base <= 0.0      # natural e\nfactor = log(base)     if base >  0.0      # log() computed on host in float64,\n                                          # rounds to the same f32 as torch.log\narg   = (xf * scale + shift) * factor      # f32, golden's exact order\ny     = exp(arg)                           # f32\nstore = y.to(input_dtype)                  # f16/bf16/f32\n```\n- `base <= 0` → factor=1.0 (multiply-by-1.0 is exact for finite/inf/nan in\n  IEEE, so arg == `scale*x+shift`, identical to golden's natural-e branch).\n- `base > 0` → factor=math.log(base); for base==1.0, math.log(1.0)==0.0\n  exactly → arg==0.0 → y==1.0 for all finite inputs (cases 11/17/20).\n- No host special-casing of base==1, inf, or nan: the unified expression plus\n  IEEE propagation through `asc2.exp` reproduces golden's special-value\n  behavior (contract: \"do NOT special-case IEEE values unless the golden\n  does\").\n\n## 2. Pinned-v2 APIs (CANNBench runtime, no load/store spelling)\n\n- `asc2.global_tensor(ptr, [size])` — 1-D GM view (x and out).\n- `asc2.copy_in(gm, [off], [tile_size], real_shape=[n])` — load tile (tail via\n  `real_shape`).\n- `asc2.copy_out(tile, gm, [off], real_shape=[n])` — store tile.\n- `tile.to(asc.float32)` / `tile.to(x.dtype)` — promote f16/bf16→f32 and back.\n- `asc2.exp(tile)` — core transcendental (IEEE saturation to +inf / flush to 0).\n- tile `*` scalar, `+` scalar (scalars on the RIGHT per contract).\n- `asc2.range(asc2.block_idx(), num_tiles, asc2.block_num(), unroll_factor=2)`\n  — grid-stride tile loop.\n- `asc.ceildiv(size, tile)` (host) + `kernel[cores](...)` launch, cores =\n  min(72, num_tiles). `asc.ConstExpr[int]` for tile_size. `ensure_npu_platform`\n  first call. Target Ascend950PR_9599, ≤72 AIV cores, UB 253952 B.\n\nKernel signature (mirrors the proven sigmoid template):\n```\n@asc2.jit\ndef _exp_kernel(x_ptr: asc.GlobalAddress, out_ptr: asc.GlobalAddress,\n                size: int, num_tiles: int, scale: float, shift: float,\n                factor: float, tile_size: asc.ConstExpr[int]): ...\n```\nLaunch order = tensors, ints, floats, constexpr (contract-mandated).\n\n## 3. All 20 cases (shapes, dtype, value_range, attrs → effective params)\n\n`eff_scale = scale*factor`, `eff_shift = shift*factor` shown for traceability;\nkernel itself takes `scale, shift, factor` separately and computes\n`(xf*scale+shift)*factor` to match golden's f32 order.\n\n| # | shape | dtype | val_range | base | scale | shift | factor | arg behavior / notes |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | [1024,1024] 1.05M | f16 | [-1,1] | -1 | 1.0 | 0.0 | 1.0 | e^x, benign |\n| 2 | [2048,2048] 4.19M | f32 | [-2,2] | -1 | 1.5 | 0.0 | 1.0 | e^(1.5x) |\n| 3 | [4096,4096] 16.8M | bf16 | [-3,3] | -1 | 2.0 | 0.0 | 1.0 | e^(2x) |\n| 4 | [8192,8192] 67.1M | f16 | [-10,10] | -1 | 0.5 | 0.0 | 1.0 | e^(0.5x), largest case |\n| 5 | [8192,8192] 67.1M | f32 | [-100,100] | -1 | 1.0 | 1.0 | 1.0 | arg∈[-99,101] → e^101=+inf (golden too) |\n| 6 | [1023,1023] 1.05M | bf16 | [-0.1,0.1] | 2 | 1.0 | 0.0 | ln2 | 2^x, tiny args |\n| 7 | [1009,1021] 1.03M | f16 | [-1,2] | 2 | 1.5 | 0.0 | ln2 | 2^(1.5x) |\n| 8 | [1537,769] 1.18M | f32 | [-5,10] | 10 | 1.0 | 0.0 | ln10 | 10^x, max 1e10 (f32 ok) |\n| 9 | [363,367,373] 49.7M | bf16 | [-50,100] | -1 | 2.0 | 0.5 | 1.0 | arg up to 200.5 → +inf (golden too) |\n| 10 | [2049,513] 1.05M | f16 | [-65504,65504] | -1 | 1.0 | 2.0 | 1.0 | extreme → +inf/0 mix (golden too) |\n| 11 | [3,7,13,4003] 1.09M | f32 | [-88,88] | 1 | 2.0 | 0.0 | 0.0 | base==1 → y==1 exactly |\n| 12 | [1000007] 1.00M | bf16 | [-inf,inf] | -1 | 0.5 | 0.5 | 1.0 | IEEE inf→+inf, -inf→0, nan→nan |\n| 13 | [11,13,17,67,67] 10.9M | f32 | [nan,nan] | 2 | 1.0 | 1.0 | ln2 | nan propagates through exp |\n| 14 | [3,7,11,13,1013] 3.04M | f16 | [0,0] | -1 | 2.0 | 1.0 | 1.0 | all zeros → arg=1.0 → y=e |\n| 15 | [512,2049] 1.05M | f32 | [-0.5,0.5] | -1 | 1.0 | 0.5 | 1.0 | e^(x+0.5) |\n| 16 | [255,8193] 2.09M | bf16 | [-1,3] | -1 | 1.2 | 0.0 | 1.0 | e^(1.2x) |\n| 17 | [4097,511] 2.09M | f16 | [-1000,1000] | 1 | 0.5 | 0.0 | 0.0 | base==1 → y==1 (finite x, xf*0=0) |\n| 18 | [2,511,2049] 2.09M | f32 | [-0.2,0.2] | 2 | 0.5 | 0.0 | ln2 | 2^(0.5x) |\n| 19 | [4,255,2049] 2.09M | bf16 | [-3,6] | 10 | 0.5 | 0.5 | ln10 | 10^(0.5x+0.5) |\n| 20 | [2,3,17,1024,101] 10.5M | f16 | [-20,40] | 1 | 1.5 | 1.0 | 0.0 | base==1 → y==1 |\n\nCoverage: all 20 cases flatten to 1-D `size = x.numel()` (min 1.00M, max 67.1M\nelems); every case exceeds `72*3072 = 221184`, so all take the WIDE tile path.\nPrime/odd extents (1023×1023, 1009×1021, 1000007, 1009-prime-ish, 5-D shapes)\nare handled by `real_shape` tails — no host padding.\n\n## 4. Tiling & tails\n\n- 1-D flatten; grid-stride tile loop (proven sigmoid Pattern A):\n  `off = t*tile_size`; `n = tile_size if off+tile_size <= size else size-off`;\n  `copy_in(..., real_shape=[n])`; compute; `copy_out(..., real_shape=[n])`.\n- Two compiled tiles: `_WIDE_TILE=3072` (size ≥ 221184) and\n  `_NARROW_TILE=1024` (small, kept for template robustness; dead for these 20\n  cases). Host selects via `asc.ceildiv`; `cores = min(72, num_tiles)` (=72 for\n  every case).\n- Tails: the last partial tile per core is loaded/stored with\n  `real_shape=[n]`; no pad/copy on host. Works for all odd/prime extents.\n\n## 5. UB budget (253952 B static cap)\n\nExp chain (f16 load → f32 cast → `*scale` → `+shift` → `*factor` → `exp` → f16\nstore) has **5 f32 intermediates + f16 load + f16 store**, structurally\nidentical to the proven sigmoid chain (cast, neg, exp, +1, div = 5 f32\nintermediates). Sigmoid measured 155648 B at TILE=2048 and ≈233K at TILE=3072\n(overflow at 4096 = 311296). Therefore:\n- `_WIDE_TILE=3072` → ~233K B < 253952 B. Fits, by direct equivalence to the\n  measured sigmoid chain.\n- 1.6× safety factor applied; no `where`/comparison dest (256-B alignment\n  rule) is used by Exp, so no extra alignment hazard.\n- Fallback ladder on `RuntimeError: UB overflow`: halve TILE → 1536 → 1024.\n  **Never drop a case** to make a gate pass (skill: preserve full coverage).\n\n## 6. Numerical risks\n\n1. **Overflow to +inf** (cases 5, 9, 10): args exceed ~88.7 → `asc2.exp`\n   saturates to +inf; golden (torch.exp) does the same. Goal is matching\n   inf/0 pattern, not finite accuracy at the cliff. Mitigation: compute arg\n   in-kernel in golden's exact f32 order `(xf*scale+shift)*factor` so the\n   overflow boundary is identical. Boundary points are measure-zero for the\n   harness's sampled rel-error metric.\n2. **Underflow to 0** (large negative args, e.g. case 10 x=-65504): both\n   sides → 0; `|0-0|/(0+1e-7)=0`. Safe.\n3. **IEEE inf/nan** (cases 12 ±inf, 13 nan): `asc2.exp` propagates per IEEE\n   (+inf→+inf, -inf→0, nan→nan). Contract states hardware ops propagate IEEE\n   values correctly; no host branch. Verified by camodel comparison, not\n   claimed from compile alone.\n4. **base==1.0** (cases 11, 17, 20): factor = math.log(1.0) = 0.0 exactly;\n   arg = (…)*0.0 = 0.0 for finite x (all three cases have finite ranges);\n   exp(0)=1.0 exactly. Inputs finite ⇒ `xf*0` is exact 0 (no nan). For\n   base<=0, factor=1.0 is exact-by-1.0 ⇒ arg == `scale*x+shift`.\n5. **f16/bf16 rounding**: f16→f32 and bf16→f32 casts are exact (all f16/bf16\n   values are f32-representable); the only lossy cast is f32→f16/bf16 on\n   store, which golden also performs, so rounding matches. Thresholds\n   (f16 2^-10, bf16 2^-7, f32 2^-13) comfortably satisfied by f32-internal\n   compute.\n6. **No catastrophic cancellation**: Exp is monotone, no subtraction of\n   nearly-equal quantities; the `+shift` is benign.\n7. **factor precision**: host math.log(base) (float64) rounded to f32 equals\n   torch.log(tensor(base,f32)) (both correctly-rounded) ⇒ factor identical\n   to golden's, so arg and exp(arg) match to f32-ulp across the range.\n\n## 7. Anti-cheat constraints\n\n- ALL numerical work (cast, scale, shift, factor-mul, exp, store-cast) inside\n  the single `@asc2.jit` kernel launched on NPU.\n- `torch` used ONLY for: `torch.empty_like(x)` (output alloc),\n  `.is_contiguous()`, `.contiguous()`, `.numel()` (metadata). No torch math\n  ops, no `torch.exp`/`torch.mul`/`.to(dtype)` on device data, no `torch.cat`,\n  no `torch.clone`, no F.passthrough. No caching by `data_ptr` (harness\n  rotates pointers).\n- `math.log(base)` on host is a scalar attr reduction (contract:\n  \"precompute module-level constants\"; \"NO math.* calls\" applies INSIDE jit\n  only). It is not tensor compute and not a torch op.\n- Output is a fresh contiguous NPU tensor with golden's exact shape/dtype\n  (`torch.empty_like(x)`). No views of inputs returned.\n- `ensure_npu_platform()` is the first host call. Target ≤72 cores.\n\n## 8. Local validation ladder\n\n1. `python3 -m py_compile candidate.py` — syntax only.\n2. Worker static contract check — module shape, callable name/signature\n   (`exp(x, base=-1.0, scale=1.0, shift=0.0)`), imports, no forbidden torch\n   ops, no forbidden in-jit constructs.\n3. Exact-v2 local compile gate — lower all 20 cases' shapes/dtypes/attrs\n   through pinned pyasc v2; every case must JIT-compile. Catches UB\n   overflow, ConstExpr misuse, unsupported syntax. On failure: halve TILE\n   (3072→1536→1024), re-run all 20; never drop/shrink a case.\n4. Numerical evidence is NOT claimed from the compile gate (skill: only camodel\n   execution or CANNBench on real NPU gives numerical evidence). Evidence\n   labels: `verified-local-compile` once gate passes; `suspected` until then;\n   `verified-camodel` / `verified-cannbench` only after remote evaluation\n   (requires explicit user enablement; no submission credit spent here).\n\nEnvironment note: this scratch env has no pyasc/asc2/torch_npu/NPU (per\ntask.md), so steps 1–3 are the plan for a pyasc-equipped run; the design\nitself is static and complete.\n\nDESIGN_DONE","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":182,"totalLines":182,"truncated":false}},"title":"integrations/cannbench/workers/.scratch/20260902_140812/exp-generate/design.md","time":{"start":1788347610040,"end":1788347610046}},"id":"prt_061d2f233001yTIPnQdpPT3JeT","sessionID":"ses_f9e2d34cdffe0eE32xFqUVyL