{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1788347622116,"sessionID":"ses_f9e2d34cdffe0eE32xFqUVyL7D","part":{"id":"prt_061d322e1001V5h0lNqyehUQGK","messageID":"msg_061d317e1001RKo5dgjmuOZB0d","sessionID":"ses_f9e2d34cdffe0eE32xFqUVyL7D","snapshot":"","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788347624729,"sessionID":"ses_f9e2d34cdffe0eE32xFqUVyL7D","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_48a9b482446b4c3b96a5f36e","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/aloschilov/workspace/pyasc-skill-stack/skills/pyasc-syntax-constraints/SKILL.md"},"output":"<path>/home/aloschilov/workspace/pyasc-skill-stack/skills/pyasc-syntax-constraints/SKILL.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: ---\n2: name: pyasc-syntax-constraints\n3: description: pyasc Python syntax support and restrictions reference. Documents supported and unsupported Python syntax within @asc2.jit functions, type constraints, and ConstExpr usage. Trigger — when writing kernel code, checking if a Python construct is supported, or debugging JIT compilation errors related to syntax.\n4: ---\n5: \n6: # pyasc Syntax Constraints\n7: \n8: Syntax and keyword support varies across v2 snapshots. For CANNBench, the\n9: source of truth is `integrations/cannbench/pyasc-v2-source` at the commit in\n10: `SOURCE_COMMIT`; load `pyasc-cannbench-kernel` and verify uncertain constructs\n11: against that source plus the exact-v2 compile gate. In particular, its\n12: `asc2.range` supports `unroll_factor` and `gm_barrier`, but not `parallel`.\n13: \n14: ## Overview\n15: \n16: pyasc compiles Python code to Ascend C via JIT. Only a subset of Python syntax is supported inside `@asc2.jit`-decorated functions. This skill documents what is and is not allowed.\n17: \n18: ## Supported Syntax\n19: \n20: | Syntax | Example | Notes |\n21: |--------|---------|-------|\n22: | Attribute access | `nums.__len__()` | |\n23: | `while` loop | `while i < n: ...` | |\n24: | `for` loop | `for i in asc2.range(n): ...` | Use `asc2.range()` inside kernel, not `range()` |\n25: | Binary operators | `a + b * c` | `+`, `-`, `*`, `/`, `%`, `//`, `**`, `&`, `\\|`, `^`, `<<`, `>>` |\n26: | Unary operators | `~a`, `-a`, `not a` | |\n27: | `tuple` | `return x, y, z` | |\n28: | `list` | `nums = [a, b, c]` | |\n29: | `pass` | `pass` | |\n30: | Ternary `if` | `ans = x if x > 0 else 0` | |\n31: | `if`/`elif`/`else` | Full conditional blocks | |\n32: | Constants | `a = 1; b = True` | |\n33: | Comparisons | `>`, `<`, `>=`, `<=`, `==`, `!=` | |\n34: | Function calls | `func()` | Must be `@asc2.jit`-decorated or asc2 built-in |\n35: | Boolean logic | `and`, `or` | |\n36: | Augmented assign | `+=`, `-=`, `*=`, `/=`, `%=` | |\n37: | Assignment | `x = expr` | |\n38: | Annotated assign | `count: int = 3` | |\n39: | f-strings + assert | `assert cond, f\"msg {val}\"` | |\n40: | Subscript | `nums[0]` | |\n41: | Slice | `tensor[offset:]` | |\n42: \n43: ## Unsupported Syntax\n44: \n45: | Syntax | Status | Workaround |\n46: |--------|--------|------------|\n47: | Nested functions | Not supported | Define separate `@asc2.jit` functions |\n48: | `global` | Not supported | Pass values as parameters |\n49: | `return` from kernel | Not supported | Kernels write to output tensors; device functions may return |\n50: | `return` inside `if`/`for` | Not supported | Use top-level return only in device functions |\n51: | `continue` | Not supported | Restructure loop logic |\n52: | `print()` | Not supported | Use `assert` with f-strings |\n53: | `with open(...)` | Not supported | Do file I/O outside JIT |\n54: | `raise` | Not supported | Use `assert` instead |\n55: | `try`/`except` | Not supported | Handle errors outside JIT |\n56: | `yield` / `yield from` | Not supported | No generators |\n57: | `lambda` | Not supported | Define named functions |\n58: | `break` | Not supported | Restructure loop logic |\n59: | `import` / `from...import` | Not supported | All imports outside JIT |\n60: | `async def` / `async with` | Not supported | No async |\n61: | `nonlocal` | Not supported | Pass values as parameters |\n62: | Class methods as kernels | Not supported | Use module-level functions |\n63: | If-scoped variables | Restricted | Initialize before `if`; use `asc.ConstExpr` for compile-time branching |\n64: | Unsigned int comparison | Restricted | Use `np.int64` or truthiness check instead |\n65: \n66: ## Type Constraints\n67: \n68: ### Kernel parameter types (runtime)\n69: \n70: | Supported | Not supported |\n71: |-----------|---------------|\n72: | `bool`, `int`, `float` | `str` |\n73: | `numpy` scalars and `ndarray` | `tuple`, `list`, `dict` |\n74: | `torch.Tensor` (NPU mode only) | Complex objects |\n75: | `asc.GlobalAddress` | |\n76: \n77: ### Compile-time parameters\n78: \n79: Use `asc.ConstExpr[T]` for values that must be resolved at compile time:\n80: ```python\n81: @asc2.jit(always_compile=True)\n82: def kernel(x_ptr: asc.GlobalAddress, tile_size: asc.ConstExpr[int]):\n83:     # tile_size is resolved at compile time and included in the JIT cache key\n84:     ...\n85: ```\n86: \n87: ### Built-in whitelist inside JIT\n88: \n89: Only these built-ins are available: `dict`, `float`, `int`, `isinstance`, `issubclass`, `len`, `list`, `range`, `repr`, `str`, `tuple`, `type`\n90: \n91: Note: For iteration inside `@asc2.jit`, use `asc2.range()` instead of `range()`.\n92: Do not assume optional keywords are portable between pyasc snapshots.\n93: \n94: ### Cube (matmul) loop + placement notes\n95: \n96: - **M/N/K tile loops are compile-time.** When the trip count is an\n97:   `asc.ConstExpr[int]` (matmul m/n/k tiles), a plain Python `for i in range(...)`\n98:   is fully unrolled at JIT time — that is the intended form for the loop that must\n99:   keep an operand resident in `L0A`/`L0B`. Use `asc2.range(...,\n100:   unroll_factor=2)` only on the *inner* tile loop you want software-pipelined\n101:   (double-buffered).\n102: - **Overlap (`gm_barrier=False`) doubles the pipelined L0 buffer.** The 2-deep\n103:   tile must fit half the L0 capacity (≤ 32 KiB for L0A/L0B, ≤ 64 KiB for the f32\n104:   L0C). A full-K `[256,128]` f16 L0B tile is 64 KiB and overflows when doubled\n105:   (`L0B overflow: 65536 bytes available, 131072 used`); shrink the tile (e.g.\n106:   `N_TILE=64`) before relying on overlap.\n107: - **Cube tile placement is explicit:** load/`asc2.copy` operands to\n108:   `asc2.TileLocation.L0A` (left) / `L0B` (right); stage from GM via\n109:   `asc2.TileLocation.L1` for reuse; the `@` result lands in L0C as f32 — cast with\n110:   `.to(asc2.float16)` before `asc2.store` if a 16-bit output is wanted.\n111: \n112: ## Kernel vs Device Function Rules\n113: \n114: | Rule | Kernel function | Device function |\n115: |------|----------------|-----------------|\n116: | Launched from | Host via `kernel[core_num](...)` | Other `@asc2.jit` functions |\n117: | `return` value | Not allowed | Allowed |\n118: | `@asc2.jit(...)` options | Effective (always_compile, etc.) | Ignored |\n119: | Nesting | Cannot be nested | Cannot be nested |\n120: \n121: ## References\n122: \n123: - [Supported Syntax Details](references/supported-syntax.md)\n124: - [Unsupported Syntax Details](references/unsupported-syntax.md)\n125: - [Type Constraints](references/type-constraints.md)\n126: - [ConstExpr Guide](references/constexpr-guide.md)\n\n(End of file - total 126 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"---\nname: pyasc-syntax-constraints\ndescription: pyasc Python syntax support and restrictions reference. Documents supported and unsupported Python syntax within @asc2.jit functions, type constraints, and ConstExpr usage. Trigger — when writing kernel code, checking if a Python construct is supported, or debugging JIT compilation errors related to syntax.\n---\n\n# pyasc Syntax Constraints\n\nSyntax and keyword support varies across v2 snapshots. For CANNBench, the\nsource of truth is `integrations/cannbench/pyasc-v2-source` at the commit in\n`SOURCE_COMMIT`; load `pyasc-cannbench-kernel` and verify uncertain constructs\nagainst that source plus the exact-v2 compile gate. In particular, its\n`asc2.range` supports `unroll_factor` and `gm_barrier`, but not `parallel`.\n\n## Overview\n\npyasc compiles Python code to Ascend C via JIT. Only a subset of Python syntax is supported inside `@asc2.jit`-decorated functions. This skill documents what is and is not allowed.\n\n## Supported Syntax\n\n| Syntax | Example | Notes |","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/aloschilov/workspace/pyasc-skill-stack/skills/pyasc-syntax-constraints/SKILL.md","text":"---\nname: pyasc-syntax-constraints\ndescription: pyasc Python syntax support and restrictions reference. Documents supported and unsupported Python syntax within @asc2.jit functions, type constraints, and ConstExpr usage. Trigger — when writing kernel code, checking if a Python construct is supported, or debugging JIT compilation errors related to syntax.\n---\n\n# pyasc Syntax Constraints\n\nSyntax and keyword support varies across v2 snapshots. For CANNBench, the\nsource of truth is `integrations/cannbench/pyasc-v2-source` at the commit in\n`SOURCE_COMMIT`; load `pyasc-cannbench-kernel` and verify uncertain constructs\nagainst that source plus the exact-v2 compile gate. In particular, its\n`asc2.range` supports `unroll_factor` and `gm_barrier`, but not `parallel`.\n\n## Overview\n\npyasc compiles Python code to Ascend C via JIT. Only a subset of Python syntax is supported inside `@asc2.jit`-decorated functions. This skill documents what is and is not allowed.\n\n## Supported Syntax\n\n| Syntax | Example | Notes |\n|--------|---------|-------|\n| Attribute access | `nums.__len__()` | |\n| `while` loop | `while i < n: ...` | |\n| `for` loop | `for i in asc2.range(n): ...` | Use `asc2.range()` inside kernel, not `range()` |\n| Binary operators | `a + b * c` | `+`, `-`, `*`, `/`, `%`, `//`, `**`, `&`, `\\|`, `^`, `<<`, `>>` |\n| Unary operators | `~a`, `-a`, `not a` | |\n| `tuple` | `return x, y, z` | |\n| `list` | `nums = [a, b, c]` | |\n| `pass` | `pass` | |\n| Ternary `if` | `ans = x if x > 0 else 0` | |\n| `if`/`elif`/`else` | Full conditional blocks | |\n| Constants | `a = 1; b = True` | |\n| Comparisons | `>`, `<`, `>=`, `<=`, `==`, `!=` | |\n| Function calls | `func()` | Must be `@asc2.jit`-decorated or asc2 built-in |\n| Boolean logic | `and`, `or` | |\n| Augmented assign | `+=`, `-=`, `*=`, `/=`, `%=` | |\n| Assignment | `x = expr` | |\n| Annotated assign | `count: int = 3` | |\n| f-strings + assert | `assert cond, f\"msg {val}\"` | |\n| Subscript | `nums[0]` | |\n| Slice | `tensor[offset:]` | |\n\n## Unsupported Syntax\n\n| Syntax | Status | Workaround |\n|--------|--------|------------|\n| Nested functions | Not supported | Define separate `@asc2.jit` functions |\n| `global` | Not supported | Pass values as parameters |\n| `return` from kernel | Not supported | Kernels write to output tensors; device functions may return |\n| `return` inside `if`/`for` | Not supported | Use top-level return only in device functions |\n| `continue` | Not supported | Restructure loop logic |\n| `print()` | Not supported | Use `assert` with f-strings |\n| `with open(...)` | Not supported | Do file I/O outside JIT |\n| `raise` | Not supported | Use `assert` instead |\n| `try`/`except` | Not supported | Handle errors outside JIT |\n| `yield` / `yield from` | Not supported | No generators |\n| `lambda` | Not supported | Define named functions |\n| `break` | Not supported | Restructure loop logic |\n| `import` / `from...import` | Not supported | All imports outside JIT |\n| `async def` / `async with` | Not supported | No async |\n| `nonlocal` | Not supported | Pass values as parameters |\n| Class methods as kernels | Not supported | Use module-level functions |\n| If-scoped variables | Restricted | Initialize before `if`; use `asc.ConstExpr` for compile-time branching |\n| Unsigned int comparison | Restricted | Use `np.int64` or truthiness check instead |\n\n## Type Constraints\n\n### Kernel parameter types (runtime)\n\n| Supported | Not supported |\n|-----------|---------------|\n| `bool`, `int`, `float` | `str` |\n| `numpy` scalars and `ndarray` | `tuple`, `list`, `dict` |\n| `torch.Tensor` (NPU mode only) | Complex objects |\n| `asc.GlobalAddress` | |\n\n### Compile-time parameters\n\nUse `asc.ConstExpr[T]` for values that must be resolved at compile time:\n```python\n@asc2.jit(always_compile=True)\ndef kernel(x_ptr: asc.GlobalAddress, tile_size: asc.ConstExpr[int]):\n    # tile_size is resolved at compile time and included in the JIT cache key\n    ...\n```\n\n### Built-in whitelist inside JIT\n\nOnly these built-ins are available: `dict`, `float`, `int`, `isinstance`, `issubclass`, `len`, `list`, `range`, `repr`, `str`, `tuple`, `type`\n\nNote: For iteration inside `@asc2.jit`, use `asc2.range()` instead of `range()`.\nDo not assume optional keywords are portable between pyasc snapshots.\n\n### Cube (matmul) loop + placement notes\n\n- **M/N/K tile loops are compile-time.** When the trip count is an\n  `asc.ConstExpr[int]` (matmul m/n/k tiles), a plain Python `for i in range(...)`\n  is fully unrolled at JIT time — that is the intended form for the loop that must\n  keep an operand resident in `L0A`/`L0B`. Use `asc2.range(...,\n  unroll_factor=2)` only on the *inner* tile loop you want software-pipelined\n  (double-buffered).\n- **Overlap (`gm_barrier=False`) doubles the pipelined L0 buffer.** The 2-deep\n  tile must fit half the L0 capacity (≤ 32 KiB for L0A/L0B, ≤ 64 KiB for the f32\n  L0C). A full-K `[256,128]` f16 L0B tile is 64 KiB and overflows when doubled\n  (`L0B overflow: 65536 bytes available, 131072 used`); shrink the tile (e.g.\n  `N_TILE=64`) before relying on overlap.\n- **Cube tile placement is explicit:** load/`asc2.copy` operands to\n  `asc2.TileLocation.L0A` (left) / `L0B` (right); stage from GM via\n  `asc2.TileLocation.L1` for reuse; the `@` result lands in L0C as f32 — cast with\n  `.to(asc2.float16)` before `asc2.store` if a 16-bit output is wanted.\n\n## Kernel vs Device Function Rules\n\n| Rule | Kernel function | Device function |\n|------|----------------|-----------------|\n| Launched from | Host via `kernel[core_num](...)` | Other `@asc2.jit` functions |\n| `return` value | Not allowed | Allowed |\n| `@asc2.jit(...)` options | Effective (always_compile, etc.) | Ignored |\n| Nesting | Cannot be nested | Cannot be nested |\n\n## References\n\n- [Supported Syntax Details](references/supported-syntax.md)\n- [Unsupported Syntax Details](references/unsupported-syntax.md)\n- [Type Constraints](references/type-constraints.md)\n- [ConstExpr Guide](references/constexpr-guide.md)","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":126,"totalLines":126,"truncated":false}},"title":"skills/pyasc-syntax-constraints/SKILL.md","time":{"start":1788347624721,"end":1788347624727}},"id":"prt_061d32ba7001rBMgY07hmI7EIm","sessionID":"ses_f9e2d34cdffe0eE32xFqUVyL7D","messageID":"msg_061d317e1001RKo5dgjmuOZB0d"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788347625088,"sessionID":"ses_f9e2d34cdffe0eE32xFqUVyL7D","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_b58fc288dfa845b795f5403f","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/aloschilov/workspace/pyasc-skill-stack/skills/pyasc-api-patterns/SKILL.md"},"output":"<path>/home/aloschilov/workspace/pyasc-skill-stack/skills/pyasc-api-patterns/SKILL.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: ---\n2: name: pyasc-api-patterns\n3: description: pyasc asc2 API usage patterns and best practices. Provides correct usage for tensor operations, tiling, memory access, JIT options, and type system. Trigger — when calling pyasc asc2 APIs, encountering parameter errors, or needing API usage guidance.\n4: ---\n5: \n6: # pyasc asc2 API Best Practices\n7: \n8: > **Select the runtime profile before copying an API pattern.** The generic\n9: > examples below describe the stack's simulator/golden profile and include\n10: > APIs from more than one pyasc v2 snapshot. For CANNBench, load\n11: > `pyasc-cannbench-kernel` instead: the submitted runtime is pinned to\n12: > `compiler-team/pyasc:v2@ac1222a48c8914d3f81297c7570d1a84f0f26778` and its\n13: > memory API is `global_tensor` / `copy_in` / `copy_out`, with `real_shape`\n14: > for tails. Do not translate a CANNBench module from the older\n15: > `tensor` / `load` / `store` examples in this file.\n16: \n17: This file is intentionally a broad catalog. Load only the directly relevant\n18: reference section after the profile is selected; loading the entire catalog\n19: can exhaust a worker's context before it writes code.\n20: \n21: ## API Category Index\n22: \n23: | API Category | Key APIs | Typical Scenarios |\n24: |-------------|----------|-------------------|\n25: | **Memory** | `asc2.tensor`, `asc2.load`, `asc2.store` | Global memory access, tile load/store |\n26: | **Computation** | `x + y`, `asc2.abs(x)`, `asc2.exp(x)`, `asc2.where()` | Element-wise and reduction ops |\n27: | **Control flow** | `asc2.range(n)` | Tile loops with optional unrolling |\n28: | **Programming model** | `asc2.block_idx()`, `asc2.block_num()` | Multi-core work distribution |\n29: | **JIT** | `@asc2.jit(always_compile=True)` | Compilation control |\n30: | **Kernel params** | `asc.GlobalAddress`, `asc.ConstExpr[int]` | Kernel function signatures |\n31: | **Tiling math** | `asc.ceildiv(a, b)` | Compute tiles per block |\n32: \n33: ## Core Types\n34: \n35: ### Kernel parameter types\n36: \n37: | Type | Purpose | Example |\n38: |------|---------|---------|\n39: | `asc.GlobalAddress` | Global memory pointer for kernel args | `def kernel(x_ptr: asc.GlobalAddress, ...)` |\n40: | `asc.ConstExpr[int]` | Compile-time integer constant (included in JIT cache key) | `tile_size: asc.ConstExpr[int]` |\n41: | `int` | Runtime integer | `size: int` |\n42: \n43: ### asc2 tensor and memory types\n44: \n45: | Type / Function | Purpose | Example |\n46: |-----------------|---------|---------|\n47: | `asc2.tensor(ptr, [shape])` | Wrap a global memory pointer as a tensor | `x_gm = asc2.tensor(x_ptr, [size])` |\n48: | `asc2.load(gm, [tile_shape], offsets=[...])` | Load a tile from global memory | `x = asc2.load(x_gm, [tile_size], offsets=[offset])` |\n49: | `asc2.store(tile, gm, offsets=[...])` | Store a tile to global memory | `asc2.store(out, out_gm, offsets=[offset])` |\n50: \n51: ### Configuration types\n52: \n53: | Type | Purpose | Example |\n54: |------|---------|---------|\n55: | `asc.runtime.config.Backend` | Execution backend | `Backend.NPU`, `Backend.Model` |\n56: | `asc.runtime.config.Platform` | Target platform | `Platform.Ascend950PR_9599` |\n57: \n58: ## Common Patterns\n59: \n60: ### Kernel function pattern (asc2)\n61: \n62: ```python\n63: import asc\n64: import asc2\n65: \n66: @asc2.jit(always_compile=True)\n67: def my_kernel(x_ptr: asc.GlobalAddress, out_ptr: asc.GlobalAddress,\n68:               size: int, tile_size: asc.ConstExpr[int], tile_per_block: asc.ConstExpr[int]):\n69:     x_gm = asc2.tensor(x_ptr, [size])\n70:     out_gm = asc2.tensor(out_ptr, [size])\n71:     base_offset = asc2.block_idx() * tile_size * tile_per_block\n72:     for i in asc2.range(tile_per_block, unroll_factor=2):\n73:         tile_offset = base_offset + i * tile_size\n74:         x = asc2.load(x_gm, [tile_size], offsets=[tile_offset])\n75:         out = asc2.abs(x)  # your operation here\n76:         asc2.store(out, out_gm, offsets=[tile_offset])\n77: ```\n78: \n79: ### Launch pattern (asc2)\n80: \n81: ```python\n82: TILE_SIZE = 128\n83: CORE_NUM = 16\n84: \n85: num_tiles = asc.ceildiv(size, TILE_SIZE)\n86: my_kernel[CORE_NUM](x, out, size, TILE_SIZE, asc.ceildiv(num_tiles, CORE_NUM))\n87: ```\n88: \n89: Note: asc2 launch uses `kernel[core_num](...)` — no stream argument needed.\n90: \n91: ### Tiling with ceildiv\n92: \n93: asc2 handles tail/non-divisible tile sizes automatically. The tiling pattern is:\n94: \n95: ```python\n96: TILE_SIZE = 128   # fixed tile size (elements per tile)\n97: CORE_NUM = 16     # number of compute cores\n98: \n99: size = data.size\n100: num_tiles = asc.ceildiv(size, TILE_SIZE)\n101: tile_per_block = asc.ceildiv(num_tiles, CORE_NUM)\n102: ```\n103: \n104: Inside the kernel:\n105: ```python\n106: base_offset = asc2.block_idx() * tile_size * tile_per_block\n107: for i in asc2.range(tile_per_block, unroll_factor=2):\n108:     tile_offset = base_offset + i * tile_size\n109:     x = asc2.load(x_gm, [tile_size], offsets=[tile_offset])\n110:     # ... compute ...\n111:     asc2.store(out, out_gm, offsets=[tile_offset])\n112: ```\n113: \n114: **Why `ConstExpr`?** `tile_size` and `tile_per_block` are passed as `asc.ConstExpr[int]` so the JIT compiler can optimize tile-level code and include these values in the cache key.\n115: \n116: ### Tile sizing for vector-only ops (perf-aware)\n117: \n118: `TILE_SIZE = 128` is a safe **correctness** default, but it is often a poor\n119: **performance** default for elementwise/reduction kernels: a 128-element tile\n120: leaves the AIV vector pipeline mostly idle behind per-tile MTE setup and loop\n121: overhead. For perf-sensitive vector ops, use a **wide tile** that keeps the\n122: vector units busy and amortises per-tile setup, mirroring the ops-math arch35\n123: elementwise tile policy:\n124: \n125: ```python\n126: # perf-aware (oracle_guided) — wide tile for AIV utilisation\n127: TILE_SIZE = 2048   # vs 128 correctness default\n128: CORE_NUM  = 16\n129: # size must be a multiple of TILE_SIZE * CORE_NUM (aligned_only)\n130: ```\n131: \n132: Evidence (camodel `Ascend950PR_9599`, abs/float16, `[32,4096]`, vs the\n133: hand-written AscendC `aclnnAbs`): `TILE_SIZE=128` → ratio ≈ 0.20;\n134: `TILE_SIZE=2048` → ratio ≈ 0.93 (within 7% of hand-written). The op is\n135: unchanged — only the tile width moves the number. See\n136: [`docs/perf-vs-ascendc-demo.md`](../../docs/perf-vs-ascendc-demo.md) and\n137: [`docs/perf-methodology/ticks-calculation.md` §8](../../docs/perf-methodology/ticks-calculation.md).\n138: \n139: **Multi-input elementwise: also widen `CORE_NUM`.** A wide tile alone is not\n140: enough when the op loads two or more input streams (e.g. `add`, `mul`,\n141: `x*y+z`). The extra MTE2 load stream is *not* overlapped today — `asc2.range`\n142: leaves `gm_barrier=False` (overlap enabled) but the software-pipelining pass\n143: that would overlap loads with compute is not yet wired up — so the second load\n144: runs serially per core. Spread the launch across **all 32 AIV cores** (not 16) so\n145: each core does half the tiles and half the serial load work:\n146: \n147: ```python\n148: # perf-aware, multi-input elementwise (add/mul/fma): use all 32 AIV cores\n149: TILE_SIZE = 2048\n150: CORE_NUM  = 32     # vs 16 for single-input; matches the reference's ~32-block split\n151: # size must be a multiple of TILE_SIZE * CORE_NUM (aligned_only)\n152: ```\n153: \n154: Evidence (camodel `Ascend950PR_9599`, add/float16, `[32,4096]`, vs hand-written\n155: `aclnnAdd`): at `CORE_NUM=16` the kernel measured `gen_ticks=6304` (ratio 0.68,\n156: **FAIL**); at `CORE_NUM=32` it drops to `gen_ticks=3623` (ratio **1.18**, PASS) —\n157: same op, only the core count moved. Single-input ops like `abs` already clear the\n158: gate at 16 cores, so this is specifically the multi-load lever.\n159: \n160: Reference tile policy (hand-written AscendC, arch35), in the remote `ops-math`\n161: repo (`https://gitcode.com/cann/ops-math`, branch `master`):\n162: [`math/abs/op_host/arch35/abs_tiling_arch35.cpp`](https://gitcode.com/cann/ops-math/blob/master/math/abs/op_host/arch35/abs_tiling_arch35.cpp)\n163: and [`math/add/op_host/arch35/add_tiling_arch35.cpp`](https://gitcode.com/cann/ops-math/blob/master/math/add/op_host/arch35/add_tiling_arch35.cpp)\n164: (local mirror when cloned: `$CANN_OPS_ROOT/ops-math/math/{abs,add}/op_host/arch35/`).\n165: \n166: > Keep the **rank-1 flatten** invariant when widening the tile: declare the GM\n167: > tensor as `asc2.tensor(x_ptr, [size])` and load `[tile_size]` with a 1D\n168: > `offsets=[tile_offset]`. Widening `TILE_SIZE` does not change the rank rules.\n169: \n170: > **Reductions have their own tile-selection rules.** A last-axis reduction is\n171: > tiled as a 2-D `[tile_rows, tile_cols]` block, not a 1-D width. Three levers\n172: > decide the ratio: use every AI core (spread rows across the full core grid),\n173: > keep the reduce axis contiguous (`tile_cols = C`, do not pad/align it), and size\n174: > `tile_rows` to the per-core row block then the ~192 KB physical UB. See\n175: > [Reduction tiling selection](references/reduction-tiling.md).\n176: \n177: > **CRITICAL**: Any value used in the **shape** argument of `asc2.load` or `asc2.tensor`\n178: > MUST be either a literal integer, a `ConstExpr[int]` parameter, or a compile-time\n179: > expression. Using a plain `int` parameter in load shape (e.g., `asc2.load(gm, [cols])` where\n180: > `cols: int`) will cause `RuntimeError: All values in 'shape' must be integers` at JIT time.\n181: > Always declare such parameters as `asc.ConstExpr[int]`.\n182: \n183: ### Recommended `asc2.range` parameters (PR 190 defaults)\n184: \n185: Two kwargs of `asc2.range` (signature in\n186: [docker/pyasc-overlay/asc_language_tile/range.py](../../docker/pyasc-overlay/asc_language_tile/range.py))\n187: materially change codegen quality:\n188: \n189: - `unroll_factor: int = 1` — loop unroll attribute placed on the emitted\n190:   `ForOp`. Higher values give the compiler more scheduling freedom.\n191: - `parallel: bool = False` — flags the loop as having no carried\n192:   dependencies, allowing iteration-level reordering / vectorisation.\n193: \n194: The compiler-team [PR 190](https://gitcode.com/compiler-team/pyasc/pull/190)\n195: upgrades these from \"advanced tuning knob\" to \"expected default\": every\n196: `asc2.range` should set `unroll_factor=2`; leave `gm_barrier=False` (the default,\n197: overlap enabled) whenever the loop has no read-after-write through a value\n198: defined outside the loop. Set `gm_barrier=True` (insert a barrier, overlap off)\n199: only when an iteration reads a value a previous iteration wrote.\n200: \n201: **Decision rule:**\n202: \n203: ```mermaid\n204: flowchart TD\n205:     Start[\"asc2.range(...) loop body\"] --> Carried{\"Reads any tile, scalar, or accumulator that a PREVIOUS iteration WROTE?\"}\n206:     Carried -- \"No (independent tiles, disjoint stores)\" --> Par[\"unroll_factor=2\"]\n207:     Carried -- \"Yes (sum/max accumulator, prefix scan, in-place mutation)\" --> NoPar[\"unroll_factor=2, gm_barrier=True\"]\n208: ```\n209: \n210: **Pattern table** (which form to ship for each loop kind in the proven\n211: golden patterns):\n212: \n213: | Loop kind | Example | `unroll_factor` | `parallel` |\n214: |---|---|---|---|\n215: | Elementwise tile loop | `for i in asc2.range(tile_per_block)` | 2 | True |\n216: | Row distribution | `for r in asc2.range(asc2.block_idx(), num_rows, asc2.block_num())` | 2 | True |\n217: | Disjoint slice loop inside a row (e.g. RMSNorm split_d write-back) | `for tile_id in asc2.range(num_tiles)` | 2 | True |\n218: | Reduction accumulator (e.g. RMSNorm split_d `sum_sq = sum_sq + ...`) | `for tile_id in asc2.range(num_tiles)` | 2 | omit (default `False`) |\n219: | Compile-time loop over `asc.ConstExpr[int]` (matmul m / n loops) | `for i in range(m_tiles_per_block)` (Python `range`, not `asc2.range`) | n/a | n/a |\n220: \n221: Compile-time loops (last row) are already fully traced/unrolled at JIT\n222: time; wrapping them in `asc2.range(unroll_factor=2)` would emit a runtime\n223: `ForOp` and is a regression. Leave them as plain Python `range`.\n224: \n225: **Worked examples:**\n226: \n227: ```python\n228: # (1) elementwise tile loop -- no carry, fully parallel\n229: for i in asc2.range(tile_per_block, unroll_factor=2):\n230:     tile_offset = base_offset + i * tile_size\n231:     x = asc2.load(x_gm, [tile_size], offsets=[tile_offset])\n232:     asc2.store(asc2.abs(x), out_gm, offsets=[tile_offset])\n233: \n234: # (2) row distribution -- each row independent\n235: for r in asc2.range(asc2.block_idx(), num_rows, asc2.block_num(),\n236:                     unroll_factor=2):\n237:     row = asc2.load(x_gm, [1, num_cols], offsets=[r, 0])\n238:     s = asc2.reduce_sum(row)            # accumulation is INSIDE one call\n239:     asc2.store(asc2.full([1, OUT_PAD], s, dtype=asc.float32), out_gm,\n240:                offsets=[r, 0])\n241: \n242: # (3) reduction accumulator -- scalar carry across iterations -> NOT parallel\n243: sum_sq = asc2.reduce_sum(asc2.full([1, tile_cols], 0.0, dtype=asc.float32))\n244: for tile_id in asc2.range(num_tiles, unroll_factor=2):     # parallel omitted\n245:     col = tile_id * tile_cols\n246:     x = asc2.load(x_gm, [1, tile_cols], offsets=[row, col]).to(asc.float32)\n247:     sum_sq = sum_sq + asc2.reduce_sum(x * x)\n248: ```\n249: \n250: ### Verification pattern (numpy)\n251: \n252: ```python\n253: import numpy as np\n254: rng = np.random.default_rng(seed=2026)\n255: \n256: # CRITICAL: numpy Generator does NOT support dtype=float16.\n257: # Always generate as float32, then cast:\n258: x = (rng.random(size, dtype=np.float32) * 10 - 5).astype(np.float16)\n259: \n260: out = kernel_launch(x)\n261: expected = np.abs(x)\n262: np.testing.assert_allclose(out, expected, atol=1e-3, rtol=1e-3)\n263: ```\n264: \n265: **Recommended tolerances** (simulator introduces rounding). Each row\n266: cites the upstream source of truth at `pyasc-v2-eval@7b85554a`:\n267: \n268: - float16 elementwise: `atol=1e-3, rtol=1e-3` — matches our golden\n269:   `golden/kernels/abs_f16.py`. Upstream `operations/test_unary_ops.py`\n270:   asserts f32 at `atol=1e-3` against `torch.abs`; we extend that\n271:   contract to f16.\n272: - float16 composed (gelu, softmax): `atol=5e-2, rtol=5e-2` — matches\n273:   `golden/kernels/gelu_f16.py`. Upstream `kernels/test_gelu.py` uses\n274:   `rtol=1e-3, atol=1e-5` on f32; we relax for the f16 composed path.\n275: - float32 elementwise (matmul output / accumulators only): `atol=1e-5,\n276:   rtol=1e-5`. **Do not use `1e-5` for elementwise unary on f32** —\n277:   upstream `operations/test_unary_ops.py` ships `asc2.abs(f32)` at\n278:   `atol=1e-3` and our `capabilities.yaml` `abs/float32` cell agrees.\n279:   Use `atol=1e-3, rtol=1e-3` for unary float32 ops.\n280: - float32 composed (gelu, lean exp restatement of tanh/Pade):\n281:   `atol=1e-2, rtol=1e-2`. Upstream `target/test_gelu.py` is tighter\n282:   (`atol=1e-3`) but uses swapped polynomial coefficients (see\n283:   `docs/golden-upstream-map.md`); our looser bound is current\n284:   headroom and may tighten after a stability sweep.\n285: \n286: The previous version of this table said `float32 elementwise: 1e-5`\n287: unconditionally, which contradicted `capabilities.yaml` and was a\n288: known driver of agent confusion. The rule of thumb: **trust the\n289: golden's `assert_allclose` value as the contract; if the golden does\n290: not exist yet, take it from upstream `operations/test_*_ops.py` for\n291: that op family**.\n292: \n293: ## Available asc2 Operations\n294: \n295: ### Unary operations (on tiles)\n296: \n297: | Operation | Usage | Notes |\n298: |-----------|-------|-------|\n299: | `asc2.abs(x)` | Absolute value | |\n300: | `asc2.exp(x)` | Exponential | |\n301: | `asc2.log(x)` | Natural log | |\n302: | `asc2.sqrt(x)` | Square root | |\n303: | `asc2.relu(x)` | ReLU activation | |\n304: | `asc2.erf(x)` | Error function | Noisy on float32 simulator (~1.84-4.7 max abs error); avoid for f32 GELU — use the lean `asc2.exp` restatement instead. |\n305: | `asc2.tanh(x)` | Hyperbolic tangent | Bit-exact on the simulator, but heavier than `asc2.exp`. For f32 GELU, prefer the algebraically-equivalent `x / (1 + asc2.exp(-sqrt(8/pi) * (x + 0.044715*x^3)))` form (see f32 GELU Pattern below) — the asc2.tanh variant pushed the gelu/f32 cell over the 150s sim budget through Phase 9. |\n306: | `asc2.exp(x)` (re-listed for emphasis) | Exponential | Lean primitive; canonical building block for the f32 GELU sigmoid restatement. |\n307: | `asc2.sin(x)` | Sine | |\n308: | `asc2.cos(x)` | Cosine | |\n309: | `-x` | Negate | Unary operator |\n310: \n311: ### Binary operations (on tiles)\n312: \n313: | Operation | Usage | Notes |\n314: |-----------|-------|-------|\n315: | `x + y` | Add | |\n316: | `x - y` | Subtract | |\n317: | `x * y` | Multiply | |\n318: | `x / y` | Divide | |\n319: | `asc2.where(cond, a, b)` | Conditional select | Like `np.where`. **Size the destination tile to a multiple of 256 bytes** — see the destination-overrun note under JIT Options, which affects every reuse setting |\n320: | `asc2.equal(x, y)`, `not_equal`, `greater`, `greater_equal`, `less`, `less_equal` | Element-wise comparison, yields a mask | **No `int64` form.** Operands must be one of `int8, int16, int32, float16, bfloat16, float32`; an `int64` operand fails at codegen with `RuntimeError: 'input' dtype must be one of ..., got int64`. Narrow first — see Common Mistakes |\n321: \n322: ### Reduction operations\n323: \n324: | Operation | Usage | Notes |\n325: |-----------|-------|-------|\n326: | `asc2.reduce_sum(x)` | Full sum reduction | Returns scalar tile |\n327: | `asc2.reduce_sum(x, dim)` | Axis sum reduction | Reduce along given dim |\n328: | `asc2.reduce_max(x)` | Max reduction | Returns scalar tile |\n329: | `x.sum()` | Sum reduction | |\n330: | `x.max()` | Max reduction | |\n331: | `x.min()` | Min reduction | |\n332: \n333: ### Tile creation\n334: \n335: | Operation | Usage | Notes |\n336: |-----------|-------|-------|\n337: | `asc2.full(shape, scalar, dtype=...)` | Create tile filled with scalar | **Required** when storing scalar reduction results — last dim must be >= 32/sizeof(dtype) bytes for alignment |\n338: \n339: ### Advanced operations\n340: \n341: | Operation | Usage | Notes |\n342: |-----------|-------|-------|\n343: | `asc2.softmax(x)` | Softmax | Operates on full rows of a 2D tile |\n344: | `asc2.matmul(a, b)` or `a @ b` | Matrix multiply | Requires `asc2.TileLocation` for memory placement |\n345: | `asc2.reduce_sum(x*x)` + `asc2.sqrt(...)` | Root-mean-square layer norm (manual) | Two-kernel + host-dispatcher pattern on **C310 (Ascend950PR_9599)** mirroring CANN's `KernelRmsNormRegBase` (full row in UB) and `KernelRmsNormRegBaseSplitD` (stream along D). Inputs are `torch.Tensor` (numpy is silently zeroed on C310). The `asc2.rms_norm` builtin is currently NOT used. |\n346: \n347: ## Proven Kernel Patterns\n348: \n349: > **Use these exact patterns.** They are extracted from golden kernels verified on the CANN 9.0.0 simulator and cross-referenced against upstream `pyasc-v2-eval@7b85554a:python/test/asc2/`. Deviating from these patterns is the primary cause of runtime failures.\n350: \n351: ### Rule: rank-consistent tiling\n352: \n353: Every `asc2.tensor`, `asc2.load`, `asc2.store`, and `offsets=...` argument\n354: in the same kernel **must use the same rank**. If you declare a 2D\n355: tensor, your load shape is 2D and your offsets are 2D. If you flatten\n356: to 1D, load shape is 1D and offsets are 1D. Never mix.\n357: \n358: This is the single most common cause of v2 generative failures —\n359: agents see 2D test shapes in the prompt and emit:\n360: \n361: ```python\n362: # WRONG -- 2D tensor, 1D load shape, 2D offsets => rank mismatch\n363: x_gm = asc2.tensor(x_ptr, [num_rows, num_cols])\n364: row_idx = tile_offset // num_cols\n365: col_idx = tile_offset % num_cols\n366: x = asc2.load(x_gm, [tile_size], offsets=[row_idx, col_idx])\n367: out = asc2.abs(x)\n368: asc2.store(out, out_gm, offsets=[row_idx, col_idx])\n369: ```\n370: \n371: v2's strict rank check rejects this immediately:\n372: \n373: ```\n374: RuntimeError: rank of 'tensor_shape' must match rank of 'shape'\n375: asc.codegen.errors.CodegenError: at <source>:N:M\n376:     x = asc2.load(x_gm, [tile_size], offsets=[row_idx, col_idx])\n377:         ^\n378: ```\n379: \n380: The fix is either **flatten to 1D** (Pattern A or B below) or **keep\n381: 2D and align the load shape to it** (Pattern C). Pick one; do not\n382: half-flatten.\n383: \n384: Three patterns are valid on v2 for elementwise / composed-elementwise\n385: kernels. Pick by the kernel's needs; do not invent a fourth.\n386: \n387: ### Pattern A — 1D flatten (simple)\n388: \n389: Source: upstream `pyasc-v2-eval@7b85554a:python/test/asc2/kernels/test_vadd.py`.\n390: Use for any unary or binary element-wise operation (abs, exp, add,\n391: sub, gelu, leaky_relu, etc.) when the test shapes are 1D or can be\n392: collapsed to 1D at the host call site.\n393: \n394: ```python\n395: TILE_SIZE = 128\n396: CORE_NUM = 16\n397: \n398: @asc2.jit(always_compile=True)\n399: def my_kernel(x_ptr: asc.GlobalAddress, out_ptr: asc.GlobalAddress,\n400:               size: int, tile_size: asc.ConstExpr[int],\n401:               tile_per_block: asc.ConstExpr[int]):\n402:     x_gm = asc2.tensor(x_ptr, [size])           # 1D tensor\n403:     out_gm = asc2.tensor(out_ptr, [size])\n404:     base_offset = asc2.block_idx() * tile_size * tile_per_block\n405:     for i in asc2.range(tile_per_block, unroll_factor=2):\n406:         tile_offset = base_offset + i * tile_size\n407:         x = asc2.load(x_gm, [tile_size], offsets=[tile_offset])  # 1D load, 1D offsets\n408:         out = asc2.abs(x)  # replace with your op\n409:         asc2.store(out, out_gm, offsets=[tile_offset])\n410: \n411: # Launch (flatten multi-dim test inputs to 1D first):\n412: num_tiles = asc.ceildiv(size, TILE_SIZE)\n413: my_kernel[CORE_NUM](x, out, size, TILE_SIZE, asc.ceildiv(num_tiles, CORE_NUM))\n414: ```\n415: \n416: If your test shape is multi-dim (e.g. `[24, 512, 1024]`), compute the\n417: flat length at the host: `size = int(np.prod(test_shape))`. The kernel\n418: sees only `size` and never reasons about row/column structure.\n419: \n420: ### Pattern B — 1D flatten with cache-line aligned tail (production)\n421: \n422: Source: upstream `pyasc-v2-eval@7b85554a:python/test/asc2/target/test_vadd.py`.\n423: Use when the kernel must accept non-aligned input sizes (the kernel\n424: declares `tail_behavior: padded` or `aligned_only` with host-side\n425: padding). All Phase 9 new goldens follow this pattern.\n426: \n427: ```python\n428: ALIGNMENT_ELEMENTS = 32 // input_dtype.itemsize  # 32 bytes / dtype.itemsize\n429: tile_length = asc2.ceildiv(tile_length_request, ALIGNMENT_ELEMENTS) * ALIGNMENT_ELEMENTS\n430: block_loop_num = asc2.ceildiv(asc2.ceildiv(length, core_num), tile_length)\n431: block_length = tile_length * block_loop_num\n432: block_loop_num_tail = asc2.ceildiv(length - block_length * (core_num - 1), tile_length)\n433: padded_length = block_length * (core_num - 1) + tile_length * block_loop_num_tail\n434: # Then pad the host input to padded_length with zeros; the kernel skips\n435: # the last block_loop_num_tail iterations on the final core.\n436: \n437: @asc2.jit(static_alloc=True, reuse_ub=True)\n438: def my_kernel(x_ptr: asc2.GlobalAddress, out_ptr: asc2.GlobalAddress,\n439:               input_shape: asc2.ConstExpr, output_shape: asc2.ConstExpr,\n440:               block_loop_num: asc2.ConstExpr, block_loop_num_tail: asc2.ConstExpr,\n441:               tile_length: asc2.ConstExpr, block_length: asc2.ConstExpr,\n442:               UNROLL_FACTOR: asc2.ConstExpr):\n443:     x_gm = asc2.tensor(x_ptr, input_shape)\n444:     out_gm = asc2.tensor(out_ptr, output_shape)\n445:     block_offset = asc2.block_idx() * block_length\n446:     loop_count = block_loop_num\n447:     if asc2.block_idx() == (asc2.block_num() - 1):\n448:         loop_count = block_loop_num_tail\n449:     for i in asc2.range(loop_count, unroll_factor=UNROLL_FACTOR):\n450:         current_offset = block_offset + i * tile_length\n451:         xt = asc2.load(x_gm, [tile_length], offsets=[current_offset])\n452:         zt = asc2.abs(xt)  # replace with your op\n453:         asc2.store(zt, out_gm, offsets=[current_offset])\n454: ```\n455: \n456: ### Pattern C — 2D row-tiled\n457: \n458: Source: upstream `pyasc-v2-eval@7b85554a:python/test/asc2/kernels/test_gelu.py`.\n459: Use when the kernel must process one row at a time (per-row reductions\n460: or per-row composed ops where the iteration index is logically a row\n461: index). Ranks are 2D everywhere.\n462: \n463: ```python\n464: @asc2.jit(always_compile=True)\n465: def my_kernel(x_ptr: asc2.GlobalAddress, out_ptr: asc2.GlobalAddress,\n466:               num_rows: asc2.ConstExpr, num_columns: asc2.ConstExpr,\n467:               tile_size: asc2.ConstExpr):\n468:     x_gm = asc2.tensor(x_ptr, [num_rows, num_columns])    # 2D tensor\n469:     out_gm = asc2.tensor(out_ptr, [num_rows, num_columns])\n470:     for i in asc2.range(asc2.block_idx(), num_rows, asc2.block_num()):\n471:         row = asc2.load(x_gm, [1, tile_size], offsets=[i, 0])  # 2D load, 2D offsets\n472:         # ... per-row computation here, e.g. erf-form GELU:\n473:         k = asc2.sqrt(0.5)\n474:         out = row * (asc2.erf(row * k) + 1) * 0.5\n475:         asc2.store(out, out_gm, offsets=[i, 0])\n476: \n477: # Launch:\n478: core_num = 16\n479: tile_size = 1024\n480: my_kernel[core_num](x, out, num_rows, num_columns, tile_size)\n481: ```\n482: \n483: Note `[1, tile_size]` for the load shape (rank 2) and `[i, 0]` for\n484: offsets (rank 2) — both match the rank of the 2D tensor.\n485: \n486: ### Composed (gelu, leaky_relu) — choose Pattern A or C\n487: \n488: Composed elementwise ops (gelu, leaky_relu) work in either Pattern A\n489: (1D flatten + composed math in the loop body) or Pattern C (2D\n490: row-tiled). Both are valid on v2 as long as **ranks stay consistent**.\n491: \n492: Two GELU forms are supported on the simulator; pick by dtype:\n493: \n494: ```python\n495: # float16 GELU (erf form -- simulator erf precision is fine at f16 tolerance):\n496: k = asc2.sqrt(0.5)\n497: out = x * (asc2.erf(x * k) + 1) * 0.5\n498: \n499: # float32 GELU (tanh/Pade form via the LEAN exp/sigmoid restatement\n500: # -- simulator erf is too noisy on f32 AND simulator asc2.tanh\n501: # pushed this cell over the 150s sim budget through Phase 9):\n502: # Math identity: 0.5*x*(1+tanh(sqrt(2/pi)*(x+0.044715*x^3)))\n503: #              = x / (1 + exp(-sqrt(8/pi)*(x+0.044715*x^3)))\n504: # Define module-level constants OUTSIDE @asc2.jit:\n505: #     GELU_C = 0.044715\n506: #     NEG_SQRT_EIGHT_OVER_PI = -math.sqrt(8.0 / math.pi)\n507: x_cub = x * x * x\n508: inner = (x_cub * GELU_C + x) * NEG_SQRT_EIGHT_OVER_PI\n509: out = x / (asc2.exp(inner) + 1)\n510: # Replaces asc2.tanh + scalar_mul + add + scalar_mul with\n511: # asc2.exp + add + div (one fewer asc2 op per tile, no tanh dep).\n512: # Verified in golden/kernels/gelu_f32.py at TILE_SIZE=64, CORE_NUM=16.\n513: \n514: # Leaky ReLU kernel op (inside @asc2.jit):\n515: out = asc2.where(x >= 0, x, x * alpha)\n516: ```\n517: \n518: Simulator constraints to honour:\n519: - **Module-level constants only.** `asc2.*` functions are valid only\n520:   inside a `@asc2.jit` body; module-level constants must use Python or\n521:   `math.*` (e.g. `NEG_SQRT_EIGHT_OVER_PI = -math.sqrt(8.0 / math.pi)`).\n522:   Calling `math.sqrt` inside a `@asc2.jit` body raises\n523:   `RuntimeError: Unsupported function referenced`. Calling `asc2.sqrt(0.5)`\n524:   outside `@asc2.jit` (at module scope) raises\n525:   `AttributeError: 'NoneType' object has no attribute 'create_math_SqrtOp'`.\n526:   Use `asc2.sqrt(0.5)` **inside** the jit body (as in the f16 GELU example)\n527:   or precompute as a module-level Python constant (as in the f32 GELU\n528:   example).\n529: - For f32 GELU, pin `TILE_SIZE = 64`, `CORE_NUM = 16`. With wider tiles\n530:   (128) only the first 64 elements get written (a wide-tile lowering\n531:   bug on C310's simulator path); the rest are silently zero. Same class\n532:   of issue as the wide-tile rms_norm history.\n533: \n534: **GELU host-side verification** (pick one):\n535: \n536: ```python\n537: import math\n538: \n539: # float16 erf form: vectorise math.erf -- do NOT use np.erf or scipy.\n540: _verf = np.vectorize(math.erf)\n541: expected_f16 = (0.5 * x * (1.0 + _verf(x.astype(np.float32) / np.sqrt(2.0)))).astype(np.float16)\n542: np.testing.assert_allclose(out_f16.astype(np.float32),\n543:                            expected_f16.astype(np.float32),\n544:                            atol=5e-2, rtol=5e-2)\n545: \n546: # float32 tanh form: use np.tanh (no scipy needed):\n547: k = np.sqrt(2.0 / np.pi)\n548: expected_f32 = 0.5 * x * (1.0 + np.tanh(k * (x + 0.044715 * x ** 3)))\n549: np.testing.assert_allclose(out_f32, expected_f32, atol=1e-2, rtol=1e-2)\n550: ```\n551: \n552: ### Tier 1 — Reduction (row-wise)\n553: \n554: Use for reduce_sum, reduce_max, etc. Key differences from elementwise:\n555: - 2D tensor layout; rows distributed across cores via `asc2.range(block_idx, num_rows, block_num)`\n556: - Scalar results must be wrapped with `asc2.full()` before storing (32-byte alignment)\n557: - Output buffer must be padded (e.g., `OUT_PAD = 8` for float32)\n558: \n559: ```python\n560: OUT_PAD = 8  # min last-dim for 32-byte alignment with float32\n561: \n562: @asc2.jit(always_compile=True)\n563: def reduce_sum_kernel(x_ptr: asc.GlobalAddress, out_ptr: asc.GlobalAddress,\n564:                       num_rows: int, num_cols: asc.ConstExpr[int],\n565:                       out_pad: asc.ConstExpr[int]):\n566:     x_gm = asc2.tensor(x_ptr, [num_rows, num_cols])\n567:     out_gm = asc2.tensor(out_ptr, [num_rows, out_pad])\n568:     for i in asc2.range(asc2.block_idx(), num_rows, asc2.block_num(),\n569:                         unroll_factor=2):\n570:         row = asc2.load(x_gm, [1, num_cols], offsets=[i, 0])\n571:         s = asc2.reduce_sum(row)            # accumulation is INSIDE one call\n572:         result = asc2.full([1, out_pad], s, dtype=row.dtype)\n573:         asc2.store(result, out_gm, offsets=[i, 0])\n574: \n575: # Launch + extract results:\n576: out = np.zeros((num_rows, OUT_PAD), dtype=x.dtype)\n577: reduce_sum_kernel[CORE_NUM](x, out, num_rows, num_cols, OUT_PAD)\n578: result = out[:, 0]  # extract first column\n579: ```\n580: \n581: ### Tier 3 — Advanced (softmax, matmul)\n582: \n583: Use `asc2.softmax()` on a block of full rows. Do NOT decompose softmax manually.\n584: \n585: **matmul** — supported. Two strict requirements:\n586: \n587: 1. **Platform must be `Ascend950PR_9599`** (cube unit) — and so does the entire\n588:    stack: it is the only simulator platform CI targets. Pass `-v Ascend950PR_9599`\n589:    when running the kernel script (the goldens default to it).\n590: 2. **Inputs must be `torch.Tensor`** (not numpy arrays). The simulator silently\n591:    lowers numpy arrays to zero for matmul. This is the *only* operation that\n592:    requires torch on the host side; everything else stays numpy-only.\n593: \n594: The `asc2.matmul` (or `@`) API loads the operands to `L0A` / `L0B` and always\n595: produces a `float32` result tile, even for `float16` inputs.\n596: \n597: Proven kernel pattern (from `golden/kernels/matmul_f16.py`):\n598: \n599: ```python\n600: import torch\n601: import asc, asc2\n602: import asc.runtime.config as config\n603: \n604: @asc2.jit(always_compile=True)\n605: def matmul_kernel(a_ptr, b_ptr, c_ptr,\n606:                   a_shape: asc.ConstExpr, b_shape: asc.ConstExpr, c_shape: asc.ConstExpr,\n607:                   m_tile: asc.ConstExpr[int], m_tiles_per_block: asc.ConstExpr[int],\n608:                   n_tile: asc.ConstExpr[int], n_tiles_per_block: asc.ConstExpr[int]):\n609:     a_gm = asc2.tensor(a_ptr, a_shape)\n610:     b_gm = asc2.tensor(b_ptr, b_shape)\n611:     c_gm = asc2.tensor(c_ptr, c_shape)\n612:     block_id = asc2.block_idx()\n613:     m_elems_per_block = m_tile * m_tiles_per_block\n614:     m_base_off = (m_elems_per_block * block_id) % a_shape[0]\n615:     n_base_off = ((m_elems_per_block * block_id) // a_shape[0]) * (n_tile * n_tiles_per_block)\n616:     for j in range(n_tiles_per_block):\n617:         b_offset = n_base_off + j * n_tile\n618:         b_j = asc2.load(b_gm, [b_shape[0], n_tile], offsets=[0, b_offset],\n619:                         location=asc2.TileLocation.L0B)\n620:         for i in range(m_tiles_per_block):\n621:             a_offset = m_base_off + i * m_tile\n622:             a_i = asc2.load(a_gm, [m_tile, a_shape[1]], offsets=[a_offset, 0],\n623:                             location=asc2.TileLocation.L0A)\n624:             c_ij = a_i @ b_j     # asc2.matmul; result is float32\n625:             asc2.store(c_ij, c_gm, offsets=[a_offset, b_offset])\n626: \n627: # Host-side launch (torch, not numpy):\n628: a = torch.rand((m, k), dtype=torch.float16)\n629: b = torch.rand((k, n), dtype=torch.float16)\n630: c = torch.zeros((m, n), dtype=torch.float32)   # output is float32\n631: matmul_kernel[core_num](a, b, c, a.shape, b.shape, c.shape,\n632:                         m_tile, m_tiles_per_block, n_tile, n_tiles_per_block)\n633: c_ref = a.to(torch.float32) @ b.to(torch.float32)\n634: torch.testing.assert_close(c, c_ref, atol=1e-2, rtol=1e-2)\n635: ```\n636: \n637: Tile-size constraints (from the proven pattern):\n638: \n639: - `m_tile % 16 == 0`, `n_tile % 16 == 0`\n640: - `m_tile * k * a.element_size() <= 64 KiB` (L0A budget)\n641: - `n_tile * k * b.element_size() <= 64 KiB` (L0B budget)\n642: - `m % m_tile == 0`, `n % n_tile == 0`\n643: - Tiles distributed evenly across `core_num` blocks\n644: \n645: Recommended starter shape: `m=k=n=16, core_num=1, m_tile=n_tile=16,\n646: m_tiles_per_block=n_tiles_per_block=1`. Once that passes, scale up.\n647: \n648: #### CUBE-only batched matmul (BatchMatMulV3) + perf levers\n649: \n650: For a **batched** matmul `C[b] = A[b] @ B[b]` (the first CUBE-only\n651: operator-generation demo), build on the single-GEMM pattern with three additions\n652: (proven in `golden/kernels/batch_mat_mul_v3_f16.py`, measured PASS at ratio 0.78\n653: vs the canonical `aclnnBatchMatMul`):\n654: \n655: 1. **Batch across cores.** Flatten `[B,M,K]`→`[B*M,K]`, `[B,K,N]`→`[B*K,N]`,\n656:    `[B,M,N]`→`[B*M,N]` on the host so each batch is a contiguous row-block, launch\n657:    `kernel[B]`, and let `bi = asc2.block_idx()` own batch `bi` (rows `bi*M` of A/C,\n658:    `bi*K` of B). All loads/offsets stay 2-D, satisfying the cube tile rules.\n659: 2. **f16 in / f32 accumulate / f16 out.** The cube result tile is always f32; cast\n660:    it on store with `c_ij = (a_i @ b_j).to(asc2.float16)` (the fixpipe cast path,\n661:    cf. `test_matmul_fixpipe.py`). Allocate the host output as `torch.float16`.\n662: 3. **L1 staging + a pipelined N-tile loop (the perf levers).** Stage the whole\n663:    per-batch `A[m,k]`/`B[k,n]` into `L1` once so every GM element is read a single\n664:    time, then copy `L1`→`L0A`/`L0B` tiles. Pipeline the inner N loop with\n665:    `asc2.range(n_tiles, unroll_factor=2)` so the next L0B copy\n666:    overlaps the current MMAD.\n667: \n668: ```python\n669: a_l1 = asc2.load(a_gm, [m, k], offsets=[bi * m, 0], location=asc2.TileLocation.L1)\n670: b_l1 = asc2.load(b_gm, [k, n], offsets=[bi * k, 0], location=asc2.TileLocation.L1)\n671: for i in range(m // m_tile):                      # plain range: A stays in L0A\n672:     a_i = asc2.copy(a_l1, [m_tile, k], offsets=[i * m_tile, 0], location=asc2.TileLocation.L0A)\n673:     for j in asc2.range(n // n_tile, unroll_factor=2):  # double-buffered L0B\n674:         b_j = asc2.copy(b_l1, [k, n_tile], offsets=[0, j * n_tile], location=asc2.TileLocation.L0B)\n675:         c_ij = (a_i @ b_j).to(asc2.float16)\n676:         asc2.store(c_ij, c_gm, offsets=[bi * m + i * m_tile, j * n_tile])\n677: ```\n678: \n679: **Perf-tuning levers, in priority order** (measure each on the camodel — no\n680: hand-edited ticks):\n681: \n682: - **L1 staging** is the first win (each GM element read once instead of re-fetched\n683:   per tile). For the `[16,256,256]` contract this alone took `gen` 23434→21620.\n684: - **`gm_barrier=False` (overlap-enabled) double-buffering** is the second win\n685:   (21620→18758, ratio 0.78). **Critical budget rule:** overlap *doubles* the\n686:   buffer it pipelines, so the 2-deep tile must fit half the L0 capacity. A\n687:   full-K `[256,128]` f16 L0B tile is already 64 KiB and overflows when doubled —\n688:   drop to `N_TILE=64` (`[256,64]` f16 = 32 KiB ⇒ 64 KiB for the pair). Only\n689:   pipeline the loop whose L0 buffer you can halve; keep the other loop a plain\n690:   `range`.\n691: - **Batch-to-core mapping** is maxed at `CORE_NUM=B` when `B` ≤ the AIC core count\n692:   (16 cube cores here, so 16 batches = one fully parallel wave). Launching more\n693:   blocks than cores adds waves, not parallelism.\n694: - **K-tiling** (`asc2.zeros_acc` + `asc2.matmul_acc` over a `parallel` K loop, cf.\n695:   `test_matmul_tiled.py`) is the alternative when a single A/B tile cannot fit L0;\n696:   not needed at K=256 (the A tile fits L0A whole).\n697: \n698: L0 budget reminder for the pair-buffered case: each pipelined L0 tile ≤ **32 KiB**\n699: (half of 64 KiB); the f32 L0C accumulator tile ≤ **64 KiB** (half of 128 KiB).\n700: \n701: **softmax** — use `asc2.softmax()` directly:\n702: \n703: ```python\n704: @asc2.jit(always_compile=True)\n705: def softmax_kernel(x_ptr: asc.GlobalAddress, out_ptr: asc.GlobalAddress,\n706:                    num_rows: int, num_cols: asc.ConstExpr[int],\n707:                    block_size: asc.ConstExpr[int]):\n708:     x_gm = asc2.tensor(x_ptr, [num_rows, num_cols])\n709:     out_gm = asc2.tensor(out_ptr, [num_rows, num_cols])\n710:     start_row = asc2.block_idx() * block_size\n711:     rows = asc2.load(x_gm, [block_size, num_cols], offsets=[start_row, 0])\n712:     out = asc2.softmax(rows)\n713:     asc2.store(out, out_gm, offsets=[start_row, 0])\n714: \n715: # Launch:\n716: block_size = asc.ceildiv(num_rows, CORE_NUM)\n717: softmax_kernel[CORE_NUM](x, out, num_rows, num_cols, block_size)\n718: ```\n719: \n720: **Softmax simulator constraint:** Test ONLY the shape specified in the prompt.\n721: The simulator is extremely slow for large softmax shapes. Do NOT add extra shapes.\n722: \n723: #### Normalization layers — two-kernel RMSNorm with host dispatcher (C310)\n724: \n725: RMSNorm in this skill stack ships **two `@asc2.jit` kernels** with a\n726: **host-side dispatcher**, mirroring CANN's arch35 (C310) `rms_norm` op\n727: ([`opp/built-in/.../rms_norm/rms_norm.cpp`](../../../home/aloschilov/Ascend/cann-9.0.0/opp/built-in/op_impl/ai_core/tbe/impl/ops_nn/ascendc/rms_norm/rms_norm.cpp))\n728: which selects between `KernelRmsNormRegBase` (tiling key `5000`) and\n729: `KernelRmsNormRegBaseSplitD` (tiling key `2001`) based on whether the\n730: row fits in UB. The pyasc analogue is structurally identical:\n731: \n732: | CANN kernel | Pyasc analogue | When |\n733: |-------------|---------------|------|\n734: | `KernelRmsNormRegBase` | `rms_norm_full_row_kernel` | Row tile fits in UB; `num_cols` is `asc.ConstExpr[int]` |\n735: | `KernelRmsNormRegBaseSplitD` | `rms_norm_split_d_kernel` | Row exceeds UB; both dims runtime int, host-padded to `tile_cols=64` chunks |\n736: \n737: **Platform / I/O contract:**\n738: \n739: - Run on `Ascend950PR_9599` (compilation arch `C310`). The skill stack's\n740:   CI routes `rms_norm_*` goldens to that simulator alongside `matmul_*`.\n741: - Inputs are `torch.Tensor` (CPU `float32`/`float16`). Numpy arrays are\n742:   silently zeroed on the C310 simulator path; this is a known property\n743:   of the `Ascend950PR_9599` runtime and applies to RMSNorm as well as\n744:   matmul.\n745: \n746: **Host-side dispatcher (verbatim from `golden/kernels/rms_norm_f32.py`):**\n747: \n748: ```python\n749: UB_BUDGET_BYTES = 64 * 1024  # conservative UB headroom for accumulator + gamma + double-buffer\n750: \n751: def rms_norm_launch(x: torch.Tensor, gamma: torch.Tensor,\n752:                     eps: float = 1e-5, core_num: int = 8) -> torch.Tensor:\n753:     num_rows, num_cols = x.shape\n754:     row_bytes = num_cols * x.element_size()\n755:     if row_bytes <= UB_BUDGET_BYTES and num_cols % 8 == 0:\n756:         return _full_row_launch(x, gamma, eps, core_num)\n757:     return _split_d_launch(x, gamma, eps, core_num)\n758: ```\n759: \n760: The threshold heuristic mirrors CANN's tiling: pick the fast path when\n761: the row fits with headroom; otherwise stream.\n762: \n763: **Full-row kernel (`KernelRmsNormRegBase` analogue):**\n764: \n765: ```python\n766: @asc2.jit(always_compile=True)\n767: def rms_norm_full_row_kernel(x_ptr, gamma_ptr, out_ptr,\n768:                              num_rows: int,\n769:                              num_cols: asc.ConstExpr[int],\n770:                              epsilon: asc.ConstExpr[float]):\n771:     x_gm = asc2.tensor(x_ptr, [num_rows, num_cols])\n772:     gamma_gm_2d = asc2.tensor(gamma_ptr, [1, num_cols])\n773:     out_gm = asc2.tensor(out_ptr, [num_rows, num_cols])\n774:     for row in asc2.range(asc2.block_idx(), num_rows, asc2.block_num(),\n775:                           unroll_factor=2):\n776:         x_row = asc2.load(x_gm, [1, num_cols], offsets=[row, 0])\n777:         x_row_f32 = x_row.to(asc.float32)\n778:         sum_sq = asc2.reduce_sum(x_row_f32 * x_row_f32)\n779:         inv_rms = 1.0 / asc2.sqrt(sum_sq / num_cols + epsilon)\n780:         gamma_row = asc2.load(gamma_gm_2d, [1, num_cols], offsets=[0, 0])\n781:         gamma_row_f32 = gamma_row.to(asc.float32)\n782:         out_f32 = x_row_f32 * gamma_row_f32 * inv_rms\n783:         asc2.store(out_f32.to(x_row.dtype), out_gm, offsets=[row, 0])\n784: ```\n785: \n786: `num_cols` is `ConstExpr` because the row tile shape `[1, num_cols]` must\n787: be compile-time known. The accumulator stays in float32 even for float16\n788: input via `.to(asc.float32)`.\n789: \n790: **Split-D kernel (`KernelRmsNormRegBaseSplitD` analogue):**\n791: \n792: Both `num_rows` and `num_cols` are runtime `int`. The row is streamed in\n793: `tile_cols=64` (one Ascend SIMD lane: 64 floats = 256 bytes) tiles, with\n794: host-side zero padding so the tail vanishes:\n795: \n796: ```python\n797: @asc2.jit(always_compile=True)\n798: def rms_norm_split_d_kernel(x_ptr, gamma_ptr, out_ptr,\n799:                             num_rows: int, num_cols: int, padded_cols: int,\n800:                             num_tiles: int,\n801:                             tile_cols: asc.ConstExpr[int],\n802:                             epsilon: asc.ConstExpr[float]):\n803:     x_gm = asc2.tensor(x_ptr, [num_rows, padded_cols])\n804:     gamma_gm_2d = asc2.tensor(gamma_ptr, [1, padded_cols])\n805:     out_gm = asc2.tensor(out_ptr, [num_rows, padded_cols])\n806:     for row in asc2.range(asc2.block_idx(), num_rows, asc2.block_num(),\n807:                           unroll_factor=2):\n808:         zero_seed = asc2.full([1, tile_cols], 0.0, dtype=asc.float32)\n809:         sum_sq = asc2.reduce_sum(zero_seed)\n810:         # Inner reduction loop carries `sum_sq` -> NOT parallel.\n811:         for tile_id in asc2.range(num_tiles, unroll_factor=2):\n812:             col = tile_id * tile_cols\n813:             x = asc2.load(x_gm, [1, tile_cols], offsets=[row, col])\n814:             x_f32 = x.to(asc.float32)\n815:             sum_sq = sum_sq + asc2.reduce_sum(x_f32 * x_f32)\n816:         inv_rms = 1.0 / asc2.sqrt(sum_sq / num_cols + epsilon)\n817:         # Disjoint write-back -> safe to parallelise.\n818:         for tile_id in asc2.range(num_tiles, unroll_factor=2):\n819:             col = tile_id * tile_cols\n820:             x = asc2.load(x_gm, [1, tile_cols], offsets=[row, col])\n821:             gamma = asc2.load(gamma_gm_2d, [1, tile_cols], offsets=[0, col])\n822:             x_f32 = x.to(asc.float32)\n823:             gamma_f32 = gamma.to(asc.float32)\n824:             out_f32 = x_f32 * gamma_f32 * inv_rms\n825:             asc2.store(out_f32.to(x.dtype), out_gm, offsets=[row, col])\n826: ```\n827: \n828: Padded zeros don't contribute to `sum_sq`; the kernel divides by REAL\n829: `num_cols` so the result is identical to a no-padding implementation.\n830: \n831: **`sum_sq` PlainValue seed (REQUIRED in split_d):** the codegen rejects\n832: `sum_sq = 0.0` because the loop-carried value becomes a `PlainValue`,\n833: giving `'sum_sq' was re-assigned to an object with different type`\n834: ([function_visitor.py L241-244](../../pyasc-fork/python/asc/codegen/function_visitor.py)).\n835: Seed with a 1-vector-lane reduce-of-zero (full_row doesn't need this\n836: because there's no carry across tiles):\n837: \n838: ```python\n839: zero_seed = asc2.full([1, tile_cols], 0.0, dtype=asc.float32)\n840: sum_sq = asc2.reduce_sum(zero_seed)\n841: ```\n842: \n843: **Host-side launch (split_d):**\n844: \n845: ```python\n846: padded_cols = ((num_cols + tile_cols - 1) // tile_cols) * tile_cols\n847: x_padded = torch.zeros((num_rows, padded_cols), dtype=x.dtype)\n848: x_padded[:, :num_cols] = x\n849: gamma_padded = torch.zeros((padded_cols,), dtype=gamma.dtype)\n850: gamma_padded[:num_cols] = gamma\n851: out_padded = torch.zeros((num_rows, padded_cols), dtype=x.dtype)\n852: num_tiles = padded_cols // tile_cols\n853: rms_norm_split_d_kernel[CORE_NUM](x_padded, gamma_padded, out_padded,\n854:                                   num_rows, num_cols, padded_cols,\n855:                                   num_tiles, tile_cols, epsilon)\n856: return out_padded[:, :num_cols].clone()\n857: ```\n858: \n859: **Host-side reference (torch):**\n860: \n861: ```python\n862: def torch_rms_norm(x, gamma, eps):\n863:     x32 = x.to(torch.float32)\n864:     mean_sq = torch.mean(x32 * x32, dim=-1, keepdim=True)\n865:     return (x32 * torch.rsqrt(mean_sq + eps) * gamma.to(torch.float32)).to(x.dtype)\n866: ```\n867: \n868: **Tolerances:** float32 `atol=rtol=1e-4` (both branches); float16\n869: `atol=rtol=2e-2` for full_row, `atol=rtol=5e-2` for split_d.\n870: \n871: **Test shapes:** `(8, 256)` exercises full_row; `(8, 1055)` exercises\n872: split_d (host pads to `1088 = 17 * 64`). Both verified in a single\n873: `run_kernel` call. The pattern rescales to e.g. `(64, 100003)` by\n874: changing only `CORE_NUM` and the test-shape constants.\n875: \n876: **Note on \"first or last dim\":** RMSNorm here normalizes along the last\n877: dim only. Prompts that mention \"first or last dimension\" should be\n878: answered with the last-dim form above; the runtime contract above is\n879: what makes the row dim dynamic.\n880: \n881: #### LayerNormV4 delta (vs RMSNorm)\n882: \n883: LayerNorm adds **mean subtraction**, a **beta** vector, and optional mean/rstd\n884: side outputs. The golden pattern mirrors RMSNorm's host dispatcher but uses a\n885: **two-stat** row kernel:\n886: \n887: ```python\n888: # full_row (when num_cols % 8 == 0 and num_cols * 4 * 6 <= UB_BUDGET_BYTES)\n889: mean = asc2.reduce_sum(x_f32, 1, keep_dims=True) / num_cols\n890: mean_b = asc2.broadcast_to(mean, 1, padded_cols)\n891: sum_x2 = asc2.reduce_sum(x_f32 * x_f32, 1, keep_dims=True) / num_cols\n892: var = sum_x2 - mean * mean          # E[x²] − mean² (padding-safe)\n893: rstd = asc2.rsqrt(var + epsilon)\n894: y = (x_f32 - mean_b) * rstd_b * gamma + beta\n895: ```\n896: \n897: **N-D contract:** flatten any input to `[rows, last_dim]` on the host\n898: (`rows = numel // last_dim`), launch, reshape `y` back. `normalizedShape=[last]`\n899: for the aclnn reference; mean/rstd shapes are `leading_dims + [1]`, dtypes\n900: `ACL_FLOAT` even when input is bf16.\n901: \n902: **split_d:** stream `tile_cols=64`; merge the mean+variance tile loops\n903: (`sum_x` and `sum_x2` in one pass); use `asc2.full([1, tile_cols], mean, ...)`\n904: to subtract a `PlainValue` mean from a tile (tile − scalar is unsupported);\n905: compute `rstd` via `1.0 / asc2.sqrt(var + eps)` (scalar `rsqrt` needs a Tile).\n906: \n907: **bf16:** build probe inputs as f32 numpy → `torch.bfloat16`; keep stats in f32\n908: inside the kernel (`.to(asc.float32)` on loads).\n909: \n910: See `golden/kernels/layer_norm_v4_f32.py` and `layer_norm_v4_bf16.py`.\n911: \n912: #### Multi-input / multi-axis copy (concat) — ONE packed input + scalar offsets\n913: \n914: Pure data-movement ops with **multiple inputs** (concat) must take **one packed\n915: input tensor**, not N `asc2.GlobalAddress` pointers, and address each logical\n916: sub-input by offset arithmetic. This is the CANN reviewer-mandated shape (MR !331)\n917: and supports runtime arity (see metadata pattern below).\n918: \n919: **Ranked 2-D sub-view of a packed buffer at a runtime offset (now supported).**\n920: `asc2.tensor(in_ptr + B_i, [rows, w_i])` builds a ranked 2-D view of input `i`'s\n921: sub-region of the single packed buffer (`B_i` = element base). `GlobalAddress +\n922: offset` emits `emitasc.ptr_offset`; this **used to fail** because the op was\n923: ranked-only and kernel-arg pointers are unranked memrefs (`'emitasc.ptr_offset'\n924: op operand #0 must be memref ... but got 'memref<*xi64>'`). It now lowers after\n925: relaxing `EmitAsc_PtrOffsetOp` `$base`/`$result` to `AnyRankedOrUnrankedMemRef`\n926: (issue #1 / discussion #2; branch `corc-concat-d-ptr-offset`). All three proposed\n927: options emit identical AscendC (`SetGlobalBuffer(base + offset)` + `DataCopy`), so\n928: the one-line constraint relaxation was chosen as minimal and fully general\n929: (`asctile.tensor` already accepts unranked bases; `ConvertTensor` forwards the\n930: base to `SetGlobalBuffer`; the emitter prints `base + offset`).\n931: \n932: This unlocks **CANN-style multi-row bulk copy** for the aligned paths: per input,\n933: copy `[ubFactorDim0, w_i]` tiles (many rows per DMA) over the 2-D sub-view instead\n934: of one DMA per row — `ubFactorDim0 = min(maxAvaliableUb / catDim1, catDim0)`,\n935: mirroring CANN `TilingUbForNosplitDim1`. Collapses `rows` DMAs/region into\n936: `ceil(rows / ubFactorDim0)` larger transfers (e.g. `2048×16` f32 → 988 rows/tile\n937: → 3 DMAs vs 2048, ~680× fewer). The runtime-arity / unaligned / wide-row paths\n938: still use the 1-D packed model with flat scalar `offsets=` (below).\n939: \n940: A concat on axis `k` reduces to a 2-D row-concat\n941: `[rows = prod(shape[:k]), out_cols = Σ_i prod(input_i.shape[k:])]`; input `i` of\n942: inner width `w_i` lives at flat input base `B_i = Σ_{j<i} rows*w_j` and output\n943: column base `C_i = Σ_{j<i} w_j`. Element (row `r`, col `c`) of input `i` copies\n944: `packed[B_i + r*w_i + c] -> out[r*out_cols + C_i + c]`. Host packs the inputs\n945: input-major: `packed = torch.cat([t.reshape(-1) for t in inputs])`. Ground truth\n946: is `torch.cat(inputs, dim=k)` reshaped.\n947: \n948: **Variable arity via RUNTIME metadata (one kernel for arity 2..N).** Don't write\n949: `concat3`/`concat4`/`concat16` (one kernel per arity) and don't unroll a Python\n950: tuple of pointers with `static_range`. Instead pass three small int metadata GM\n951: tensors (`widths[i]=w_i`, `in_bases[i]=B_i`, `out_bases[i]=C_i`) and a runtime\n952: `num_inputs`. **GM-loaded scalars are usable as `asc2.ceildiv` loop bounds and\n953: inside `offsets=`**, so per-input geometry is resolved at runtime with no\n954: per-arity recompile:\n955: \n956: ```python\n957: @asc2.jit(static_alloc=True, reuse_ub=True)\n958: def concat_generic(in_ptr, w_ptr, ib_ptr, ob_ptr, out_ptr, num_inputs, rows, out_cols, total_in,\n959:                    chunk: asc2.ConstExpr, ubf: asc2.ConstExpr, unroll_factor: asc2.ConstExpr):\n960:     widths = asc2.tensor(w_ptr, [num_inputs])\n961:     in_bases = asc2.tensor(ib_ptr, [num_inputs])\n962:     out_bases = asc2.tensor(ob_ptr, [num_inputs])\n963:     out2d = asc2.tensor(out_ptr, [rows, out_cols])     # 2-D output view\n964:     for s in asc2.range(num_inputs):\n965:         w = asc2.load(widths, offsets=[s])             # GM scalar (dynamic width)\n966:         ib = asc2.load(in_bases, offsets=[s])\n967:         ob = asc2.load(out_bases, offsets=[s])\n968:         in_s = asc2.tensor(in_ptr + ib, [rows, w])     # ranked 2-D sub-view at runtime base ib\n969:         # multi-row bulk copy: ubf rows per DMA (ptr_offset AnyRankedOrUnrankedMemRef relaxation, issue #1)\n970:         for t in range(asc2.block_idx(), asc2.ceildiv(rows, ubf), asc2.block_num(),\n971:                        unroll_factor=unroll_factor):\n972:             r0 = t * ubf\n973:             nr = ubf if r0 + ubf <= rows else rows - r0          # ternary -> select, NOT a guard\n974:             for c in asc2.range(asc2.ceildiv(w, chunk)):         # runtime bound -> any width fits\n975:                 c0 = c * chunk\n976:                 csize = chunk if c0 + chunk <= w else w - c0\n977:                 tile = asc2.load(in_s, [ubf, chunk], real_shape=[nr, csize], offsets=[r0, c0])\n978:                 asc2.store(tile, out2d, real_shape=[nr, csize], offsets=[r0, ob + c0])\n979: ```\n980: \n981: This is **CANN-style multi-row bulk copy**: per input, the ranked 2-D sub-view\n982: `asc2.tensor(in_ptr + ib, [rows, w])` lets one DMA move `ubf` rows (sized so\n983: `ubf*chunk` fits the per-region UB budget), collapsing the prior one-DMA-per-row\n984: storm into `ceil(rows/ubf)` transfers per (input, column-chunk). It recovered a\n985: 4-6x regression on narrow-width multi-input cases (arity 3/4 middle/last). The\n986: earlier per-row path (`asc2.load(inp, [chunk], offsets=[ib + i*w + off])` over a\n987: 1-D packed view) is what regressed; do not reintroduce it.\n988: \n989: For a **first-axis** concat the input-major packed buffer is **byte-identical to\n990: the output**, so it is one contiguous `total`-element copy: route to a flat\n991: chunked copy (`concat_flat_copy`) that fans `ub_chunk`-sized chunks of the whole\n992: buffer across cores. Do NOT route first-axis multi-input through `concat_generic`\n993: with `rows=1`: parallelism there is over rows, so only one core works while the\n994: rest idle (this single-core path was a measured regression for arity-16 first-axis).\n995: \n996: **Don't ship a kernel that is a special case of another.** A single-core\n997: whole-row copy (`concat_simt`) is just the row-distributed `concat_all_align`\n998: launched with `block_num=1` — delete the special case and route to the general\n999: kernel via launch params. Likewise, **size the launch so every core has work**\n1000: instead of guarding inside the kernel: for a contiguous dim0 split, launch\n1001: `usedCoreNum = ceildiv(dim0, block_factor)` cores (CANN `blockFactor`/\n1002: `usedCoreNum`/`tailBlockFactor`) so `r_start = block_idx*block_factor < dim0`\n1003: always holds and no `if r_start < dim0:` guard is needed.\n1004: \n1005: **UB budgeting rule (CRITICAL — sizing `chunk`), grounded in CANN.** Mirror the\n1006: CANN concat tiling (`conversion/concat/op_host/arch35/concat_tiling_arch35.cpp`,\n1007: `TilingUb`): `maxAvaliableUb = (UB_CAPACITY − INDEX_USE_UB) / dtypeSize` with\n1008: `INDEX_USE_UB = 1024`, and the non-aligned in-UB-concat path divides by\n1009: `BUFFER_NUM = 2` (double buffer). The reused-buffer loop is double-buffered by\n1010: `unroll_factor=2`, and any **sibling** region copy loops are NOT liveness-merged\n1011: under `static_alloc`, so live UB = `sibling_regions × BUFFER_NUM × chunk_bytes`:\n1012: \n1013: ```\n1014: chunk_elems = ((UB_CAPACITY - 1024) // dtype.itemsize) // BUFFER_NUM // sibling_regions\n1015: # Ascend950PR_9599 UB_CAPACITY = 253952 B; a 2-region kernel (concat_no_align /\n1016: # concat_flat / concat_flat_copy) uses sibling_regions=2. concat_generic now copies\n1017: # [ubf, chunk] multi-row tiles: size chunk to the widest input (capped at the per-region\n1018: # budget) and ubf = per_region // chunk, so ubf*chunk stays within the same budget.\n1019: ```\n1020: \n1021: Diagnosed empirically: a fp32 column-chunk kernel sized at the full\n1022: `maxAvaliableUb/BUFFER_NUM` overflowed (`UB overflow: 253952 available, 505856\n1023: used` = 4 × chunk) because its two region loops each double-buffer; dividing by\n1024: `sibling_regions` fixed it. Always validate the **static** path with\n1025: `pytest --compile-only` — it is the worst case for UB.\n1026: \n1027: ## Common Mistakes\n1028: \n1029: > These mistakes cause runtime failures even when static verification passes.\n1030: \n1031: | Mistake | Why it fails | Fix |\n1032: |---------|-------------|-----|\n\n(Output capped at 50 KB. Showing lines 1-1032. Use offset=1033 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"---\nname: pyasc-api-patterns\ndescription: pyasc asc2 API usage patterns and best practices. Provides correct usage for tensor operations, tiling, memory access, JIT options, and type system. Trigger — when calling pyasc asc2 APIs, encountering parameter errors, or needing API usage guidance.\n---\n\n# pyasc asc2 API Best Practices\n\n> **Select the runtime profile before copying an API pattern.** The generic\n> examples below describe the stack's simulator/golden profile and include\n> APIs from more than one pyasc v2 snapshot. For CANNBench, load\n> `pyasc-cannbench-kernel` instead: the submitted runtime is pinned to\n> `compiler-team/pyasc:v2@ac1222a48c8914d3f81297c7570d1a84f0f26778` and its\n> memory API is `global_tensor` / `copy_in` / `copy_out`, with `real_shape`\n> for tails. Do not translate a CANNBench module from the older\n> `tensor` / `load` / `store` examples in this file.\n\nThis file is intentionally a broad catalog. Load only the directly relevant\nreference section after the profile is selected; loading the entire catalog\ncan exhaust a worker's context before it writes code.\n","truncated":true,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/aloschilov/workspace/pyasc-skill-stack/skills/pyasc-api-patterns/SKILL.md","text":"---\nname: pyasc-api-patterns\ndescription: pyasc asc2 API usage patterns and best practices. Provides correct usage for tensor operations, tiling, memory access, JIT options, and type system. Trigger — when calling pyasc asc2 APIs, encountering parameter errors, or needing API usage guidance.\n---\n\n# pyasc asc2 API Best Practices\n\n> **Select the runtime profile before copying an API pattern.** The generic\n> examples below describe the stack's simulator/golden profile and include\n> APIs from more than one pyasc v2 snapshot. For CANNBench, load\n> `pyasc-cannbench-kernel` instead: the submitted runtime is pinned to\n> `compiler-team/pyasc:v2@ac1222a48c8914d3f81297c7570d1a84f0f26778` and its\n> memory API is `global_tensor` / `copy_in` / `copy_out`, with `real_shape`\n> for tails. Do not translate a CANNBench module from the older\n> `tensor` / `load` / `store` examples in this file.\n\nThis file is intentionally a broad catalog. Load only the directly relevant\nreference section after the profile is selected; loading the entire catalog\ncan exhaust a worker's context before it writes code.\n\n## API Category Index\n\n| API Category | Key APIs | Typical Scenarios |\n|-------------|----------|-------------------|\n| **Memory** | `asc2.tensor`, `asc2.load`, `asc2.store` | Global memory access, tile load/store |\n| **Computation** | `x + y`, `asc2.abs(x)`, `asc2.exp(x)`, `asc2.where()` | Element-wise and reduction ops |\n| **Control flow** | `asc2.range(n)` | Tile loops with optional unrolling |\n| **Programming model** | `asc2.block_idx()`, `asc2.block_num()` | Multi-core work distribution |\n| **JIT** | `@asc2.jit(always_compile=True)` | Compilation control |\n| **Kernel params** | `asc.GlobalAddress`, `asc.ConstExpr[int]` | Kernel function signatures |\n| **Tiling math** | `asc.ceildiv(a, b)` | Compute tiles per block |\n\n## Core Types\n\n### Kernel parameter types\n\n| Type | Purpose | Example |\n|------|---------|---------|\n| `asc.GlobalAddress` | Global memory pointer for kernel args | `def kernel(x_ptr: asc.GlobalAddress, ...)` |\n| `asc.ConstExpr[int]` | Compile-time integer constant (included in JIT cache key) | `tile_size: asc.ConstExpr[int]` |\n| `int` | Runtime integer | `size: int` |\n\n### asc2 tensor and memory types\n\n| Type / Function | Purpose | Example |\n|-----------------|---------|---------|\n| `asc2.tensor(ptr, [shape])` | Wrap a global memory pointer as a tensor | `x_gm = asc2.tensor(x_ptr, [size])` |\n| `asc2.load(gm, [tile_shape], offsets=[...])` | Load a tile from global memory | `x = asc2.load(x_gm, [tile_size], offsets=[offset])` |\n| `asc2.store(tile, gm, offsets=[...])` | Store a tile to global memory | `asc2.store(out, out_gm, offsets=[offset])` |\n\n### Configuration types\n\n| Type | Purpose | Example |\n|------|---------|---------|\n| `asc.runtime.config.Backend` | Execution backend | `Backend.NPU`, `Backend.Model` |\n| `asc.runtime.config.Platform` | Target platform | `Platform.Ascend950PR_9599` |\n\n## Common Patterns\n\n### Kernel function pattern (asc2)\n\n```python\nimport asc\nimport asc2\n\n@asc2.jit(always_compile=True)\ndef my_kernel(x_ptr: asc.GlobalAddress, out_ptr: asc.GlobalAddress,\n              size: int, tile_size: asc.ConstExpr[int], tile_per_block: asc.ConstExpr[int]):\n    x_gm = asc2.tensor(x_ptr, [size])\n    out_gm = asc2.tensor(out_ptr, [size])\n    base_offset = asc2.block_idx() * tile_size * tile_per_block\n    for i in asc2.range(tile_per_block, unroll_factor=2):\n        tile_offset = base_offset + i * tile_size\n        x = asc2.load(x_gm, [tile_size], offsets=[tile_offset])\n        out = asc2.abs(x)  # your operation here\n        asc2.store(out, out_gm, offsets=[tile_offset])\n```\n\n### Launch pattern (asc2)\n\n```python\nTILE_SIZE = 128\nCORE_NUM = 16\n\nnum_tiles = asc.ceildiv(size, TILE_SIZE)\nmy_kernel[CORE_NUM](x, out, size, TILE_SIZE, asc.ceildiv(num_tiles, CORE_NUM))\n```\n\nNote: asc2 launch uses `kernel[core_num](...)` — no stream argument needed.\n\n### Tiling with ceildiv\n\nasc2 handles tail/non-divisible tile sizes automatically. The tiling pattern is:\n\n```python\nTILE_SIZE = 128   # fixed tile size (elements per tile)\nCORE_NUM = 16     # number of compute cores\n\nsize = data.size\nnum_tiles = asc.ceildiv(size, TILE_SIZE)\ntile_per_block = asc.ceildiv(num_tiles, CORE_NUM)\n```\n\nInside the kernel:\n```python\nbase_offset = asc2.block_idx() * tile_size * tile_per_block\nfor i in asc2.range(tile_per_block, unroll_factor=2):\n    tile_offset = base_offset + i * tile_size\n    x = asc2.load(x_gm, [tile_size], offsets=[tile_offset])\n    # ... compute ...\n    asc2.store(out, out_gm, offsets=[tile_offset])\n```\n\n**Why `ConstExpr`?** `tile_size` and `tile_per_block` are passed as `asc.ConstExpr[int]` so the JIT compiler can optimize tile-level code and include these values in the cache key.\n\n### Tile sizing for vector-only ops (perf-aware)\n\n`TILE_SIZE = 128` is a safe **correctness** default, but it is often a poor\n**performance** default for elementwise/reduction kernels: a 128-element tile\nleaves the AIV vector pipeline mostly idle behind per-tile MTE setup and loop\noverhead. For perf-sensitive vector ops, use a **wide tile** that keeps the\nvector units busy and amortises per-tile setup, mirroring the ops-math arch35\nelementwise tile policy:\n\n```python\n# perf-aware (oracle_guided) — wide tile for AIV utilisation\nTILE_SIZE = 2048   # vs 128 correctness default\nCORE_NUM  = 16\n# size must be a multiple of TILE_SIZE * CORE_NUM (aligned_only)\n```\n\nEvidence (camodel `Ascend950PR_9599`, abs/float16, `[32,4096]`, vs the\nhand-written AscendC `aclnnAbs`): `TILE_SIZE=128` → ratio ≈ 0.20;\n`TILE_SIZE=2048` → ratio ≈ 0.93 (within 7% of hand-written). The op is\nunchanged — only the tile width moves the number. See\n[`docs/perf-vs-ascendc-demo.md`](../../docs/perf-vs-ascendc-demo.md) and\n[`docs/perf-methodology/ticks-calculation.md` §8](../../docs/perf-methodology/ticks-calculation.md).\n\n**Multi-input elementwise: also widen `CORE_NUM`.** A wide tile alone is not\nenough when the op loads two or more input streams (e.g. `add`, `mul`,\n`x*y+z`). The extra MTE2 load stream is *not* overlapped today — `asc2.range`\nleaves `gm_barrier=False` (overlap enabled) but the software-pipelining pass\nthat would overlap loads with compute is not yet wired up — so the second load\nruns serially per core. Spread the launch across **all 32 AIV cores** (not 16) so\neach core does half the tiles and half the serial load work:\n\n```python\n# perf-aware, multi-input elementwise (add/mul/fma): use all 32 AIV cores\nTILE_SIZE = 2048\nCORE_NUM  = 32     # vs 16 for single-input; matches the reference's ~32-block split\n# size must be a multiple of TILE_SIZE * CORE_NUM (aligned_only)\n```\n\nEvidence (camodel `Ascend950PR_9599`, add/float16, `[32,4096]`, vs hand-written\n`aclnnAdd`): at `CORE_NUM=16` the kernel measured `gen_ticks=6304` (ratio 0.68,\n**FAIL**); at `CORE_NUM=32` it drops to `gen_ticks=3623` (ratio **1.18**, PASS) —\nsame op, only the core count moved. Single-input ops like `abs` already clear the\ngate at 16 cores, so this is specifically the multi-load lever.\n\nReference tile policy (hand-written AscendC, arch35), in the remote `ops-math`\nrepo (`https://gitcode.com/cann/ops-math`, branch `master`):\n[`math/abs/op_host/arch35/abs_tiling_arch35.cpp`](https://gitcode.com/cann/ops-math/blob/master/math/abs/op_host/arch35/abs_tiling_arch35.cpp)\nand [`math/add/op_host/arch35/add_tiling_arch35.cpp`](https://gitcode.com/cann/ops-math/blob/master/math/add/op_host/arch35/add_tiling_arch35.cpp)\n(local mirror when cloned: `$CANN_OPS_ROOT/ops-math/math/{abs,add}/op_host/arch35/`).\n\n> Keep the **rank-1 flatten** invariant when widening the tile: declare the GM\n> tensor as `asc2.tensor(x_ptr, [size])` and load `[tile_size]` with a 1D\n> `offsets=[tile_offset]`. Widening `TILE_SIZE` does not change the rank rules.\n\n> **Reductions have their own tile-selection rules.** A last-axis reduction is\n> tiled as a 2-D `[tile_rows, tile_cols]` block, not a 1-D width. Three levers\n> decide the ratio: use every AI core (spread rows across the full core grid),\n> keep the reduce axis contiguous (`tile_cols = C`, do not pad/align it), and size\n> `tile_rows` to the per-core row block then the ~192 KB physical UB. See\n> [Reduction tiling selection](references/reduction-tiling.md).\n\n> **CRITICAL**: Any value used in the **shape** argument of `asc2.load` or `asc2.tensor`\n> MUST be either a literal integer, a `ConstExpr[int]` parameter, or a compile-time\n> expression. Using a plain `int` parameter in load shape (e.g., `asc2.load(gm, [cols])` where\n> `cols: int`) will cause `RuntimeError: All values in 'shape' must be integers` at JIT time.\n> Always declare such parameters as `asc.ConstExpr[int]`.\n\n### Recommended `asc2.range` parameters (PR 190 defaults)\n\nTwo kwargs of `asc2.range` (signature in\n[docker/pyasc-overlay/asc_language_tile/range.py](../../docker/pyasc-overlay/asc_language_tile/range.py))\nmaterially change codegen quality:\n\n- `unroll_factor: int = 1` — loop unroll attribute placed on the emitted\n  `ForOp`. Higher values give the compiler more scheduling freedom.\n- `parallel: bool = False` — flags the loop as having no carried\n  dependencies, allowing iteration-level reordering / vectorisation.\n\nThe compiler-team [PR 190](https://gitcode.com/compiler-team/pyasc/pull/190)\nupgrades these from \"advanced tuning knob\" to \"expected default\": every\n`asc2.range` should set `unroll_factor=2`; leave `gm_barrier=False` (the default,\noverlap enabled) whenever the loop has no read-after-write through a value\ndefined outside the loop. Set `gm_barrier=True` (insert a barrier, overlap off)\nonly when an iteration reads a value a previous iteration wrote.\n\n**Decision rule:**\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    Start[\"asc2.range(...) loop body\"] --> Carried{\"Reads any tile, scalar, or accumulator that a PREVIOUS iteration WROTE?\"}\n    Carried -- \"No (independent tiles, disjoint stores)\" --> Par[\"unroll_factor=2\"]\n    Carried -- \"Yes (sum/max accumulator, prefix scan, in-place mutation)\" --> NoPar[\"unroll_factor=2, gm_barrier=True\"]\n```\n\n**Pattern table** (which form to ship for each loop kind in the proven\ngolden patterns):\n\n| Loop kind | Example | `unroll_factor` | `parallel` |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Elementwise tile loop | `for i in asc2.range(tile_per_block)` | 2 | True |\n| Row distribution | `for r in asc2.range(asc2.block_idx(), num_rows, asc2.block_num())` | 2 | True |\n| Disjoint slice loop inside a row (e.g. RMSNorm split_d write-back) | `for tile_id in asc2.range(num_tiles)` | 2 | True |\n| Reduction accumulator (e.g. RMSNorm split_d `sum_sq = sum_sq + ...`) | `for tile_id in asc2.range(num_tiles)` | 2 | omit (default `False`) |\n| Compile-time loop over `asc.ConstExpr[int]` (matmul m / n loops) | `for i in range(m_tiles_per_block)` (Python `range`, not `asc2.range`) | n/a | n/a |\n\nCompile-time loops (last row) are already fully traced/unrolled at JIT\ntime; wrapping them in `asc2.range(unroll_factor=2)` would emit a runtime\n`ForOp` and is a regression. Leave them as plain Python `range`.\n\n**Worked examples:**\n\n```python\n# (1) elementwise tile loop -- no carry, fully parallel\nfor i in asc2.range(tile_per_block, unroll_factor=2):\n    tile_offset = base_offset + i * tile_size\n    x = asc2.load(x_gm, [tile_size], offsets=[tile_offset])\n    asc2.store(asc2.abs(x), out_gm, offsets=[tile_offset])\n\n# (2) row distribution -- each row independent\nfor r in asc2.range(asc2.block_idx(), num_rows, asc2.block_num(),\n                    unroll_factor=2):\n    row = asc2.load(x_gm, [1, num_cols], offsets=[r, 0])\n    s = asc2.reduce_sum(row)            # accumulation is INSIDE one call\n    asc2.store(asc2.full([1, OUT_PAD], s, dtype=asc.float32), out_gm,\n               offsets=[r, 0])\n\n# (3) reduction accumulator -- scalar carry across iterations -> NOT parallel\nsum_sq = asc2.reduce_sum(asc2.full([1, tile_cols], 0.0, dtype=asc.float32))\nfor tile_id in asc2.range(num_tiles, unroll_factor=2):     # parallel omitted\n    col = tile_id * tile_cols\n    x = asc2.load(x_gm, [1, tile_cols], offsets=[row, col]).to(asc.float32)\n    sum_sq = sum_sq + asc2.reduce_sum(x * x)\n```\n\n### Verification pattern (numpy)\n\n```python\nimport numpy as np\nrng = np.random.default_rng(seed=2026)\n\n# CRITICAL: numpy Generator does NOT support dtype=float16.\n# Always generate as float32, then cast:\nx = (rng.random(size, dtype=np.float32) * 10 - 5).astype(np.float16)\n\nout = kernel_launch(x)\nexpected = np.abs(x)\nnp.testing.assert_allclose(out, expected, atol=1e-3, rtol=1e-3)\n```\n\n**Recommended tolerances** (simulator introduces rounding). Each row\ncites the upstream source of truth at `pyasc-v2-eval@7b85554a`:\n\n- float16 elementwise: `atol=1e-3, rtol=1e-3` — matches our golden\n  `golden/kernels/abs_f16.py`. Upstream `operations/test_unary_ops.py`\n  asserts f32 at `atol=1e-3` against `torch.abs`; we extend that\n  contract to f16.\n- float16 composed (gelu, softmax): `atol=5e-2, rtol=5e-2` — matches\n  `golden/kernels/gelu_f16.py`. Upstream `kernels/test_gelu.py` uses\n  `rtol=1e-3, atol=1e-5` on f32; we relax for the f16 composed path.\n- float32 elementwise (matmul output / accumulators only): `atol=1e-5,\n  rtol=1e-5`. **Do not use `1e-5` for elementwise unary on f32** —\n  upstream `operations/test_unary_ops.py` ships `asc2.abs(f32)` at\n  `atol=1e-3` and our `capabilities.yaml` `abs/float32` cell agrees.\n  Use `atol=1e-3, rtol=1e-3` for unary float32 ops.\n- float32 composed (gelu, lean exp restatement of tanh/Pade):\n  `atol=1e-2, rtol=1e-2`. Upstream `target/test_gelu.py` is tighter\n  (`atol=1e-3`) but uses swapped polynomial coefficients (see\n  `docs/golden-upstream-map.md`); our looser bound is current\n  headroom and may tighten after a stability sweep.\n\nThe previous version of this table said `float32 elementwise: 1e-5`\nunconditionally, which contradicted `capabilities.yaml` and was a\nknown driver of agent confusion. The rule of thumb: **trust the\ngolden's `assert_allclose` value as the contract; if the golden does\nnot exist yet, take it from upstream `operations/test_*_ops.py` for\nthat op family**.\n\n## Available asc2 Operations\n\n### Unary operations (on tiles)\n\n| Operation | Usage | Notes |\n|-----------|-------|-------|\n| `asc2.abs(x)` | Absolute value | |\n| `asc2.exp(x)` | Exponential | |\n| `asc2.log(x)` | Natural log | |\n| `asc2.sqrt(x)` | Square root | |\n| `asc2.relu(x)` | ReLU activation | |\n| `asc2.erf(x)` | Error function | Noisy on float32 simulator (~1.84-4.7 max abs error); avoid for f32 GELU — use the lean `asc2.exp` restatement instead. |\n| `asc2.tanh(x)` | Hyperbolic tangent | Bit-exact on the simulator, but heavier than `asc2.exp`. For f32 GELU, prefer the algebraically-equivalent `x / (1 + asc2.exp(-sqrt(8/pi) * (x + 0.044715*x^3)))` form (see f32 GELU Pattern below) — the asc2.tanh variant pushed the gelu/f32 cell over the 150s sim budget through Phase 9. |\n| `asc2.exp(x)` (re-listed for emphasis) | Exponential | Lean primitive; canonical building block for the f32 GELU sigmoid restatement. |\n| `asc2.sin(x)` | Sine | |\n| `asc2.cos(x)` | Cosine | |\n| `-x` | Negate | Unary operator |\n\n### Binary operations (on tiles)\n\n| Operation | Usage | Notes |\n|-----------|-------|-------|\n| `x + y` | Add | |\n| `x - y` | Subtract | |\n| `x * y` | Multiply | |\n| `x / y` | Divide | |\n| `asc2.where(cond, a, b)` | Conditional select | Like `np.where`. **Size the destination tile to a multiple of 256 bytes** — see the destination-overrun note under JIT Options, which affects every reuse setting |\n| `asc2.equal(x, y)`, `not_equal`, `greater`, `greater_equal`, `less`, `less_equal` | Element-wise comparison, yields a mask | **No `int64` form.** Operands must be one of `int8, int16, int32, float16, bfloat16, float32`; an `int64` operand fails at codegen with `RuntimeError: 'input' dtype must be one of ..., got int64`. Narrow first — see Common Mistakes |\n\n### Reduction operations\n\n| Operation | Usage | Notes |\n|-----------|-------|-------|\n| `asc2.reduce_sum(x)` | Full sum reduction | Returns scalar tile |\n| `asc2.reduce_sum(x, dim)` | Axis sum reduction | Reduce along given dim |\n| `asc2.reduce_max(x)` | Max reduction | Returns scalar tile |\n| `x.sum()` | Sum reduction | |\n| `x.max()` | Max reduction | |\n| `x.min()` | Min reduction | |\n\n### Tile creation\n\n| Operation | Usage | Notes |\n|-----------|-------|-------|\n| `asc2.full(shape, scalar, dtype=...)` | Create tile filled with scalar | **Required** when storing scalar reduction results — last dim must be >= 32/sizeof(dtype) bytes for alignment |\n\n### Advanced operations\n\n| Operation | Usage | Notes |\n|-----------|-------|-------|\n| `asc2.softmax(x)` | Softmax | Operates on full rows of a 2D tile |\n| `asc2.matmul(a, b)` or `a @ b` | Matrix multiply | Requires `asc2.TileLocation` for memory placement |\n| `asc2.reduce_sum(x*x)` + `asc2.sqrt(...)` | Root-mean-square layer norm (manual) | Two-kernel + host-dispatcher pattern on **C310 (Ascend950PR_9599)** mirroring CANN's `KernelRmsNormRegBase` (full row in UB) and `KernelRmsNormRegBaseSplitD` (stream along D). Inputs are `torch.Tensor` (numpy is silently zeroed on C310). The `asc2.rms_norm` builtin is currently NOT used. |\n\n## Proven Kernel Patterns\n\n> **Use these exact patterns.** They are extracted from golden kernels verified on the CANN 9.0.0 simulator and cross-referenced against upstream `pyasc-v2-eval@7b85554a:python/test/asc2/`. Deviating from these patterns is the primary cause of runtime failures.\n\n### Rule: rank-consistent tiling\n\nEvery `asc2.tensor`, `asc2.load`, `asc2.store`, and `offsets=...` argument\nin the same kernel **must use the same rank**. If you declare a 2D\ntensor, your load shape is 2D and your offsets are 2D. If you flatten\nto 1D, load shape is 1D and offsets are 1D. Never mix.\n\nThis is the single most common cause of v2 generative failures —\nagents see 2D test shapes in the prompt and emit:\n\n```python\n# WRONG -- 2D tensor, 1D load shape, 2D offsets => rank mismatch\nx_gm = asc2.tensor(x_ptr, [num_rows, num_cols])\nrow_idx = tile_offset // num_cols\ncol_idx = tile_offset % num_cols\nx = asc2.load(x_gm, [tile_size], offsets=[row_idx, col_idx])\nout = asc2.abs(x)\nasc2.store(out, out_gm, offsets=[row_idx, col_idx])\n```\n\nv2's strict rank check rejects this immediately:\n\n```\nRuntimeError: rank of 'tensor_shape' must match rank of 'shape'\nasc.codegen.errors.CodegenError: at <source>:N:M\n    x = asc2.load(x_gm, [tile_size], offsets=[row_idx, col_idx])\n        ^\n```\n\nThe fix is either **flatten to 1D** (Pattern A or B below) or **keep\n2D and align the load shape to it** (Pattern C). Pick one; do not\nhalf-flatten.\n\nThree patterns are valid on v2 for elementwise / composed-elementwise\nkernels. Pick by the kernel's needs; do not invent a fourth.\n\n### Pattern A — 1D flatten (simple)\n\nSource: upstream `pyasc-v2-eval@7b85554a:python/test/asc2/kernels/test_vadd.py`.\nUse for any unary or binary element-wise operation (abs, exp, add,\nsub, gelu, leaky_relu, etc.) when the test shapes are 1D or can be\ncollapsed to 1D at the host call site.\n\n```python\nTILE_SIZE = 128\nCORE_NUM = 16\n\n@asc2.jit(always_compile=True)\ndef my_kernel(x_ptr: asc.GlobalAddress, out_ptr: asc.GlobalAddress,\n              size: int, tile_size: asc.ConstExpr[int],\n              tile_per_block: asc.ConstExpr[int]):\n    x_gm = asc2.tensor(x_ptr, [size])           # 1D tensor\n    out_gm = asc2.tensor(out_ptr, [size])\n    base_offset = asc2.block_idx() * tile_size * tile_per_block\n    for i in asc2.range(tile_per_block, unroll_factor=2):\n        tile_offset = base_offset + i * tile_size\n        x = asc2.load(x_gm, [tile_size], offsets=[tile_offset])  # 1D load, 1D offsets\n        out = asc2.abs(x)  # replace with your op\n        asc2.store(out, out_gm, offsets=[tile_offset])\n\n# Launch (flatten multi-dim test inputs to 1D first):\nnum_tiles = asc.ceildiv(size, TILE_SIZE)\nmy_kernel[CORE_NUM](x, out, size, TILE_SIZE, asc.ceildiv(num_tiles, CORE_NUM))\n```\n\nIf your test shape is multi-dim (e.g. `[24, 512, 1024]`), compute the\nflat length at the host: `size = int(np.prod(test_shape))`. The kernel\nsees only `size` and never reasons about row/column structure.\n\n### Pattern B — 1D flatten with cache-line aligned tail (production)\n\nSource: upstream `pyasc-v2-eval@7b85554a:python/test/asc2/target/test_vadd.py`.\nUse when the kernel must accept non-aligned input sizes (the kernel\ndeclares `tail_behavior: padded` or `aligned_only` with host-side\npadding). All Phase 9 new goldens follow this pattern.\n\n```python\nALIGNMENT_ELEMENTS = 32 // input_dtype.itemsize  # 32 bytes / dtype.itemsize\ntile_length = asc2.ceildiv(tile_length_request, ALIGNMENT_ELEMENTS) * ALIGNMENT_ELEMENTS\nblock_loop_num = asc2.ceildiv(asc2.ceildiv(length, core_num), tile_length)\nblock_length = tile_length * block_loop_num\nblock_loop_num_tail = asc2.ceildiv(length - block_length * (core_num - 1), tile_length)\npadded_length = block_length * (core_num - 1) + tile_length * block_loop_num_tail\n# Then pad the host input to padded_length with zeros; the kernel skips\n# the last block_loop_num_tail iterations on the final core.\n\n@asc2.jit(static_alloc=True, reuse_ub=True)\ndef my_kernel(x_ptr: asc2.GlobalAddress, out_ptr: asc2.GlobalAddress,\n              input_shape: asc2.ConstExpr, output_shape: asc2.ConstExpr,\n              block_loop_num: asc2.ConstExpr, block_loop_num_tail: asc2.ConstExpr,\n              tile_length: asc2.ConstExpr, block_length: asc2.ConstExpr,\n              UNROLL_FACTOR: asc2.ConstExpr):\n    x_gm = asc2.tensor(x_ptr, input_shape)\n    out_gm = asc2.tensor(out_ptr, output_shape)\n    block_offset = asc2.block_idx() * block_length\n    loop_count = block_loop_num\n    if asc2.block_idx() == (asc2.block_num() - 1):\n        loop_count = block_loop_num_tail\n    for i in asc2.range(loop_count, unroll_factor=UNROLL_FACTOR):\n        current_offset = block_offset + i * tile_length\n        xt = asc2.load(x_gm, [tile_length], offsets=[current_offset])\n        zt = asc2.abs(xt)  # replace with your op\n        asc2.store(zt, out_gm, offsets=[current_offset])\n```\n\n### Pattern C — 2D row-tiled\n\nSource: upstream `pyasc-v2-eval@7b85554a:python/test/asc2/kernels/test_gelu.py`.\nUse when the kernel must process one row at a time (per-row reductions\nor per-row composed ops where the iteration index is logically a row\nindex). Ranks are 2D everywhere.\n\n```python\n@asc2.jit(always_compile=True)\ndef my_kernel(x_ptr: asc2.GlobalAddress, out_ptr: asc2.GlobalAddress,\n              num_rows: asc2.ConstExpr, num_columns: asc2.ConstExpr,\n              tile_size: asc2.ConstExpr):\n    x_gm = asc2.tensor(x_ptr, [num_rows, num_columns])    # 2D tensor\n    out_gm = asc2.tensor(out_ptr, [num_rows, num_columns])\n    for i in asc2.range(asc2.block_idx(), num_rows, asc2.block_num()):\n        row = asc2.load(x_gm, [1, tile_size], offsets=[i, 0])  # 2D load, 2D offsets\n        # ... per-row computation here, e.g. erf-form GELU:\n        k = asc2.sqrt(0.5)\n        out = row * (asc2.erf(row * k) + 1) * 0.5\n        asc2.store(out, out_gm, offsets=[i, 0])\n\n# Launch:\ncore_num = 16\ntile_size = 1024\nmy_kernel[core_num](x, out, num_rows, num_columns, tile_size)\n```\n\nNote `[1, tile_size]` for the load shape (rank 2) and `[i, 0]` for\noffsets (rank 2) — both match the rank of the 2D tensor.\n\n### Composed (gelu, leaky_relu) — choose Pattern A or C\n\nComposed elementwise ops (gelu, leaky_relu) work in either Pattern A\n(1D flatten + composed math in the loop body) or Pattern C (2D\nrow-tiled). Both are valid on v2 as long as **ranks stay consistent**.\n\nTwo GELU forms are supported on the simulator; pick by dtype:\n\n```python\n# float16 GELU (erf form -- simulator erf precision is fine at f16 tolerance):\nk = asc2.sqrt(0.5)\nout = x * (asc2.erf(x * k) + 1) * 0.5\n\n# float32 GELU (tanh/Pade form via the LEAN exp/sigmoid restatement\n# -- simulator erf is too noisy on f32 AND simulator asc2.tanh\n# pushed this cell over the 150s sim budget through Phase 9):\n# Math identity: 0.5*x*(1+tanh(sqrt(2/pi)*(x+0.044715*x^3)))\n#              = x / (1 + exp(-sqrt(8/pi)*(x+0.044715*x^3)))\n# Define module-level constants OUTSIDE @asc2.jit:\n#     GELU_C = 0.044715\n#     NEG_SQRT_EIGHT_OVER_PI = -math.sqrt(8.0 / math.pi)\nx_cub = x * x * x\ninner = (x_cub * GELU_C + x) * NEG_SQRT_EIGHT_OVER_PI\nout = x / (asc2.exp(inner) + 1)\n# Replaces asc2.tanh + scalar_mul + add + scalar_mul with\n# asc2.exp + add + div (one fewer asc2 op per tile, no tanh dep).\n# Verified in golden/kernels/gelu_f32.py at TILE_SIZE=64, CORE_NUM=16.\n\n# Leaky ReLU kernel op (inside @asc2.jit):\nout = asc2.where(x >= 0, x, x * alpha)\n```\n\nSimulator constraints to honour:\n- **Module-level constants only.** `asc2.*` functions are valid only\n  inside a `@asc2.jit` body; module-level constants must use Python or\n  `math.*` (e.g. `NEG_SQRT_EIGHT_OVER_PI = -math.sqrt(8.0 / math.pi)`).\n  Calling `math.sqrt` inside a `@asc2.jit` body raises\n  `RuntimeError: Unsupported function referenced`. Calling `asc2.sqrt(0.5)`\n  outside `@asc2.jit` (at module scope) raises\n  `AttributeError: 'NoneType' object has no attribute 'create_math_SqrtOp'`.\n  Use `asc2.sqrt(0.5)` **inside** the jit body (as in the f16 GELU example)\n  or precompute as a module-level Python constant (as in the f32 GELU\n  example).\n- For f32 GELU, pin `TILE_SIZE = 64`, `CORE_NUM = 16`. With wider tiles\n  (128) only the first 64 elements get written (a wide-tile lowering\n  bug on C310's simulator path); the rest are silently zero. Same class\n  of issue as the wide-tile rms_norm history.\n\n**GELU host-side verification** (pick one):\n\n```python\nimport math\n\n# float16 erf form: vectorise math.erf -- do NOT use np.erf or scipy.\n_verf = np.vectorize(math.erf)\nexpected_f16 = (0.5 * x * (1.0 + _verf(x.astype(np.float32) / np.sqrt(2.0)))).astype(np.float16)\nnp.testing.assert_allclose(out_f16.astype(np.float32),\n                           expected_f16.astype(np.float32),\n                           atol=5e-2, rtol=5e-2)\n\n# float32 tanh form: use np.tanh (no scipy needed):\nk = np.sqrt(2.0 / np.pi)\nexpected_f32 = 0.5 * x * (1.0 + np.tanh(k * (x + 0.044715 * x ** 3)))\nnp.testing.assert_allclose(out_f32, expected_f32, atol=1e-2, rtol=1e-2)\n```\n\n### Tier 1 — Reduction (row-wise)\n\nUse for reduce_sum, reduce_max, etc. Key differences from elementwise:\n- 2D tensor layout; rows distributed across cores via `asc2.range(block_idx, num_rows, block_num)`\n- Scalar results must be wrapped with `asc2.full()` before storing (32-byte alignment)\n- Output buffer must be padded (e.g., `OUT_PAD = 8` for float32)\n\n```python\nOUT_PAD = 8  # min last-dim for 32-byte alignment with float32\n\n@asc2.jit(always_compile=True)\ndef reduce_sum_kernel(x_ptr: asc.GlobalAddress, out_ptr: asc.GlobalAddress,\n                      num_rows: int, num_cols: asc.ConstExpr[int],\n                      out_pad: asc.ConstExpr[int]):\n    x_gm = asc2.tensor(x_ptr, [num_rows, num_cols])\n    out_gm = asc2.tensor(out_ptr, [num_rows, out_pad])\n    for i in asc2.range(asc2.block_idx(), num_rows, asc2.block_num(),\n                        unroll_factor=2):\n        row = asc2.load(x_gm, [1, num_cols], offsets=[i, 0])\n        s = asc2.reduce_sum(row)            # accumulation is INSIDE one call\n        result = asc2.full([1, out_pad], s, dtype=row.dtype)\n        asc2.store(result, out_gm, offsets=[i, 0])\n\n# Launch + extract results:\nout = np.zeros((num_rows, OUT_PAD), dtype=x.dtype)\nreduce_sum_kernel[CORE_NUM](x, out, num_rows, num_cols, OUT_PAD)\nresult = out[:, 0]  # extract first column\n```\n\n### Tier 3 — Advanced (softmax, matmul)\n\nUse `asc2.softmax()` on a block of full rows. Do NOT decompose softmax manually.\n\n**matmul** — supported. Two strict requirements:\n\n1. **Platform must be `Ascend950PR_9599`** (cube unit) — and so does the entire\n   stack: it is the only simulator platform CI targets. Pass `-v Ascend950PR_9599`\n   when running the kernel script (the goldens default to it).\n2. **Inputs must be `torch.Tensor`** (not numpy arrays). The simulator silently\n   lowers numpy arrays to zero for matmul. This is the *only* operation that\n   requires torch on the host side; everything else stays numpy-only.\n\nThe `asc2.matmul` (or `@`) API loads the operands to `L0A` / `L0B` and always\nproduces a `float32` result tile, even for `float16` inputs.\n\nProven kernel pattern (from `golden/kernels/matmul_f16.py`):\n\n```python\nimport torch\nimport asc, asc2\nimport asc.runtime.config as config\n\n@asc2.jit(always_compile=True)\ndef matmul_kernel(a_ptr, b_ptr, c_ptr,\n                  a_shape: asc.ConstExpr, b_shape: asc.ConstExpr, c_shape: asc.ConstExpr,\n                  m_tile: asc.ConstExpr[int], m_tiles_per_block: asc.ConstExpr[int],\n                  n_tile: asc.ConstExpr[int], n_tiles_per_block: asc.ConstExpr[int]):\n    a_gm = asc2.tensor(a_ptr, a_shape)\n    b_gm = asc2.tensor(b_ptr, b_shape)\n    c_gm = asc2.tensor(c_ptr, c_shape)\n    block_id = asc2.block_idx()\n    m_elems_per_block = m_tile * m_tiles_per_block\n    m_base_off = (m_elems_per_block * block_id) % a_shape[0]\n    n_base_off = ((m_elems_per_block * block_id) // a_shape[0]) * (n_tile * n_tiles_per_block)\n    for j in range(n_tiles_per_block):\n        b_offset = n_base_off + j * n_tile\n        b_j = asc2.load(b_gm, [b_shape[0], n_tile], offsets=[0, b_offset],\n                        location=asc2.TileLocation.L0B)\n        for i in range(m_tiles_per_block):\n            a_offset = m_base_off + i * m_tile\n            a_i = asc2.load(a_gm, [m_tile, a_shape[1]], offsets=[a_offset, 0],\n                            location=asc2.TileLocation.L0A)\n            c_ij = a_i @ b_j     # asc2.matmul; result is float32\n            asc2.store(c_ij, c_gm, offsets=[a_offset, b_offset])\n\n# Host-side launch (torch, not numpy):\na = torch.rand((m, k), dtype=torch.float16)\nb = torch.rand((k, n), dtype=torch.float16)\nc = torch.zeros((m, n), dtype=torch.float32)   # output is float32\nmatmul_kernel[core_num](a, b, c, a.shape, b.shape, c.shape,\n                        m_tile, m_tiles_per_block, n_tile, n_tiles_per_block)\nc_ref = a.to(torch.float32) @ b.to(torch.float32)\ntorch.testing.assert_close(c, c_ref, atol=1e-2, rtol=1e-2)\n```\n\nTile-size constraints (from the proven pattern):\n\n- `m_tile % 16 == 0`, `n_tile % 16 == 0`\n- `m_tile * k * a.element_size() <= 64 KiB` (L0A budget)\n- `n_tile * k * b.element_size() <= 64 KiB` (L0B budget)\n- `m % m_tile == 0`, `n % n_tile == 0`\n- Tiles distributed evenly across `core_num` blocks\n\nRecommended starter shape: `m=k=n=16, core_num=1, m_tile=n_tile=16,\nm_tiles_per_block=n_tiles_per_block=1`. Once that passes, scale up.\n\n#### CUBE-only batched matmul (BatchMatMulV3) + perf levers\n\nFor a **batched** matmul `C[b] = A[b] @ B[b]` (the first CUBE-only\noperator-generation demo), build on the single-GEMM pattern with three additions\n(proven in `golden/kernels/batch_mat_mul_v3_f16.py`, measured PASS at ratio 0.78\nvs the canonical `aclnnBatchMatMul`):\n\n1. **Batch across cores.** Flatten `[B,M,K]`→`[B*M,K]`, `[B,K,N]`→`[B*K,N]`,\n   `[B,M,N]`→`[B*M,N]` on the host so each batch is a contiguous row-block, launch\n   `kernel[B]`, and let `bi = asc2.block_idx()` own batch `bi` (rows `bi*M` of A/C,\n   `bi*K` of B). All loads/offsets stay 2-D, satisfying the cube tile rules.\n2. **f16 in / f32 accumulate / f16 out.** The cube result tile is always f32; cast\n   it on store with `c_ij = (a_i @ b_j).to(asc2.float16)` (the fixpipe cast path,\n   cf. `test_matmul_fixpipe.py`). Allocate the host output as `torch.float16`.\n3. **L1 staging + a pipelined N-tile loop (the perf levers).** Stage the whole\n   per-batch `A[m,k]`/`B[k,n]` into `L1` once so every GM element is read a single\n   time, then copy `L1`→`L0A`/`L0B` tiles. Pipeline the inner N loop with\n   `asc2.range(n_tiles, unroll_factor=2)` so the next L0B copy\n   overlaps the current MMAD.\n\n```python\na_l1 = asc2.load(a_gm, [m, k], offsets=[bi * m, 0], location=asc2.TileLocation.L1)\nb_l1 = asc2.load(b_gm, [k, n], offsets=[bi * k, 0], location=asc2.TileLocation.L1)\nfor i in range(m // m_tile):                      # plain range: A stays in L0A\n    a_i = asc2.copy(a_l1, [m_tile, k], offsets=[i * m_tile, 0], location=asc2.TileLocation.L0A)\n    for j in asc2.range(n // n_tile, unroll_factor=2):  # double-buffered L0B\n        b_j = asc2.copy(b_l1, [k, n_tile], offsets=[0, j * n_tile], location=asc2.TileLocation.L0B)\n        c_ij = (a_i @ b_j).to(asc2.float16)\n        asc2.store(c_ij, c_gm, offsets=[bi * m + i * m_tile, j * n_tile])\n```\n\n**Perf-tuning levers, in priority order** (measure each on the camodel — no\nhand-edited ticks):\n\n- **L1 staging** is the first win (each GM element read once instead of re-fetched\n  per tile). For the `[16,256,256]` contract this alone took `gen` 23434→21620.\n- **`gm_barrier=False` (overlap-enabled) double-buffering** is the second win\n  (21620→18758, ratio 0.78). **Critical budget rule:** overlap *doubles* the\n  buffer it pipelines, so the 2-deep tile must fit half the L0 capacity. A\n  full-K `[256,128]` f16 L0B tile is already 64 KiB and overflows when doubled —\n  drop to `N_TILE=64` (`[256,64]` f16 = 32 KiB ⇒ 64 KiB for the pair). Only\n  pipeline the loop whose L0 buffer you can halve; keep the other loop a plain\n  `range`.\n- **Batch-to-core mapping** is maxed at `CORE_NUM=B` when `B` ≤ the AIC core count\n  (16 cube cores here, so 16 batches = one fully parallel wave). Launching more\n  blocks than cores adds waves, not parallelism.\n- **K-tiling** (`asc2.zeros_acc` + `asc2.matmul_acc` over a `parallel` K loop, cf.\n  `test_matmul_tiled.py`) is the alternative when a single A/B tile cannot fit L0;\n  not needed at K=256 (the A tile fits L0A whole).\n\nL0 budget reminder for the pair-buffered case: each pipelined L0 tile ≤ **32 KiB**\n(half of 64 KiB); the f32 L0C accumulator tile ≤ **64 KiB** (half of 128 KiB).\n\n**softmax** — use `asc2.softmax()` directly:\n\n```python\n@asc2.jit(always_compile=True)\ndef softmax_kernel(x_ptr: asc.GlobalAddress, out_ptr: asc.GlobalAddress,\n                   num_rows: int, num_cols: asc.ConstExpr[int],\n                   block_size: asc.ConstExpr[int]):\n    x_gm = asc2.tensor(x_ptr, [num_rows, num_cols])\n    out_gm = asc2.tensor(out_ptr, [num_rows, num_cols])\n    start_row = asc2.block_idx() * block_size\n    rows = asc2.load(x_gm, [block_size, num_cols], offsets=[start_row, 0])\n    out = asc2.softmax(rows)\n    asc2.store(out, out_gm, offsets=[start_row, 0])\n\n# Launch:\nblock_size = asc.ceildiv(num_rows, CORE_NUM)\nsoftmax_kernel[CORE_NUM](x, out, num_rows, num_cols, block_size)\n```\n\n**Softmax simulator constraint:** Test ONLY the shape specified in the prompt.\nThe simulator is extremely slow for large softmax shapes. Do NOT add extra shapes.\n\n#### Normalization layers — two-kernel RMSNorm with host dispatcher (C310)\n\nRMSNorm in this skill stack ships **two `@asc2.jit` kernels** with a\n**host-side dispatcher**, mirroring CANN's arch35 (C310) `rms_norm` op\n([`opp/built-in/.../rms_norm/rms_norm.cpp`](../../../home/aloschilov/Ascend/cann-9.0.0/opp/built-in/op_impl/ai_core/tbe/impl/ops_nn/ascendc/rms_norm/rms_norm.cpp))\nwhich selects between `KernelRmsNormRegBase` (tiling key `5000`) and\n`KernelRmsNormRegBaseSplitD` (tiling key `2001`) based on whether the\nrow fits in UB. The pyasc analogue is structurally identical:\n\n| CANN kernel | Pyasc analogue | When |\n|-------------|---------------|------|\n| `KernelRmsNormRegBase` | `rms_norm_full_row_kernel` | Row tile fits in UB; `num_cols` is `asc.ConstExpr[int]` |\n| `KernelRmsNormRegBaseSplitD` | `rms_norm_split_d_kernel` | Row exceeds UB; both dims runtime int, host-padded to `tile_cols=64` chunks |\n\n**Platform / I/O contract:**\n\n- Run on `Ascend950PR_9599` (compilation arch `C310`). The skill stack's\n  CI routes `rms_norm_*` goldens to that simulator alongside `matmul_*`.\n- Inputs are `torch.Tensor` (CPU `float32`/`float16`). Numpy arrays are\n  silently zeroed on the C310 simulator path; this is a known property\n  of the `Ascend950PR_9599` runtime and applies to RMSNorm as well as\n  matmul.\n\n**Host-side dispatcher (verbatim from `golden/kernels/rms_norm_f32.py`):**\n\n```python\nUB_BUDGET_BYTES = 64 * 1024  # conservative UB headroom for accumulator + gamma + double-buffer\n\ndef rms_norm_launch(x: torch.Tensor, gamma: torch.Tensor,\n                    eps: float = 1e-5, core_num: int = 8) -> torch.Tensor:\n    num_rows, num_cols = x.shape\n    row_bytes = num_cols * x.element_size()\n    if row_bytes <= UB_BUDGET_BYTES and num_cols % 8 == 0:\n        return _full_row_launch(x, gamma, eps, core_num)\n    return _split_d_launch(x, gamma, eps, core_num)\n```\n\nThe threshold heuristic mirrors CANN's tiling: pick the fast path when\nthe row fits with headroom; otherwise stream.\n\n**Full-row kernel (`KernelRmsNormRegBase` analogue):**\n\n```python\n@asc2.jit(always_compile=True)\ndef rms_norm_full_row_kernel(x_ptr, gamma_ptr, out_ptr,\n                             num_rows: int,\n                             num_cols: asc.ConstExpr[int],\n                             epsilon: asc.ConstExpr[float]):\n    x_gm = asc2.tensor(x_ptr, [num_rows, num_cols])\n    gamma_gm_2d = asc2.tensor(gamma_ptr, [1, num_cols])\n    out_gm = asc2.tensor(out_ptr, [num_rows, num_cols])\n    for row in asc2.range(asc2.block_idx(), num_rows, asc2.block_num(),\n                          unroll_factor=2):\n        x_row = asc2.load(x_gm, [1, num_cols], offsets=[row, 0])\n        x_row_f32 = x_row.to(asc.float32)\n        sum_sq = asc2.reduce_sum(x_row_f32 * x_row_f32)\n        inv_rms = 1.0 / asc2.sqrt(sum_sq / num_cols + epsilon)\n        gamma_row = asc2.load(gamma_gm_2d, [1, num_cols], offsets=[0, 0])\n        gamma_row_f32 = gamma_row.to(asc.float32)\n        out_f32 = x_row_f32 * gamma_row_f32 * inv_rms\n        asc2.store(out_f32.to(x_row.dtype), out_gm, offsets=[row, 0])\n```\n\n`num_cols` is `ConstExpr` because the row tile shape `[1, num_cols]` must\nbe compile-time known. The accumulator stays in float32 even for float16\ninput via `.to(asc.float32)`.\n\n**Split-D kernel (`KernelRmsNormRegBaseSplitD` analogue):**\n\nBoth `num_rows` and `num_cols` are runtime `int`. The row is streamed in\n`tile_cols=64` (one Ascend SIMD lane: 64 floats = 256 bytes) tiles, with\nhost-side zero padding so the tail vanishes:\n\n```python\n@asc2.jit(always_compile=True)\ndef rms_norm_split_d_kernel(x_ptr, gamma_ptr, out_ptr,\n                            num_rows: int, num_cols: int, padded_cols: int,\n                            num_tiles: int,\n                            tile_cols: asc.ConstExpr[int],\n                            epsilon: asc.ConstExpr[float]):\n    x_gm = asc2.tensor(x_ptr, [num_rows, padded_cols])\n    gamma_gm_2d = asc2.tensor(gamma_ptr, [1, padded_cols])\n    out_gm = asc2.tensor(out_ptr, [num_rows, padded_cols])\n    for row in asc2.range(asc2.block_idx(), num_rows, asc2.block_num(),\n                          unroll_factor=2):\n        zero_seed = asc2.full([1, tile_cols], 0.0, dtype=asc.float32)\n        sum_sq = asc2.reduce_sum(zero_seed)\n        # Inner reduction loop carries `sum_sq` -> NOT parallel.\n        for tile_id in asc2.range(num_tiles, unroll_factor=2):\n            col = tile_id * tile_cols\n            x = asc2.load(x_gm, [1, tile_cols], offsets=[row, col])\n            x_f32 = x.to(asc.float32)\n            sum_sq = sum_sq + asc2.reduce_sum(x_f32 * x_f32)\n        inv_rms = 1.0 / asc2.sqrt(sum_sq / num_cols + epsilon)\n        # Disjoint write-back -> safe to parallelise.\n        for tile_id in asc2.range(num_tiles, unroll_factor=2):\n            col = tile_id * tile_cols\n            x = asc2.load(x_gm, [1, tile_cols], offsets=[row, col])\n            gamma = asc2.load(gamma_gm_2d, [1, tile_cols], offsets=[0, col])\n            x_f32 = x.to(asc.float32)\n            gamma_f32 = gamma.to(asc.float32)\n            out_f32 = x_f32 * gamma_f32 * inv_rms\n            asc2.store(out_f32.to(x.dtype), out_gm, offsets=[row, col])\n```\n\nPadded zeros don't contribute to `sum_sq`; the kernel divides by REAL\n`num_cols` so the result is identical to a no-padding implementation.\n\n**`sum_sq` PlainValue seed (REQUIRED in split_d):** the codegen rejects\n`sum_sq = 0.0` because the loop-carried value becomes a `PlainValue`,\ngiving `'sum_sq' was re-assigned to an object with different type`\n([function_visitor.py L241-244](../../pyasc-fork/python/asc/codegen/function_visitor.py)).\nSeed with a 1-vector-lane reduce-of-zero (full_row doesn't need this\nbecause there's no carry across tiles):\n\n```python\nzero_seed = asc2.full([1, tile_cols], 0.0, dtype=asc.float32)\nsum_sq = asc2.reduce_sum(zero_seed)\n```\n\n**Host-side launch (split_d):**\n\n```python\npadded_cols = ((num_cols + tile_cols - 1) // tile_cols) * tile_cols\nx_padded = torch.zeros((num_rows, padded_cols), dtype=x.dtype)\nx_padded[:, :num_cols] = x\ngamma_padded = torch.zeros((padded_cols,), dtype=gamma.dtype)\ngamma_padded[:num_cols] = gamma\nout_padded = torch.zeros((num_rows, padded_cols), dtype=x.dtype)\nnum_tiles = padded_cols // tile_cols\nrms_norm_split_d_kernel[CORE_NUM](x_padded, gamma_padded, out_padded,\n                                  num_rows, num_cols, padded_cols,\n                                  num_tiles, tile_cols, epsilon)\nreturn out_padded[:, :num_cols].clone()\n```\n\n**Host-side reference (torch):**\n\n```python\ndef torch_rms_norm(x, gamma, eps):\n    x32 = x.to(torch.float32)\n    mean_sq = torch.mean(x32 * x32, dim=-1, keepdim=True)\n    return (x32 * torch.rsqrt(mean_sq + eps) * gamma.to(torch.float32)).to(x.dtype)\n```\n\n**Tolerances:** float32 `atol=rtol=1e-4` (both branches); float16\n`atol=rtol=2e-2` for full_row, `atol=rtol=5e-2` for split_d.\n\n**Test shapes:** `(8, 256)` exercises full_row; `(8, 1055)` exercises\nsplit_d (host pads to `1088 = 17 * 64`). Both verified in a single\n`run_kernel` call. The pattern rescales to e.g. `(64, 100003)` by\nchanging only `CORE_NUM` and the test-shape constants.\n\n**Note on \"first or last dim\":** RMSNorm here normalizes along the last\ndim only. Prompts that mention \"first or last dimension\" should be\nanswered with the last-dim form above; the runtime contract above is\nwhat makes the row dim dynamic.\n\n#### LayerNormV4 delta (vs RMSNorm)\n\nLayerNorm adds **mean subtraction**, a **beta** vector, and optional mean/rstd\nside outputs. The golden pattern mirrors RMSNorm's host dispatcher but uses a\n**two-stat** row kernel:\n\n```python\n# full_row (when num_cols % 8 == 0 and num_cols * 4 * 6 <= UB_BUDGET_BYTES)\nmean = asc2.reduce_sum(x_f32, 1, keep_dims=True) / num_cols\nmean_b = asc2.broadcast_to(mean, 1, padded_cols)\nsum_x2 = asc2.reduce_sum(x_f32 * x_f32, 1, keep_dims=True) / num_cols\nvar = sum_x2 - mean * mean          # E[x²] − mean² (padding-safe)\nrstd = asc2.rsqrt(var + epsilon)\ny = (x_f32 - mean_b) * rstd_b * gamma + beta\n```\n\n**N-D contract:** flatten any input to `[rows, last_dim]` on the host\n(`rows = numel // last_dim`), launch, reshape `y` back. `normalizedShape=[last]`\nfor the aclnn reference; mean/rstd shapes are `leading_dims + [1]`, dtypes\n`ACL_FLOAT` even when input is bf16.\n\n**split_d:** stream `tile_cols=64`; merge the mean+variance tile loops\n(`sum_x` and `sum_x2` in one pass); use `asc2.full([1, tile_cols], mean, ...)`\nto subtract a `PlainValue` mean from a tile (tile − scalar is unsupported);\ncompute `rstd` via `1.0 / asc2.sqrt(var + eps)` (scalar `rsqrt` needs a Tile).\n\n**bf16:** build probe inputs as f32 numpy → `torch.bfloat16`; keep stats in f32\ninside the kernel (`.to(asc.float32)` on loads).\n\nSee `golden/kernels/layer_norm_v4_f32.py` and `layer_norm_v4_bf16.py`.\n\n#### Multi-input / multi-axis copy (concat) — ONE packed input + scalar offsets\n\nPure data-movement ops with **multiple inputs** (concat) must take **one packed\ninput tensor**, not N `asc2.GlobalAddress` pointers, and address each logical\nsub-input by offset arithmetic. This is the CANN reviewer-mandated shape (MR !331)\nand supports runtime arity (see metadata pattern below).\n\n**Ranked 2-D sub-view of a packed buffer at a runtime offset (now supported).**\n`asc2.tensor(in_ptr + B_i, [rows, w_i])` builds a ranked 2-D view of input `i`'s\nsub-region of the single packed buffer (`B_i` = element base). `GlobalAddress +\noffset` emits `emitasc.ptr_offset`; this **used to fail** because the op was\nranked-only and kernel-arg pointers are unranked memrefs (`'emitasc.ptr_offset'\nop operand #0 must be memref ... but got 'memref<*xi64>'`). It now lowers after\nrelaxing `EmitAsc_PtrOffsetOp` `$base`/`$result` to `AnyRankedOrUnrankedMemRef`\n(issue #1 / discussion #2; branch `corc-concat-d-ptr-offset`). All three proposed\noptions emit identical AscendC (`SetGlobalBuffer(base + offset)` + `DataCopy`), so\nthe one-line constraint relaxation was chosen as minimal and fully general\n(`asctile.tensor` already accepts unranked bases; `ConvertTensor` forwards the\nbase to `SetGlobalBuffer`; the emitter prints `base + offset`).\n\nThis unlocks **CANN-style multi-row bulk copy** for the aligned paths: per input,\ncopy `[ubFactorDim0, w_i]` tiles (many rows per DMA) over the 2-D sub-view instead\nof one DMA per row — `ubFactorDim0 = min(maxAvaliableUb / catDim1, catDim0)`,\nmirroring CANN `TilingUbForNosplitDim1`. Collapses `rows` DMAs/region into\n`ceil(rows / ubFactorDim0)` larger transfers (e.g. `2048×16` f32 → 988 rows/tile\n→ 3 DMAs vs 2048, ~680× fewer). The runtime-arity / unaligned / wide-row paths\nstill use the 1-D packed model with flat scalar `offsets=` (below).\n\nA concat on axis `k` reduces to a 2-D row-concat\n`[rows = prod(shape[:k]), out_cols = Σ_i prod(input_i.shape[k:])]`; input `i` of\ninner width `w_i` lives at flat input base `B_i = Σ_{j<i} rows*w_j` and output\ncolumn base `C_i = Σ_{j<i} w_j`. Element (row `r`, col `c`) of input `i` copies\n`packed[B_i + r*w_i + c] -> out[r*out_cols + C_i + c]`. Host packs the inputs\ninput-major: `packed = torch.cat([t.reshape(-1) for t in inputs])`. Ground truth\nis `torch.cat(inputs, dim=k)` reshaped.\n\n**Variable arity via RUNTIME metadata (one kernel for arity 2..N).** Don't write\n`concat3`/`concat4`/`concat16` (one kernel per arity) and don't unroll a Python\ntuple of pointers with `static_range`. Instead pass three small int metadata GM\ntensors (`widths[i]=w_i`, `in_bases[i]=B_i`, `out_bases[i]=C_i`) and a runtime\n`num_inputs`. **GM-loaded scalars are usable as `asc2.ceildiv` loop bounds and\ninside `offsets=`**, so per-input geometry is resolved at runtime with no\nper-arity recompile:\n\n```python\n@asc2.jit(static_alloc=True, reuse_ub=True)\ndef concat_generic(in_ptr, w_ptr, ib_ptr, ob_ptr, out_ptr, num_inputs, rows, out_cols, total_in,\n                   chunk: asc2.ConstExpr, ubf: asc2.ConstExpr, unroll_factor: asc2.ConstExpr):\n    widths = asc2.tensor(w_ptr, [num_inputs])\n    in_bases = asc2.tensor(ib_ptr, [num_inputs])\n    out_bases = asc2.tensor(ob_ptr, [num_inputs])\n    out2d = asc2.tensor(out_ptr, [rows, out_cols])     # 2-D output view\n    for s in asc2.range(num_inputs):\n        w = asc2.load(widths, offsets=[s])             # GM scalar (dynamic width)\n        ib = asc2.load(in_bases, offsets=[s])\n        ob = asc2.load(out_bases, offsets=[s])\n        in_s = asc2.tensor(in_ptr + ib, [rows, w])     # ranked 2-D sub-view at runtime base ib\n        # multi-row bulk copy: ubf rows per DMA (ptr_offset AnyRankedOrUnrankedMemRef relaxation, issue #1)\n        for t in range(asc2.block_idx(), asc2.ceildiv(rows, ubf), asc2.block_num(),\n                       unroll_factor=unroll_factor):\n            r0 = t * ubf\n            nr = ubf if r0 + ubf <= rows else rows - r0          # ternary -> select, NOT a guard\n            for c in asc2.range(asc2.ceildiv(w, chunk)):         # runtime bound -> any width fits\n                c0 = c * chunk\n                csize = chunk if c0 + chunk <= w else w - c0\n                tile = asc2.load(in_s, [ubf, chunk], real_shape=[nr, csize], offsets=[r0, c0])\n                asc2.store(tile, out2d, real_shape=[nr, csize], offsets=[r0, ob + c0])\n```\n\nThis is **CANN-style multi-row bulk copy**: per input, the ranked 2-D sub-view\n`asc2.tensor(in_ptr + ib, [rows, w])` lets one DMA move `ubf` rows (sized so\n`ubf*chunk` fits the per-region UB budget), collapsing the prior one-DMA-per-row\nstorm into `ceil(rows/ubf)` transfers per (input, column-chunk). It recovered a\n4-6x regression on narrow-width multi-input cases (arity 3/4 middle/last). The\nearlier per-row path (`asc2.load(inp, [chunk], offsets=[ib + i*w + off])` over a\n1-D packed view) is what regressed; do not reintroduce it.\n\nFor a **first-axis** concat the input-major packed buffer is **byte-identical to\nthe output**, so it is one contiguous `total`-element copy: route to a flat\nchunked copy (`concat_flat_copy`) that fans `ub_chunk`-sized chunks of the whole\nbuffer across cores. Do NOT route first-axis multi-input through `concat_generic`\nwith `rows=1`: parallelism there is over rows, so only one core works while the\nrest idle (this single-core path was a measured regression for arity-16 first-axis).\n\n**Don't ship a kernel that is a special case of another.** A single-core\nwhole-row copy (`concat_simt`) is just the row-distributed `concat_all_align`\nlaunched with `block_num=1` — delete the special case and route to the general\nkernel via launch params. Likewise, **size the launch so every core has work**\ninstead of guarding inside the kernel: for a contiguous dim0 split, launch\n`usedCoreNum = ceildiv(dim0, block_factor)` cores (CANN `blockFactor`/\n`usedCoreNum`/`tailBlockFactor`) so `r_start = block_idx*block_factor < dim0`\nalways holds and no `if r_start < dim0:` guard is needed.\n\n**UB budgeting rule (CRITICAL — sizing `chunk`), grounded in CANN.** Mirror the\nCANN concat tiling (`conversion/concat/op_host/arch35/concat_tiling_arch35.cpp`,\n`TilingUb`): `maxAvaliableUb = (UB_CAPACITY − INDEX_USE_UB) / dtypeSize` with\n`INDEX_USE_UB = 1024`, and the non-aligned in-UB-concat path divides by\n`BUFFER_NUM = 2` (double buffer). The reused-buffer loop is double-buffered by\n`unroll_factor=2`, and any **sibling** region copy loops are NOT liveness-merged\nunder `static_alloc`, so live UB = `sibling_regions × BUFFER_NUM × chunk_bytes`:\n\n```\nchunk_elems = ((UB_CAPACITY - 1024) // dtype.itemsize) // BUFFER_NUM // sibling_regions\n# Ascend950PR_9599 UB_CAPACITY = 253952 B; a 2-region kernel (concat_no_align /\n# concat_flat / concat_flat_copy) uses sibling_regions=2. concat_generic now copies\n# [ubf, chunk] multi-row tiles: size chunk to the widest input (capped at the per-region\n# budget) and ubf = per_region // chunk, so ubf*chunk stays within the same budget.\n```\n\nDiagnosed empirically: a fp32 column-chunk kernel sized at the full\n`maxAvaliableUb/BUFFER_NUM` overflowed (`UB overflow: 253952 available, 505856\nused` = 4 × chunk) because its two region loops each double-buffer; dividing by\n`sibling_regions` fixed it. Always validate the **static** path with\n`pytest --compile-only` — it is the worst case for UB.\n\n## Common Mistakes\n\n> These mistakes cause runtime failures even when static verification passes.\n\n| Mistake | Why it fails | Fix |\n|---------|-------------|-----|","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":1032,"totalLines":1033,"truncated":true}},"title":"skills/pyasc-api-patterns/SKILL.md","time":{"start":1788347625069,"end":1788347625082}},"id":"prt_061d32d2c0013GSsSmJyhNf7Jh","sessionID":"ses_f9e2d34cdffe0eE32xFqUVyL7D","messageID":"msg_061d317e1001RKo5dgjmuOZB0d"}}
